      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>VISIT TO POMPEII.</head>
        <p>A VISIT TO POMPEII.</p>
        <p>THE BURIED CITY OF THE ROMANS.,</p>
        <p>By Charles E. Parker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>[ARTICLE NO. II</head>
        <p>[ARTICLE NO. II</p>
        <p>Pompeii. the buried city, at the base of Mount Vesuvius, was visited by my wife, daughter and self early this year. We found the old Roman town so full of interest that I should like to convey to the readers of the Examiner" some of this interest. Pompeii lies on the southern slope of the mountain not far from the sea,—that is the Bay of Naples. In earlier times the sea was close to the city, but has since receded. The city was taken by the Romans in 79 8.C., was partially destroyed by an earthquake in . .</p>
        <p>as scythes, sickles, bill hooks, rakes, plough shares, and spades. Many of these differ little from those used about Naples at the present day. There is fishing tackle, a wire rope formed of fifteen strands laid up like modern wire rope, harness for horses con- sisting of bits, nose bands, curb chains, spurs, stirrups, etc; golden bracelets, hairpins, tooth and ear pick, ivory combs, bronze thimbles, safety pins, a pot of rock-crystal containing rouge. These are now carefully treasured in the Museum at Naples.</p>
        <p>63 A.D., and totally buried by a violent erup- tion of Vesuvius in 79 A.D. The same eruption buried the city of Herculaneum which lies to the north-west of the mountain.</p>
        <p>THE FORUM. The Forum, or Market Place, was always an important feature in a Roman City. It was the focus and_ centre of all the public</p>
        <p>There is this important difference in the life and movement of the place. The term is 1 entombment of these cities. While said to be related to our word fair. The Herculaneum was buried by the lava which Forum in Pompeii occupies the high part of towed over the lips of the crater, Pompeii the town, and it is 165 yards long and 35 was buried by the lighter ashes, mostly of yards broad. Our photograph shows what the character of pumice stone, which were is still standing ; we look down the side vomited into the air and fell all round, bury- street towards Vesuvius, and the Forum is ing Pompeii and its surroundings about on the right. It was surrounded by a twenty feet deep. If was as if the froth or covered portico, there are evidences of two scum in the boiling cauldron was sent into stories, and some parts of the stairs still</p>
        <p>_ 11=1/I•NNP=I/1•1•0</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>I I , , - , ^ . , , - • - , - lippi • '. - . ..,, - ,i ' ..4.1,;_1. ii . - -.. * ',Tr ' --3P--....wilii .114/g ' - '.: ~,,... :''' ~' , -1--us .4 :t . ..)1.61,1 . „. ..,. , v., . ....4 --.- - iz,t,:. • -.•,-.A.\-.; •-.,..-- ~ .. - , -, i • ,i- -1 It , 40110 • *. . • . ~ .„ '2.-' • , ' ' ' ,45.-:*t..."'"',"-- . 1 - .• . , , ''''' I • flit, e' -.. '4-0-•.:AW. - i - .• -**--1'; 4.- *. —t. * ''' ,-. • ' Itißtie*--4 £- ...•:-. . --...-: .i 1 ',•-• ~•...' :, • ~..* - .-t 1 -•:...- 1 . _.,..., _ , • , ~„,...'. * • . . . : ........ _ %..,‘ , , . . s ~..4.i , 4.* , I , ‘ , ... ~ _ -..</p>
