      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>RUINS OF POMPEII.-THE FOWL. M, OR MARK:</head>
        <p>RUINS OF POMPEII.-THE FOWL. M, OR MARK:</p>
        <p>the air, buried Pompeii, and then there came the overflow of the molten basaltic lava . .</p>
        <p>which buried Herculaneum and hardened to rock as it cooled. This fact has made the excavation of Pompeii much easier than that of the neighbouring city, and has also made the preservation of the buried treasure a comparatively easy task. The deposit has hardened somewhat with age, but most of it can be cut with a spade without great difficulty. The stone,if stone it can be called, is named tutu hi-Naples, and the hardest of it is largely used in building. Most of Naples is built of it. It is dressed with a blunt axe, and as it does not stand the weather well,the outside walls are generally coated with cement. It is evident when the eruption occurred the inhabitants of Pompeii had time to remove their most valuable belongings, as there has been very little jewellery found. Lord Lytton in his "Last Days of Pompeii" has drawn heavily on his imagination when he pictures the devastation overtaking many of the inhabitants as they, all unconscious, were engaged witnessing a gladiatorial show. Vesuvius kindly gives warning when preparing for her great firework displays. Only about forty corpses were found in Pompeii; some were prisoners who were fast in the stocks, others were cripples who could not shift for themselves, and were forgotten, or forsaken, in the general confusion. The extent of the City of Pompeii is estimated at about 140 acres, of which about half is excavated. The excavations are now carried on by the Italian Government, who are doing it in a very leisurely fashion. The circuit of the walls of the City is two miles, and there are eight gates. Amongst the articles excavated we find various tools, such</p>
        <p>exist. At the South end there are the remains of what has evidently been a !Court house, corresponding to our magistrates' Court. There is the central hall containing a raised platform for the magistrates or tribunes, with passages and rooms on either side for prisoners, witnesses, &amp;c. These Roman Forums although primarily the market [squares became naturally the place where all public gatherings were held and where orators of all kinds held forth. I suppose if there happened to be a strike of Transport Workers, of oxen, mule, and donkey drivers, it would be here they would fore- gather to demand a living wage. Probably the last meetings held before the overthrow would be to advance the candidature of the various patricians who were competing for the position of tribunes or duumoirs in an election about due. We learn this fact from the election cries scrawled on the outside walls of several of the houses. Here is one " Phillipus beseeches you to create Holconius Priscus Duumoir of Justice." And again " Propidius' friends solicit support on the ground that he is a modest and illustrious youth." THE STREETS.</p>
        <p>These are nr;rio—w,-n-one-tnO;e than twenty- four feet, most about sixteen. They are made in the usual Roman manner of irregular pologonal slabs of basaltic lava well fitted to each other. These are about two feet square in area, and from ten to fourteen inches deep. About every hundred yards, and at all the cross roads, there are large stepping stones, said to be provided, first, for the comfort of the walkers to keep them out of the street dirt in getting from one side walk to the other; and second, to act as an impediment to an attack by outside enemies, aided by broad gauge chariots. The gauge of</p>
        <p>the ordinary Pompean vehicles appears to have been only four feet. The openings between these stepping stones are much worn, the ruts are in some places seven inches deep. Mark Twain puts the fault on the Street Commissioner and thus " goes for" him:</p>
        <p>"I wish I knew the name of the last one who held office in Pompeii, so that I could give him a blast. I speak with feeling on this subject, because I caught my foot in one of those ruts, and the sadness that came over me when I saw the first poor skeleton, with ashes and lava sticking to it, was tempered by the reflection that, may be, that party was the Street Commissioner." We in Warrington would lay the fault at the door of the Paving aud Sewerage Com- mittee. If such a body existed in Pompeii there was evidence of its activity, for in one of the streets there was accumulated a stock of rough stones preparatory to repairing the worst of these streets. Possibly this activity was the result of the coming election.</p>
        <p>In Italy there are many parts of the roads still in use the surface of which is as laid by the Romans. The Appian Way, by which Paul was brought a prisoner into Rome affords an example. The road level has been a good deal altered in parts, but there are still several sections as laid down by the Romans. These stones have stood the wear of two thousand years, and if left undis- turbed will last for centuries. It would appear that the pavements in Pompeii, apart from the curbing, were made and looked after by the individual frontagers. This is evident from the variety of designs and , colours of the mosaics forming the pavement to each front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Stolen Picture and its Peinter.—Mona Lisa, the famous picture which mysteriously disappeared from the Louvre, is the portrait of the third wife of Francesco del Giocoodo. It was painted by the Italian artist Leonardo de Vinci, the great rival of ktichsel Angelo, who lived mostly in Florince, from the years 1452 to 1519 He is said to hive taken four years to paint the stolen maaterpiece. which was finished in 1501. It was acquired for France by Francois 1., and was trenferred first from Fontainebleau to Versailles, and from there to the Louvre, where it was exhibited in the salon cam, the apartment in which Napoleon married Marie-Louisa. Although of obscure birth, Leonardo de Vinci was a man of extraordinary versatile genius. Besides paintinz one of the half-a-dozen pictures of the world, he was poet, sculptor, musician, engineer and philosopher. Ile left designs which showed that he bed some anticipations of the flying machines of the present day. He could cook almost as well as he could paint, and invented the old mechanical roasting jack for the kitchen. The Ginephonograph.—Mr. Edison, who is now on a visit to Europe, is said to have perfected his invention for combining the cinematograph and gramophone. The new machine, which will he capehle of presenting playe, scenery, actors, music, and talk, all inseparable and simultaneous, is apparently to he culled the cineophonograph, and it will be welcomed by the enormous public, whose fancy has been caught by the moving picture entertainment. For keeping records of historical events and departed genies, it may be doubted whether we are making the most use of these mcdern inventions, bict there is no question that weave doing ' this for porp sea of popular amusement. As a form of entertainment, it is a serious competitor to the struggling actor, and will become more so when plays can be presented together with their vocal and ropsicel parts, without employing men and women to pdVforrn them. The " Extras "of Railways Travelling.—Among the grievances of railwaymen is the coo plaint that the lusgsge-inadvance system has greatly reduced the volume of " tips" to porters. Tte companies arc *apposed to hare no cognizance of this " tipping " system, but it has always existed, and was the principal originating muse for this new development in railway travelling. Where a journey invhlved the use of two or thee different lice' passengers found that the cost in tips for handling their impedimenta was often as much as for the carriage of themselves, awl some four or fl e</p>
        <p>years ago they began to make use of facilities, which offered an escape from this burden. Since then, the I lilt:gage-in- advance system has grown to enormous dimensions, despite the drawback that trunks and I baggage suffer a good deal more from rough 'handling than they do when the passenger I accompanies them. If as a result of the adjustment of the railwayroens' grievances we are to pay more for travelling, we shall have less to spend on tine, and one can only say, what a blessing it would be if" tipping " in all its mtnitilations could Le rooted oat of our social system and made illegal. Blackberries.—The drought, as well as the strikes, ii held responsible for a general fruit shortage, which will to relleutert in an increaied cost of jam, accentuated by the higher price of sugar. In Loess circumstances it is a little surprising that we do not make good user of a plentiful crop of blackberries. That there is a market and use for this fruit is 'hewn by the fact that we import hondreds of tons annually from the north of France. Toe French, like ourselves, rather look down on the blackberry, and it is seldom seen on the tables of the middle classes, but they are mare enterprising and industrious, for they pick it for sale in large quantities on the London markets," whereas we leave the bulk of our crop to waste on the heagerows. Old Garden Ornaments.—There is, this summer a further decided demand for quaint old garden ornaments and this particular study is highly interesting, for the introduction of garden statuary and crnamental stone and porphyry work dates far bob, It was, indeed, introduced by the Egyptians, who hooded the art down to the tieeeks and the Romans. So popular did it become during the end of the 17th and beginning of the 18th century, that it can boast a literature of its own, written during that period, when formal gardens had reacted their zenith and the grounds of every historic castle and mansion abouneed with fountains and sun- dials, flights of stone steps, seats and noses, sod groups of classic figures carved in stone, or fashioned of lead. The late King Edward was greatly interested in the revival of old garden ornaments and secured may valuable specimens. Lady Theeddsle, Lady Eisev, Lady ' Brownlow, are well-koown devotees of thin art, and the Duchess of Northumberland likewise collects stone ' ornaments, among the treasures in the gardens of Syon House being some old marble columns, dug cut of the hod of the Tiber. The popular demand, however, is [ for stone vases, decorated-glib floral designs and heads fount airs, dolphins, and elaborate groups of birds and beasts, the latter hailing very frequently from the gardens of old Italian villas. Not a few enterprising women se, at the r reseat mcment. discovering the</p>
        <p>feet that the I uying sad selling of such ornaments is an interesting and lucrative profession. Early Autumn Modes.—This is quite the dullest time of the year, so far es the new modes are concerned. since the Cowan of our country bestow very little attention upon between reason dress. As a rule, summer gowns and wraps are worn until quite late in the autumn, when the season's fashions are more or less di fioitely settled, lint this rule does not obtain in France. There the woman of fashion comdicee the latest summer with the earliest autumn ;nodes, and the result is dem i-sa ison dresses that can be donned on the hottest or the chilliest autumn dada, and which are equelly suitable for either. Then a touch of for, or other seasonable trimming, converts these same dresses into robes that Con be ; orn well into the winter. Our designers are, however, not idle, and a visit to our leading houses makes it poesible to forecast pretty sccurately early winter fashions. Coats, for instance, are to be longer, and some of the new models hove swallow tails. Sailor collars are still popular, but one huge raver takes the place of the two with which we have become so familiar. Velvet forms the revere on tweed and cloth gowns. Short skirts for walking dresses are of course still to be worn, for they are too sensible and practical ever to be replaced by long ones. Panel effects are to continue, and many new skirts are eplit up to show three-cornered piece of a velvet underskirt. Not many buttons are used this season, and the high waist is often suggested by seams at the sine of the coat, and this gives a torque effect, ver7 popular at present. Small Nets Paper.— bashionable women are rising , what is known as the Louis XVI note, which is too small save for the shcr.est invitations. Indeed it looks very like the paper on which children send out notes to their little friends, or writs doll correspondence. Possibly in a few short weeks the" correct" size will be too large to be persuaded when folded and in its envelope, to enter an ordinary letter box, but the wise among u; will have nothing to do with such eccentricities, which however" fashionable" always savour of vulgarity.</p>
        <p>0.P.. ORDERS FIFTY MOTOR-VANS •</p>
        <p>Th." Standard "says i—An order for the supply of fifty Royal mil motor-vans f)r the G.P.O bas been placed with the English Brm of Monis Bros. of Guild- ford, Surrey.</p>
        <p>valued none (it her other companions, and was keen to keep her with her; indeed, so keen that she was determined nobody should maze her acquaintance, for fear the girl should meet with a lover and get married, so when visitors called—and Miss Holdsworth enjoyed seeing people—Joy was told that she could go out for a walk. Nothing loth, the girl would escape from her ' bondage and make a rush across the parade. with its smartly-dressed strollers, to get to the sloping, shingly beach, and, nestling down in a nook of her favourite breakwater, would be almost happy with a book or scribbling with a pencil. One day she had let her sketch-book slip down beside her, and was gazing rather miserably across tile grey ezpanse of sea, thinking of her father, and wondering how long it would be before lie sent for her to come out to him as he had promised, when she suddenly became aware that a mischiev- ous dog had got hold of the book and was worrying it in its teeth. The waves were boisterous, and the tide coming in, but she did not think of the wetting to her feet as she ran after the wicked little terrier who was making havoc of her drawings. " You little beast!" she cried, and at the sound of her voice a man rose up from the other side of the breakwater where he had been lying at full length, with his straw hat tipped over his nose, apparently asleep, and shouted peremptorily to the terrier to "Drop :* "</p>
        <p>"I'm awfully sorry; I'm 'afraid beg done some damage." said the young man, ruefully, as he picked up the gnawed book and en- deavoured to smooth the puckered paper. "By Jove! how clever." he cried. impul- sively. 1114 his casual glence caught the bold- ness of the figures in one of the drawings; evidently portraits taken of people the girl had noticed on the parade. "It doesn't matter," answered Joy, with the ripple of a laugh, then turned scarlet as the leaves fluttered over in the breeze, and behold ! there was the portrait of the figure of the man himself stretched out upon the shingle, with the tip of his nose peeping from below the tilted hat.</p>
        <p>"By Jove !" He looked at her, and their eyes met, hers half laughing but bashful, his full of interest and admiration at her talent.</p>
        <p>"What penalty is there for an artist who dares to take a portrait without permission?" he asked. _</p>
        <p>" The temptation was to much for me, but I am sorry, I will tear it up," she said, hold- ing out her hand for the book with its tell- tale pages. . . , • _</p>
        <p>" I ,:;ould be a pity to do that; supposing you pay the penalty by giving me my own portrait?" said the man, watching Joy's ex- pressive face. "If you demand it—of course," she answered, slightly offended. " I would not demand, I would plead for it as a favour—a very great favour," was his reply; but he save the book into the girl's hand as he spoice. Instantly Joy slit the sketch out and offered it him. " May I really have it?"</p>
        <p>" Ee7tainlYTlt-i;•-wO;ih no more than this pebble," she said, carelessly tossing a stone into the water that the dog tore after to find.</p>
        <p>" You are not polite _to my portrait" _Raid the man, rather ruefully; "as to the worth- lessness, that remains to be seen. I should soy that if you wanted to you could make a good bit of money by your talent." "Could I? i)o you really think so? Oh! please tell me what to do. I want to earn some money .dreadfully, I'm a sort of com- panion now, and I hate it so. I sometimes think I would rather be anything else, no matter what," said the girl, with a sort of despair that brought a look of pity to the man's eyes.</p>
        <p>"Well, then, will you trust me with your sketch-book, and lend it me to shew to a man I know who is down at Brighton just now with me? The fact is, he has started a new paper. and I fancy you might become a regu- lar contributor if you cared to; these little sketches are just the thing, don't you know? I hope you don't think me impertinent?" said the stranger. . . .</p>
        <p>But there wcita nothing in the least degree impertinent about him. and Joy felt inclined to tell him that the hope lie had given her made her happier than she had bean since her parting with the Colonel. "I think you are just too good to me. Twke it, please, and if you can do anything for me. I shall be grateful all my days.". was what she said, with a lovely rush of colour to her cheeks, and a sparkle of light in her eyes—that were grey. with black lashes, and Ken Crawford thought the loveliest eyes be had ever looked into. This has all been unconventional. and we don't know each other's names. Don't you think we may consider ourselves introduced by this rascal here?" he said, flicking one of the ears of the terrier.</p>
        <p>"Why, yes. I think wz. might. My name is Joy Holdsaorth. and I live with my aunt in Greville Gardens—No. 9," replied the girl, frankly. "And mine is Kenneth Crawford. and I am staying at the Old Ship mith my friend Bol- lingbroke. But what is the matter?" or a change had come over Joy's face; it shadowed, and her lip trembled. "You are—Kenneth Crawford?" she fal- tered.</p>
        <p>"Yes—why? Do you know anything about me? Great Scott ! are you a relative of Colonel Holdswortli. who has smashed up?" "I am his daughter; and you are the only creditor my father was unable to pay. But he will—oh believe me. he will one day. He declared be would never rest until he had done so. and—and if I could only make • little money I should he able to help. Mr. Crawford. ray father was foolish. perhaps, and reckless, but he is not dishonest."</p>
        <p>Jove eyes filled with tears, her sweet mouth quivered, her voice shook. It was more than Crawford could stand, and, forget- ting that they were strangers to one an- other, be laid his hands upon the girl's shoul- ders, and looking straight down into those deep eyes of here spoke earnestly. "Please say no more. Your father and I parted friends. That money will be paid. I know—some day; indeed, it has been paid already by this meeting with you to-day. I have heard of you, and have always *anted to knou Joy Holdsworth, and now we m&amp;— like thie!"</p>
        <p>Eine could not speak for some minutes, and turned to walk towards hour., the man and the dog by her side. Slia was thinking that ni.n can be very generous, for the debt to Kenc•li Crawford, the young architect who had designed a riveroid, cottage and spent time and money over the building that was tearly finished, was a heavy one, and hard indeed on • young man only just beginning to get on. They reaehed No. 9, and an they stopped at the imposing portico entrance. Crawford said: " This is au revoir. I hope?" " I don't Anow. I should think von didm't want to see Joy Holdsworth again. You can't make me forget all that we one you. Oh! I wish—"</p>
        <p>"Don't wish anything except that you and I may become friends—i,bs." Slis could say nothing. Somehow. he made her dumb, but he fancied that her fingers re- turned the pressure of his hand; and as he walked away with her book under his arm, he found himself whistling a song that had esinght his fancy. It was called ""the Lose that lives alway."</p>
        <p>"What's the matter pith the child? Graci- ous: Joy. )our cheeks are like peonies. and your eyes blazing ! You look vulgar. I sup- re you have been racing along the beach Oct,. any schuol?i-i—v.oo• bM sum You:</p>
        <p>snoes—wny. toey are B:Axing:- declared MigiS Holdeworth, with annoyance. Joy looked penitently down at her wet feet, gave a pull to her hat to fix it straight. and said she was sorry. She had been going to tell her aunt about her meeting with Ken- neth Crawford, but now she found her in one of her cantankerous moods she resolved to put off the evil moment to a better time. It was meat annoying your being out, for a visitor called to see you—that sister of yours who married for money. and has re- pented ever since I heard. Oh. I didn't see her—not I! I don't bold with a niece of mine marrying a man who is not a gentleman —money doesn't make up for that, I can tell you. So though I don't refuse to let you MIAs meet. I sent is meeinme that you should go and see her, and she sent up word that she would be obliged if I would allow you to go to-morrow night to dine with them at the Corona—just the sort of loud, smart hotel you would expect such people to go to. Bo you are to be there at half-past seven, but t insist on your returning at ten—not a minute later. Your new brother-in-lan will, no doubt, see you home. Now go and change those shoes at once, or I shall have a doctor's bill to pay; and then come and read to me until dinner-time. I've been too long alone; my visitors left me an hour ago. You must. not stay out so long, Joy; I don't like it." It was a dry, tedious book that Joy had to read aloud from for a couple of hours, till her throat was dry, and her eyes ached from the close print; bit she hardly noticed these discomforts, so full of wild delight was her mind.</p>
        <p>It was not Ilte thought of going out to dinner the next evening at the smart hotel and meeting Viola that absorbed her. but the memory of those nice blue eyes looking into hers, diat frank, genial voice, that firm clasp of her hand. Her father's creditor, and yet her friend. Yea, that is how she thought of the man who had all at once brought a dif- ference into her life.. so _grey and dull.</p>
        <p>It was but a little afierjeeven when Joy arrived at the hotel, eager to have a few minutes alone with her sister, whom she had not seen since her marriage three months ago. She was shewn upstairs to Viola'e own suite of rooms. where she was dressing for dinner. But was this Viola who came quickly for. ward to greet her—this girl, or, rather, wo.. man, with haggard cheeks, hollow eyes. all her girlish beauty gone? "Viola!" cried Joy, horrified at the 'eliange that she saw in her sister. "So you think me altered?" answered Viola. with a forced little laugh—that laugh flint always was meant to hide her feelings. "Aren't you happy?" demanded Joy,</p>
        <p>ybroptly. " I am the most wretched woman in the world. Marcus is a master, not a husband. I wish, with all my heart, that I had gone into a milliner's shop; it is what I shall come to. Yes. Joy, if things go on as they are 110,s. I shall leave my husband, and work for my living. as I ought to have done. I haven't a penny for myself—he pays my bills, likes to nee me in fine clothes; but I have to go to him for every farthing. I could not go on the pier to-day because I hadn't twopence to pay. I envy the very maids in the hotel, and scorn myself for my madness. I have paid the penalty, and would welcome poverty now- -with open arms WI gain freedom." Viola spoke rapidly, and with a pa.ssionater fierceness that amazed her sister, who had</p>
        <p>thought that riches would satisfy so frivolous a mind: but they had not. and the married girl's lot was a worse one than her own with the fractious old maid.</p>
        <p>"And I was 'going to beg you to pay the dad's debt to Mr. Crawford-, I felt sure you would be able to. Oh! Viola, lam no sorry for you—"</p>
        <p>There's the gong. We dine at table d'hote, thank Heaven. and not tete.n.tke. You'll enjoy seeing the people. But you look rather dowdy, Joy; that high Motive looks frumpy. Here, quick, twke it oft; here is • new one of mine that will fit you. Isn't it sweet?"</p>
        <p>_ Overruling Joy's °injections. Viola insisted on the chatigs being made, and certainly it WB.l an improvement; the soft. filmy folds of palest pink chiffon and silk that opened in a square at the girl's white neck, the string of pearls round her pretty throat. and a real rose tucked in the coils of her golden -brown hair, made her look, as Viola declared, "quite pretty." Somebody else thought so too, for, as luck would have it, Kenneth Crawford, with his friend Bollingbroke, was seated at the very next little table. 80 near that an Joy took her seat she held out her right hand to clasp that of far new friend.</p>
        <p>"So it i au reroir," he said, gaily; and her smile responded gladly. . .</p>
        <p>IntroductioiLi followed. and the two yming men were invited to change their seats and share the table with the other party; for Marcus Iverson could ba an agreeable host when he chose, as he did now. though occa- sionally his vulgarity jarred a little. ..• .• . • . . .</p>
        <p>Viola thrust 'aside 'her troubles. and Joy basked in the happinesn of the hour. Mr. Bollinghroke whispered to her that he was charmed with her sketches, and she nhould hear from him in a business capacity on the morrow; and Ken Crawford was falling deeper and deeper in love with the daughter of the debtor he had the greatest regard' for. For the next week or two there were. snatched moments on the beach, there were letters that passed between these two—and Letters can mean a great deal; and then one day—the happiest day of Joy's life—he asked her if she thought she could make up her mind to be a poor man's wife. They were seated on the shingle. under the shelter of the breakwater where they had first met, and somehow Ken Crawford had got hold of her little hand and was calmly drawing off her doeskin glove. 'Viols belongs to a rich man. I think should hate it. But are you quite sure you can afford to marry me—Ken?" she said.. with pretty hesitation pronouncing his name. He had the glove off now. so he kissed the tip of each taper finger, sure that there was no on.. near to see him in the, foolish act; and then he told her that he was doing rather well. and that if she liked she could earn her drens money by her sketches. for Bollim(hrolte wanted to engage her on the staff of his new paper, and so— She tilted up her chin in the most provoca- tive fashion, and his head bent nearer till their four lips met. The waves danced and sang an accompani- . .</p>
        <p>mont to love. and Jov neolea elwrier to</p>
        <p>layer, happy and truStful as to the future, that seemed to them both yo full of nun.halp.</p>
        <p>And Ken Crawford wrote to the CoINA bat hi, d.,bt wax more than paid, for Joy ,vas to be hi, wife. And it was only Dfi.is Holdsworth whiz !;rumbled.</p>
