      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>DOCUMENTS.</head>
        <p>DOCUMENTS.</p>
        <p>In 1875 a large strong box was found in a house named from the contents of the box, the" House of Lucius Coecilius Jucundus." This was full of contracts, bonds, and receipts. Jocundus appears to have been a banker and money lender, who had amassed a good deal of wealth. He-transacted loans at the rate of two per cent. per month ; the loans appear to have been for one month although these were doubtless often renewed. What became Of the loans owing to Jucundus at the time of the entombment? An historian tells us that the Roman Emperor Titus devoted the property of those who lost their lives and had no heirs to the relief of the survivors, but what about debts? Did Jucundus survive and collect his debts? Or did an eruption count as equivalent to a year of Jubilee in the Hebrew economy when all debts were cancelled? We annex a translation of one of these documents :</p>
        <p>"Eight thousand five hundred and sixty-two sesterces (seventy-five pounds sterling) in specie. This sum has been credited by L. Coecilius Jucundus on account of the auction of Pullia Lempuris —interest deducted. Pullia Lempuris acknowledges the rec?ipt from L.Coecilius Jucundus. Executed at Pompeii the tenth day before the Kalends of January (23rd December, A.D. 57) under the second consulship of Nero Caesar and of L. Caesius Martial."</p>
        <p>These documents are written on thin pieces of wood covered with wax on the inner side, and folding bookwise on a hinge. The manuscript was scratched upon the wax with a pointed stylus. Next week I hope to deal in detail with the houses and life of the people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>LAU. RIGHTS 11111111•110.1 THE COLONEL'S "JOY."</head>
        <p>LAU. RIGHTS 11111111•110.1 THE COLONEL'S "JOY."</p>
        <p>I3y FLORENCE HOPE Author of "A Love Knot." The Bells of St. Clement',- "April Folly," " A Marriage of Trickery," "A Vivid Impression," ac.</p>
        <p>"What am I to do, Joy, I want them all. Do you think it would be too great an ex- travagance to buy six hats all at once? How can one choose between so many absolutely lovely things, it is too much of an embarras du choix." The girl who spoke PAS possessed of a pretty face that almost any kind of head- gear would suit; she had also a stock figure, and might have done well in a fashionable milliner's shop, or as a lay figure in a smart modiste's establishment. But as she was the elder daughter of Colonel Holdsworth, and See.' much in society, no thought of earning a nithood in either of these capacities entered aer charming head. Joy was drawing caricatures on the back of a theatre programme, and had barely given a glance at the six beautiful hats that adorned the round table in the girls' sitting-room, so absorbed was she in her ridiculous talent that really was rather a clever one. However, she looked up now, with a preoccupied air, and remarked that six hats all at one go was de- cidedly a large order, adding: "And, you know, you have spent all this quarter's dress money already." "You are so unsympathetic, Joy. I believe you would be just as happy in a motor-cap and sack- cloth coat as in velvet and feathers," answered her sister, poising a picture hat on her waved tresses, and regard- ing herself with admiration in the long glass between the windows.</p>
        <p>Joy made no reply, her pencil was at work again, and a whimsical smile twisted her pretty lips; for the particular caricature she was sketching amused her, she had caught the likeness so well.</p>
        <p>The loud bang of a door made her start and drop her pencil. " That's the dad come in. Viola, there's something the matter, he's worried a good deal these last days. and he doesn't sleep, he looks more tired in the Inorning than he does when he goes to bed. '-wat night I could hear him walking up and down his room—up and down, up and down— it bothered me dreadfully. I wish— Here he comes</p>
        <p>The last words were spoken under her breath, and Joy looked sharply at her father as he came into the room.</p>
        <p>He was a tall man, with a military air about him, though he had left the Service many years ago, and given himself up to mak- ing money on the Stock Exchange; had made it rapidly, succeeding in a marvellous manner, and with the extravagance of the spendthrift living up to every penny of his income, en- joying life to the full, and generous to a fault. At this moment lie looked anxious and troubled, and Joy, who was observant, saw that he was pale and there was a nervous twitch of one eyelid. He glanced from one girl to the other, wondering in himself how they would take the news he had brought them—bad news that could be kept back no</p>
        <p>longer. " Halloa, dad, come and help me to choose a hat. I want them all, but—"</p>
        <p>"You can't have one; put them away in their boxes, Viola, they must be sent back to the milliner's. Girls, I have come to tell you that I am ruined; it's my own fault, I risked too much, it was a tempting coup, and it has gone against me. I want you both to give me your help. I want my smash to be an honest Jae, and if we sell everything we possess I think we can face the creditors, and, though lee shall suffer, they won't. Are you going to stand by me?" He spoke out with a force that appealed to Joy intensely, and as he asked the question that he might have put to a couple of men, rathr. than. these young . girls, he stretched</p>
        <p>3ut his hands, one eTch7 Joy gripped the one offered to her, and her eves flashed her willingness to do all that. her .•</p>
        <p>father expected of hei-; but Viola, turning crimson, drew back with anger, astonish- ment, and unbelief all struggling in her ea- pression. " It's not true— what do you mean, father?" she gasped. "Exactly what I say. We have nothing of our own; even your pretty clothes must be sold, your jewellery, your pictures "—glanc- ing at the walls adorned with artists' proofs— "your—your books "—his gaze passing to Joy's cherished library—" all must go, and I must start afresh—work as a clerk if needs be. Joy, little woman, will you face the fire?" "You know that I will, dad, and—yes, I am proud of you, proud that you mean to let us suffer with you, that—that—"—her voice trembled, but grew strong under her self. control—" that you have confidence and trust in your girls. Speak, Viola, say that you are ready too." But the other girl turned on her sister with almost savage indignation. "But I'm not. I think father has behaved abominably; he had no right to bring this disgrace upon us. I ' hate poverty, and I don't intend to be poor. I mean to marry Mr. Leverson and get away from this as soon as possible." " Leverson !" exclaimed her father. "Mr. Leverson!" echoed Joy, and snatch- ing at the clever little sketch that had pleased her so much, she tore it to bits. "You don't know what you are saying, Viola, you can't marry a man whom you have treated with contempt—scorned one day, and half-encouraged the next; besides, he is not a gentleman, he is a cad!" cried Joy. " He has loads of money, he can give me all . . .</p>
        <p>these hats, all the clothes- i want; lie can take</p>
        <p>me out of the very thought of poverty. I'm not made to be poor, I couldn't struggle, I haven't it in me; we are different, you and I. I shall write and ask Mr. Leverson to 'iome and see me this afternoon, before he 'sears of the crash. I intend him to propose ;.ct me, and I mean to accept him." " He will have to see me first and hear the truth„'" said Colonel Holdsworth, sternly. . .</p>
        <p>Vioh laughed, but there was no miith in the laugh that jarred upon the ears of her father and sister, and abruptly she quitted the room. .</p>
        <p>Colonel Holdsworth sank down on a chair near the table, pushing a costly hat away from him, and thrusting out his arms with a gesture of bitter despair. " I might have known she would take it so, and yet—yet I thought that she, too, had some grit in her. Poor Viola!" Then, turning to tll3 other girl, a mist came over his eyes, and a tremor in his voice. "Joy, little girl, what can I say to you? You i are the light n my darkness, the silver lining to the cloud, my—joy!" His voice broke in a husky sob, and the girl's arms went round him, her wet cheek was pressed against his ro she whispered, ten- deny:</p>
        <p>--;:iion't mind, daddy dear, we'll face the Ere together."</p>
        <p>But this, alas ! was denied them, for the Colonel had a chance offered him to go to America, and, for the present at any rate, it was considered wiser to leave Joy behind with her Aunt Isabel. a maiden lady living at Brighton, who offered to give Joy a home in return for her services as a companion and amanuensis. Viola had chosen her own way, and was married quietly and quickly to Marcus Leverson, avho never ceased to remind her of his generosity in taking compassion on her in her poverty-stricken st cte. She had other suitors, but none so wealthy as the middle - aged jew. who purchased his wife as he would have bought a valuable piece of furniture to adorn his establishment, • gor- geous mansion at Chislehurst. Perhaps hers was the hardest fate of the three. The Colonel had the excitement of a new life, and the struggle to mend his fallen for- tunes, and Joy—dear, brave little Joy—did what her father thought best, and entered her new existence determined to make the best of it; but it was not easy this life with her Aunt Isabel—far from it, for Miss Holds. worth •as exacting, selfish, and ceaselessly complaining, and after wearing out the nerves of her former lady companions, she v. 38 trying her heM to do the same with her And vet sh valued her as she had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sundsy last, p-osi ged ow by Mr. flimawortb. Mr. Parkes gave the • I 1'... ups." The Ministry of Serving." taking as his text our Lords own words when he said "Ii came not to be ministered tinto, but to minister. ' Christian people were not to expect to be helped, their colcsra should bete be of help to others. lisose who have in all times given themselves to tbe ministry of serrin4. have received • Dame, that has been handed down t. all time, great men of old, In the political world, in the literary world and the miebaterial world. Their name, are banded down on men who hare faithfully served God and their fellows. Let Gs cultivate the spirit of work and service. Mr. Mcgachren and Mr. A. Healey also took psrt in the meeting. Mr. Payton metaled at the organ. The secretary s .id the P.S.A. Savings Sank would be open for deposit on Sunday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>FOOTBALL NOTES.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL NOTES.</p>
        <p>By " Ovallst. "</p>
        <p>The season opens, the new stand is to be opened and Warrington list of victories should also open tc-day. Leigh are, as usual, Warrington's first opponents, and the occasion is to be taken advantage of for a ceremonial opening of the new stand at 3 o'clock by the President of the Club (Mr. T. Locker, J.P.), with the Mayor and Aldermen and ladies in attendance. Lord Derby (President of the N.C.) was invited to perform the opening ceremony', bat aprior engagement prevented his acceptance. I understand that he has, however, promised t attend a match at Wilderspool later in the season.</p>
        <p>There is rod news for the Warrington supporters ' this week. On Wednesday evening the Club signed on W. Woodburn. the crack wing three-quarter of the Coventry NC. Club. •Last Eeason Woodburn, who hails from tilverston, played as an amateur for Covent, y and gained quite a distinguished repu'ation, not only at Coventry but all over the Union. Con- sidering that he was playing with a side whivh won very few match, s, his record of 23 tries and 36 goals was a remarkable one, and speaks well for his prowess on the right wing. I well remember the capital game he played, and the fine try he scored against War- rington in the Cup-tie at Coventry. With youth on his side Woodburn is one of the most promising players in the Un'on, and Warrington are to be con- gratulated on having secured his signature to a professional form. He ia 23 years old this mooth, stands 5 feet 9 inches tall, and weighs eleven stotes.</p>
        <p>There is reasonah'e hope of a class centre being secured are long, and then the team will be something like itself again. Ssveral of the juniors who have been tried in the last week or two have sh Aim dis- tinct prom'se and ought to develop rapidly in the -A" team.</p>
        <p>The team to meet Leigh to-day will be as Mows: Tilley, full back ; Bradshaw, Dickenson, Unsworth, and Woodburn, three-.luarters ; Brooks and Creevey, half hanks ; Johnston, Stingers, A. Boardman, Thomas, Cheaters, and Shelhorn, forwards.</p>
        <p>Nut much fault can be found with the above Thirteen. Tilley gets the preference over Mclntyre for the simple reason that he has put in more practices mid shown better form than the younger man. Waywell is hurt, and Bradshaw has played such a capital game in practice that he once more gets his chance in first-class company. He has been • disappointine player for the first team, though excellent among the juniors, and I should be glad to see him justify expectations. Unesvortb, Brooks, and Creevey have all played fine football in the trials, :Ind the same may be said of the forwmds who should be capable of holding their own in scrummage work with the best.</p>
        <p>The L igh team will be : Bolewski ; Jolansm (S), Johnson (T. II.), Atkinson, Ganley ; Whittaker, licGeiver ; Gallop, Davies, Marsh, Lowe, Lee, Barton.</p>
        <p>September will be a busy month for the Wire- pullers, and with six fixtures they should have a good chance of making headway. After to-day they meet St. Helens (a), Hull (h), Barrow (a), Huddersfield (a) And Swintoa (h). Their supporters would like to see at least five of these six matches won. Pity up Wi repullers !</p>
        <p>The League fixtures for to-day are as follow : York v Batley. Halifax v Bradford. Bromley v Wakefield Trinity. Wigan v Broughton Rangers. Runcorn v Coventry. Dewsbury v Hunslet. Ebbw Vale v Huddersfield. Leeds v Hull. Barrow v St Helens Hull Kingston Rovers v Oldham. Warrington v Leigh. Salford v Rochdale Homets Swinton v Widnes.</p>
        <p>The " A " team to meet Leigh " A " at Leigh will be : Mclntyre, fall-hack ; Clare, Critchley, Taylor, and Davies, threcquarters ; Lloyd and Jordan, half- backs; Naylor, Harmer, Bracklehurst, Gibbs, P. Boardman, and A. Bradshaw, forwards.</p>
        <p>Davies, Critchley and Brocklel-arat are young players who have shaped well in practice, the first- named being frcut South Wales. Daintith is a compulsory alAentee owing to injury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CRICKET, BOWLING, AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>CRICKET, BOWLING, AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket Notes.</p>
        <p>Warrington were entertained by Wigan last Satnr day, and the result was a draw, which naturally did not afford much satisfaction to either side. Croirg in first on a splendid wicket, Wigan gave ienesed evidence of strong batting talent, declaring at the fine total of 182 for 8 wickets. Tyler and Selkirk made splendid ai titers obtaining 36 and 47 respectively. Tyler was in capital form and made many forcing strokes over the wicket. He withstood the bowling of Partridge and Paten, but eventually when trying to send the, +mil to the leg boundary he placed it into Partridge's I hands. It is interesting to note that Tyler has obtained over 900 runs this season. Selkirk wielded the willow in forceful style and young Woodcock had hard luck in not being responsible for checking his career when early on, for two splendid catches were missed. A. Jones was also in form and rapidly knccked up 28, the next man on the list being C. Armstrong with 20 runs to his credit.</p>
        <p>When the game concluded Warrington stood at PS runs for 4 wickets. Richardson was in rare form and his 3,-; rues were obtained without a faulty stroke. He made sme crispy cuts and gave the fielders a great deal of trouble. S. Partridge was an excellent partner and gave a capital exhibition of driving power. He hardly gave a chance, throughout a keen display.</p>
        <p>For Warrington B. Si[cock was the most successful with the ball, obtaining 4 wickets for 39 rune, while for Wigan. Broughton secured the colours, capturing 3 wickets for 27 runs.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington end District League there was a ,greet surprise, Warrington Conservatives going down -to Walker's Brewery. Walker's men were in form, and knocked up 105 for 6 wickets, and the Conserva- t.res responded with 61.</p>
        <p>Orford and Whitecrosa Rees met on the ground of the former. It was a bowler's afternoon, and each side was only able to raise Ist, the match thus ending ins draw.</p>
        <p>Sankey triumphed over Wilderspool Brewery by 44 runs to 49. For Sankey, T. Whitfield batted splendidly, obtaining 35 run; in capital style. The only other on his pile to reach double:figures was E. Whitfield, who compiled 17. A. Smith, with 13 runs, was the best batsman far the Brewery. Millington. of Sankey, was respoosib'e for 5 wickets, and Taylor of the Brewery, captured 7 wickets.</p>
        <p>The following is the League Table op-to•date : Pitt Woo Lost Drn. Pt!. Crg't'n Causer. l5 ... 13 ... 2 ... 0 ... 213 Ssnkey l4 10 3 ... 1 ... 21 Whitecross Rec Sankey Wire 'Mills 16 ... 7 ... 7 .... 2 ... 16 Walkers Brewery Latchford Wes. l3 ... 4 7 2 ... 10 Orford l4 Rvissis Bros Wildsrs'pl Brewery 14 ... 2 ... 12 0 ... 4</p>
        <p>Winwick Asylum were completely out-classed when they entertained Manchester Y.M.C.A. Winwick batted first and wera dismissed for 41. Manchester went in after the interval, and a short delay on account of the rain, and knocked 55 for the loss of ti wickets. R lin again intervened and put an end to the game. The bawling and fielding of the visitors was very good, a particularly fine catch being made by T. Clegg, who lied t run at full speed and caught the ball about aa inch from the ground. Cunningham bowled well for the home team capturing 3 wickets for 21 runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Bowling Notes. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT BOWLING</head>
        <p>Bowling Notes. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT BOWLING</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. INDIVIDFAL MERIT COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Rain greeted late arrivals at the King and Queen last Saturday. There was every indication of a rough time, but the only effect of the downpour was to make the top greasy ; in fact, after the preliminary stages of the competition, there was more sunshine. So far as the attendance was concerned, it was in every way satisfactory. To-day will witness the final, and the programme arranged shows interesting sport. I don't know any competition where such a fine lot of players will toe the line as to-day, and the quatrion is being asked who is going to be the conqueror? As in many tbi-gs, time will tell ! Consistency rather than the brilliancy, was the feature of min), games played last Saturday. rAM.</p>
        <p>W.simfaloTE V. W,</p>
        <p>Norg•ove, with youth on his side, played a faultless game against W. Cottam. Though it was on the card that the Cemetery representative would get home, Norgrove made much headway, and gained the prolonged game by one chalk 21-20.</p>
        <p>SVOIT V. E. SHAW.</p>
        <p>A': Scott gave an exhibition in no polished a style that Shaw was simply non-plussed. Scott won comfortably - 21-9.</p>
        <p>R. MA.KCI.I. V. ,1 VON.</p>
        <p>These two club mates gave a character;stic game. Neither of them could make much headway, nor could anym esp t the winner. It took 32 ends to complete the game. Vernon only required one chalk at the 2At't end, and it seemed scarcely credible that be failed to get the necessary one chalk when he had yards of room to net home. Maskell must be com- plimsnted on his victory, 21-20.</p>
        <p>D. BRoMLET V. J. 111DIRD.</p>
        <p>Bromley gave a delightful display. He very soon polished (ft Torn Hackett in the 2nd mind. George Holbrook could not stand up against such bowling in the preliminary round, and Jonathan High.= failed also. Branley taking such a commanding noeition punished Jonathan to the tune of 21.11. Bromley deserves t 3 be amongst the prizes.</p>
        <p>kI.P.ERT EA \C T. N. TIELDIN.</p>
        <p>Thee two players had the utmost confidence The outstanding haiure of their bowling was the remark- able male they play( d. It was ui doubtedly aln Mem pale. Gleave's success was due to excel!ent bowling, in fact at times you could plau3 a pocket handkerchief over all the four woods and limit. Fielding playing soundly and well was hardly beaten by 21-13.</p>
        <p>E 111.ACKLEDGE.</p>
        <p>There was a mildly sensational finish to this game. The scorer calling 20 all, Jolley then won by one chalk. It was an excellent game all through. Had Jolley been defeated it. would have been a severe blow to his club mates. They think that they have the winner from the Bull's Head Club.</p>
        <p>R. SUNDERLAND V. J. MoNFS.</p>
        <p>Funderland's averages have invariably been remark- able and be added to his list of victories on Saturd ly last by a game which was worth going a long way to witness. For Sunderland to score 17 chalks off tbe reel was a good display. Monks caught four points, but Sunderlan4 proceeded in deliberate fashion, wincing easily 21-5.</p>
        <p>T HUMPHREYS</p>
        <p>Hutuphreys had to go all the way to beat young Riley. At the twelfth end Riley was in front with fur chalks, but Tom had a punishing mood on and polled a hard game out of the fire by 3 chalks. Humphreys end Albert Gleava meet in to-day's encounter and it should be a splendid game.</p>
        <p>G. ,TIAW, JEN. N. 11. LAW:HORN. This was a ding dong game. Experience told, and Shaw put, in all he knew, to shake cif Langhorn. Shaw had the satisfaction of sinning by the odd chalk 21-20.</p>
        <p>T. QUAURIE Y. I AKIN. Tom Quarrie did excellently in defeating Ike Makin. Makin opened with &amp;single, but Tom capturing the block did what be liked. His length was perfect and occasionally be got the woods away et isply.</p>
        <p>A. JOLLEY V. TOM TATIES.</p>
        <p>A. Jolley failed to produce the form be gave against George Ball jun. the previous week. At the fourteenth end Davies was in front by 13 chalks. Jolley tried hard, but had to retire defeated by 12 chalks. Tom Davies was quite est op by his victory.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON DISTRICT. 1.0 0.F., 3LU. ANNUAL HANDICAP TO-DAY. The annual handicap of the above-named district will take place to-day on the green attached to the Bowling Green Hotel, Bank Quay. There is an entry of 77 and the winner will be entitled to bold, for 12 months, the handsome silver challenge cup the gift of Bro. Sir Gilbert Greenell, Bart., C.V.O. It may be mentioned for future guidance that the conditions in connection with this handic ip are such as to entitle members of the district who are members of clubs affiliated to tha Ltacashire County Ama'eur Bowling .Association to enter. Toe following is the entry and draw for the Seat round .1 Sankey 6,v W Peskin 5. Hankinson 5, • II Whitelegg 6. .1 Hurst 4, v W Holbrook 6. T Bolton sc , • Joseph Gleave sc. W Barland 6, • W Roughley sc. W Butterworth 1, v A Ponniogt ,n 4. G Holbrook sc., v A Lawton 2. C Cross 5, R M Tilling 2. II McNishon 3, • J W Davies 3. A Glover 3, v C Whittaker 4. J Lowe 4, • J Wright 3. T P Bradshaw 4, • 0 Wilcox 4. J Poisons 1, v F Clare 3. Byes :—W Bibby, 2 ; J Hitchmongh, 3 ; P McLachlan, 4.; W Pemberton, ; F' Wright,4 ; W E Midileton, 5; J Savage, 3 ; P Lester,G ; W Challoner, 4 ;J Broad- hurst. 9 Walter Daniels, 4 ; W Webster, 6 ; J 11 Teare, 6; W E Clare. 4; A Clare, 3; W H Ball, 1 ; A Duckworth, 3; 0 Monks, 2; H Halford, 2 ; 'l' Watson, 6; Alfred Glee, 1 ; H Taylor. 3 ; J Burrows, 5; F Freemen, 5 ; W Jones, ; 0 Garner, 4 ; Ernest Glom, so; W DeWitt', 6 ; H Faulkner, 3 ; W Gleave, leen., 1 ; .1 IV lienwright, 6; T Perry, jun., 3 ; R Shaw. 3; John Gime, no; F Webster, 4; A E Hare, 6; E Llewellyn,</p>
        <p>4; W Holloway, 2; 8 Taylor, ; W Miller, 3; C Taylor, 2; W Hammett, 4 ; 11 Broaiburst,3; T P Forbes, 5; W Taylor, 3 ; AE Atkinson, 3 ; F Bradborn, 4 ; G Higham, 6; F Rigby, 2; P E Croft, 3 ; H Stringer, 5. Play to commence at 2 o'clock. Time limit 30 minutes.</p>
      </div>
