      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WHITECROSS LIBERALS AT BOWLS.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS LIBERALS AT BOWLS.</p>
        <p>Several Liberal committees of the various wards have this season enjoys.," a bowling main either at Penketh or Sankey and the Whitecross committee on Saturday indulged in similar sport on the beautiful green in Heath-lane, Penketh. Some CO players com- peted for the 16 handsome pri/es offered. After the second round tea was partaken of and was much enjoyed by the members who were enthnsisatic as to the catering of Mr. Jackson. The prize winners were as follows—l, Messrs Hardy clock and vases ; 2, C Nunnerley, case of carvers; 3, W Jones, copper kettle; 4, H lionnion, gent's umbrella; 5, F W Williams, cruet ; 6, J Rigby, clock; 7,11 Farrington, case of spoons ;8, C Mescisin, pipe in case ; 9, G Wilcock, pipe in case ; 10, W Prinsep, moor; 11, G Walker, preserve dish ; 12, W Hancock, set of brushes ; 13, 11 Edaards, tobacco pouch; 14, T Richardson, fluster vase ; 15,1 Knox, penknife ; 16, J Rudge, teapot and stand. Mrs. James Dodds distributed the prizes.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Dutton (bon. secretary) said how pleased they all were that afternoon to have with them Mrs. James Dodda to present the prizes. When he asked her if she would come along she said she was very willing to do all she could for Liberals and to make the main a success.</p>
        <p>After presenting the prizes Mrs. Dodds made a short speech in the course of which she said thst she had enjoyed witnessing the bowling very touch. She was very glad they had assembled on a teetotal green and it only showed that they could spend an enjoyable time on a green other than one attached toe publuo-bouse.(Hear, bear.) She hoped it would not he the last occasion on which the would see them at bowls. (Applause). On the propoeit ion of Mr. J Elwell seconded by Mr. A. Edwards, a vote of thsnke was heartily accorded to Mrs. Dodds and donors of the prizes whose names were appended :—Slessre P Peacock, A II Crosfleld, T Pemberton, J Bailey, Councillor Locker, T Plizston Councillor .1 Hawthorn and N Hough. The excellent arrangements were in the bands of • committee consisting of Mews. J Elwell, A Edwards, A Buchanan, H Thomas, A Thomas, G Robinsin, H Edwards, T Ashworth and J Dutton hon secretary). LADIES ANNUAL BOWLING MAIN.</p>
        <p>At the Cemetery Hotel on Thursday, glorious weather and the prospects of the most interesting ladies day for yearn drew a splendid entry of 53 competiton compared with 63 last year. There was a splendid gathering. The ladics for years have given a welcome to this red letter day which is a perennizl. It was quite an arena of fashion with felt covered shapes and other specimens of autumn millinery. The ofiloials did excellent justice and are to be complimented on the expeditious manner In which they conducted the competition : 31esars J Willett (chairman), A Forster, C Smith, J Baldwin, Horace George Wilcox, Alf. Ford, Jim Roberts, Harry Melbourne, Tom Garnett and J E Cruobam. Mn Allison tindly entertained the ladies to afternoon tea, which was fully appreciated. The draw was an interesting one, past winners Miss Ilialsam and Mrs W Cottam, being together. Miss iligham came out the winner by a majority of flee. There was write a flutter when the defeated competitors learned that a consolation competitition would Le played. As had been foreshadowed the prizes were a splendid collection.</p>
      </div>
