      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FORESTERS' MAIN.</head>
        <p>FORESTERS' MAIN.</p>
        <p>About 100 members of the Warringtnn District Ancient Order of Foresters journeyed on Satnrday last to the Sankey Chapel House, to indulge in their fourth annual bowling main and tea. The weather was all that could be desired. and everyone seemed in happy holiday mood. Eighty-five members entered the bowling main, and the committe very quickly realized that a prompt start was necessiry to get through before dark. The final round was being played aJout seven o'clock, and great praise is due to the committee for the splendid manner they organised the whole thing through whilst there was plenty of light. A hearty vote of thanks was passed to the host tald hostess, Mr. and Mrs. Unsworth, for the splendid tea they provided, and although the number to be provided for far exceeded their expectation', they rose to the occasion in a highly comrceidable manner. After tea the room was cleared, and the members assembled, and the District Chief Ranger Bro. George iklaybury distributed the prizes in suitable terms to the winners, in the following order of merit :—F Davenport copper tea urn suitably Inscribed •, E Shaw, pair of Marley horses J H Holline, pair of trousers to measure It Marsh, bar master ; .1 Dagnall, copper kettle : W Gleave, cruet ; r Clare, copper kettle; W James, jam dish ; J T tlelnhy, net of brushes ; W Gibbins, new hat ; J Crosland, pipe and case ; W Bibby, copper kettle; J Turner, 53. worth goods; L Des ion, net of military brushes ; W Roden, bottle of whisky ; W Critchley, pipe and case ; D Glea,e, boAle of whisky ; G W It Baines, pipe and cam, ; W Delooze bottle of whisky ; .1 Hamer, pouch and tobacco ; M Brookes, box cigarettes ; and the following were recipients of very serviceable prizes J Davies, J Massey, J Barlow, S Fletcher, A Law, II Erumerson and El Perdue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Strike Interferes With Food Supplies.</p>
        <p>PTOMAINE POISONING SEQUEL</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held in the boardroom at the Workhouse on Wednesday evening. Mr George Mather presided, and there were present Mrs Simpson, Miss Broadbent, Messrs E L Bent, G Allen. J Billinge, A Brooks, W C Brooks, W Flanagan, W Furness, W Gill, A J Jolley, W Knowles, R Lee, Rev A M Mitchell, M W Seton, A Bottomley (clerk), R I Partridge (assistant cleik).</p>
        <p>NO !,I:IIGARI!SI.I,I3i'T :JAM !</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Huse Committee held on August 25th, the Master reported that the only food shortage experienced daring the strike was margarine. He purchased in lieu, therefore, marmalade for the inmates breakfast and jam for their teas, all ,wing two ounces for each person.</p>
        <p>DOM AN CA mitics AD DENOMINATIONAL HOMES. At a meeting of the Cottage Homes Committee held on Angust 29, it was recommended that 20 girls or any number not exceeding 20—orphans and adopted—be sent to certified homes under the Liverpool Roman Catholic Diocesan Society.</p>
        <p>THE DOCTOR'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>REV. A. M. MITCHELL AND VACCINATION. Mr. A. J. JonnY formally moved that the salary of Dr. Thomas Taylor, resident medical officer at the Workhouse be increased from £125 to £l5O per annum. Mr. BENT seconlei the motion and the chairman was putting the vote when The Rev A M MITCHELL rrse atd said, " Step a minute, the question of salaries is a very important one, especially when the ratepayers are concerned. " He did not know whetter there were any grounds for the iecrease in salary—he had not heard of say and he xauld like ta know a little more about the matter , before the application was granted. He would like to I know what the agreement between the Board and the doctor covered and whether it stated that there was to beau advauce at ce:tain stated times?</p>
        <p>. The CHAIRMAN : This matter has beta before the House Committee and the Finance Committse. The increase of salary had been asked for by the doctor because he had served a long lime in the Institution. Rev A M MITCHELL : What is the length of time? The CHAIRMAN : Fourteen or fifteen months. The Rev A M MITCHELL: It is rather early, but I do not wish to make any further comment on that. I ehould like to know more about the agreement and whether or not the resident doctor was the medical officer?</p>
        <p>_ . The CHAIRMAN: You give notice to the Clerk about that—it has nothing to do with this. Mr. MITCHELL : It is in connection with the Doctor's duties. I want to know whether when the agreement was signed the Doctor undertook the duties of vaccination officer to the Workhouse.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : The agreement is not here, but if you call at the Clerk's office he will show it you. Mr. MITCHELL : Ido not want to call at the (Ace. The CLERK: The Vaccination Officer is Dr. Elodgkiosoo, who is the Medicll Olcer of the House Mr. MITCHELL : Does Dr. Ta;lor vaccinate ? The CLERK : As Dr. Hodgkinaon's deputy I suppose he does. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Um HELL : Has he it salary for that? The CLERK : He has no fees for that. H Dr. Hodgkinson cares he can give him something, but he tuts no salary whatever over and above what you give him. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL said he would not raise any opposition to the increase it it were quite clear that Dr. Taylor was not the vacciaation officer. . .</p>
        <p>The motion to increase Dr. Taylor's salary was then carried unanimously. The Rev. A. it. MIT,IIELL asked that at the next alerting a return be submitted to the Board staring the number of children born lathe House daring ths last vaccination year and showing the number of children vaccinated and ale, their ages. NEW DINING lIAI.L AND KITCHENS</p>
        <p>The tender of Messrs. W. sad A..-111;t-o'n'of Bridgo :cot for the erection of kitchens and a dining hall was accepted. The tender amounted to £3,375. Six fi,ms competed.</p>
        <p>PTOMAINE POISONING SEQUEL. WHO IS TO PAY THE ANALYST'S RILL ? . . _</p>
        <p>The CLERK read a latter from Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (Town Clerk) stating that Professor Delapine, of Manchester, had sent in an account of £l5 153. for making an examination of the food alleged to have caused ptomaine poisoning amongst the nurses at the Workhouse a few months ago. The Town Clerk said be was requested to ask if the Board of Guardians would be good enough to make a contribution to the same. Mr. W. C. BROOKS : Who authorised Professor Delapine to make the examination The CHAIRMAN: The Health Authority. Mr. liNowtes ; Let those who called the tune pay the piper. Besides we are only • little body com- pared with the Corporation. Rev. A. M. MITCHELL I think we ought to do something. Mr. W. C. Bnoons : When we asked for the report we were refused. Rev. A. M. MITCHELL mooed that the account be divided between the Board and the Corporation. Mr. BENT moved that the letter from the Town Clerk be referred to the House Committee. 31r.A. BROOKS seconded and the Rev. A.M.Mirenxtr. withdrew his motion.</p>
        <p>Mr. 13E31.'8 motion was agreed to. The estimate of expenditure for the half-year ending 31st Marco, 1912 was stennitted. Among the chief items were the following</p>
        <p>C1131111"11 ill ti Chalget. Entrada are Natintatel for the halt- !X pendit ure year entiett for the half- 3:et MYrch, 1911. yearentling 31 March. 1912.</p>
        <p>..d. £s.d. IninsintenanceWorkhoose :3,835 IS 7 .?„soo o o Iomgintennece Schools Out-repel (including you</p>
        <p>resident Poor) Lunatics in Asylums 2,509 7 9 2,500 0 0 LOtins (Instalment and Interest) Salaries of Officers Officers Rations (Workhouse) 643 5 0 640 0 0 Officers' Rations (Schools Paupers in Hospitals and Schools Vaccination Union Af seta ment Committee Espouses Amouut required £13,300; corresponding half-year, £13.900. . ..... . . _</p>
        <p>Balancein Tresnra's band.,ltb August 1911,E,1,210 Estimated balsinceat end of present balt-pmr,</p>
        <p>TUC L,TINI.ATV:i.</p>
        <p>The ninon C3mmittes approved and adopred the estimate and recommended that the calls he made upon the overseers of the different t,wnships within the Union for the ,urna of £13,350.</p>
