      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>LESSON OF THE STRIKES.</head>
        <p>THE LESSON OF THE STRIKES.</p>
        <p>MR. LL. GEORGE ON THE BURDEN OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>The Respasibility of the Churches.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd George, addressing a gathering of Free Church members in Glainorgan on Tuesday made extended reference to the lesion of the recent indus- tr:al troubles.</p>
        <p>The Chancellor of the Ev:hequer, accompanied by Mrs. and the Misses Olwen and Megan Lloyd George. , visited the mining village of Seven Sisters for the purpose of laying the Imodatim-stone of a now Baptist chapel. The party motored from Criccieth and were met at the entrance to the village by the local band and a large gathering of representative men from various parts :4 the Principality. At the luncheon which f, llowed the stone-laying ceremony the Chancellor of the Exchequer made an important reference to the recent industrial troubles and the lessons to be learned from them.</p>
        <p>A !section of the English press, he said, hail called ths people of Wales half civilized, if civilized at all." Those papers, however, were very careful not to refer to similar evenwi at Liverpool and Belfast where skulls were broken to show intensity of faith in the Christian religion, and these occurrences would happen at Liverpool and Belfast as well as at. Llanelly and Tredegar and they were to be very much deplored, but the best thing that the critics could do, instead of making prejudical racial comments, wattld be to look at things in a spirit of sobriety.</p>
        <p>TIIE ANANIAi OF TO-DAL.</p>
        <p>They had witnessedi tidal wave of impatience with the harshneas of our economic eunditions sweeping over the land. and he we, not cure if the Churches realized the reiponsibility which called upon them to contend with the symptoms and cure them, for dm Church had a material as well as a spiritual aspect and duty. Multitudes might not believe ii the promise of the unseen until they saw a better realiza- tion of the promise. There were certain people to-day who talked about Ananias. Ananias was the man who made a false return of the property he had sohl in order to reduce his contribution to the community to which be belonged. There were many men of this type to-day, and responsibilities had iccreased enormously since the time of Ananias.</p>
        <p>THE FIRST RESPONSIBILITY OF THE cRRISTIAN CIICRCIIES</p>
        <p>was to set right wrongs from which the masses of the country were suffering. There were multitudes in the country who, in spite of grinding toil, did not earn enough to keep body and soul together. On the other hand, there were ethers who toiled not neither did they spin, and yet they possessed a superabund- ance of this world's goad things. As long as these conditions existed they would have these outbreaks. It would be said that he was setting class against I class. That was just the parrot-screech of every barren scribbler and tattler who did not possess enough imagination to invent a new phrase of invective. If it were a criminal offence against society to call atteutisrn to the inequality which existed to-day and every day in life, then all he could say was that the social conditions and organizations called for immediate readjustment.</p>
        <p>THE WO!IST OF SINS,</p>
        <p>Let them examine the coulitions under which hundreds of thousands—nay, millions—of people sought to earn a living for themselves aud families. and if they thcught these satisfactory he would advise them to live for s;x months upon the wages of these people. That would cure them of the worst of sins uncharitableness.</p>
        <p>They had just emerged from what might have been one of the most disastrous catastrophes which etuld have happened, and they now had time for reflection. Let them honestly search out the causes and not recriminate. If they did not honestly set themselves to the recmnal of those causes the next strike would be infinitely wolse. The community was becoming more intelligent anl would not be imposed upm. No self-respecting peop!e could endure more of these conditions, and unless the Christian Churches aseisted in remedying , them they would be left behind. He entreated them on behalf of the people, the natiou and the Churches, to realize their relmnsibilities before it was too late, and in the spirit of self-sacrifice, which was the essential spirit to lift the people up to the level of a great life.</p>
      </div>
