      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Medical.</head>
        <p>Medical.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SA CURDAY. SEPTEMBER 2, 1911</p>
        <p>There appears to be</p>
        <p>THE TRADE some danger,arising out DISPUTES ACT. of recent events, of cer-</p>
        <p>tam n sections of th e community losing their sense of perspective regarding the Trade Disputes Act of 1906. A week ago the London Chamber of Commerce, evidently suffering from panic, passed a resolution calling for the repeal of the operative sections of the Act. They sent this out to other Chambers of Commerce and to associations of employers in all parts of the country, and it was suggested that resolutions and protests should be sent to the Prime Minister, the Board of Trade, the Home Office, and so forth. That is the explanation of the Chamber of Commerce activity during the last week, and it also explains the proceedings at the annual meetings of the Associated Chambers of Commerce that have been held in Dublin this week. One and all the resolutions being sent up call for the repeal of sections 2 and 4 of the Trade Disputes Act, and, to judge from the speeches, it is more section 2 than section 4 about which the Chambers are most con- cerned. Section 2of the Act is that which defines the law in regard to picketing during a strike, while section 4 is that which relieves trades unions from the possibility of actions for damages. To repeal these sections, as is suggested, would leave trade-union law where it was left regarding picketing by the case of Lyon v. Wilkin in 1899, a case which practically made the most peaceful of picketing illegal, and where it was left regarding damages by the Taff Vale decision which meant that a trade union could be mulcted in heavy damages for the irrespon- ible act of any of its members. These two decisions created great unrest among the members of trades unions. A Royal Com- mission was appointed by Mr. BALFOUR in' 1903, but, recognising that as a mere artifice for getting rid of an inconvenient problem, the trades unions declined to give evidence before it. They complained, moreover, that they were not represented on it This Commission reported in February, 1906, but its report was inconclusive, and the present Government brought in its bill later in the same year.</p>
        <p>Now it is safe to say that if the same cir- cumstances arose again Parliament would do the same thing it previously did. There is some confusion of thought among those who are crying out for a repeal of the provisions allowing picketing. Without picketing strikes become impossible, and it is very late in the day to want to deny the workman the right to strike. But there is all the difference in the world between what the law allows and what those who are calling for its repeal bave in their minds. The law allows picketing, which must, however, be peaceful, as must' also any attempt to persuade" blacklegs" not to work. The law does not allow violence, and any breaches of the peace or attempts at intimidation can be dealt with under the ordinary law without the necessity of repeal- ing section 2of the Trade Disputes Act. The circular issued from the Home Office a week or two ago accurately describes what is</p>
        <p>Medical.</p>
        <p>1711 HY be Itsld Viughen's" Hairsrower " rpsedily V produces luxuriant noir. Most satoombing results. Trial bon 64.—Y. Varemaa, 30, Forrest Avenue, Woo.] Green, Losslon N.</p>
        <p>A C:OUCHMENT.—Comfortable APARTMENTS tor ia-patieat ; very moderate terms. !triter Pzimer. 15, Mindy rd, Fairfield, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>FOR MORS TIRAN TWENTY-F/I'R YRARS- DR. DAVIS'S FAMOUS !NASALS PILLS _ - • -- • . . .</p>
        <p>I'AVE been -u-n-i;;;Foliy-adinittedVe-i; a Boos to Womankind. They are the but hooero Remedy fcr Anemia, diddineer, Fulness sod Swelling after Meals. Loss of Appetite, Hysteria% Palpitation of the Heart, Debility, Depression Weakness, Irregnleritien, and all Female Ailmenta. Boxes Is. lA., 2s. N., 48.6 d. and lla. (Sample rent tree). Only addrees :- 809,PORTOBELLO RD.. NOTT/NO HILL, LONDON</p>
        <p>possible, and makes it clear that all the law allows is that a striker may stop and speak to a " blackleg" if he is " willing to listen." There is nothing here that allows interfer- ence, intimidation, the unseating of carmen, assaults on engine-drivers, or insults to a non-striker's wife and family. All these things can be punished now as they could before 1906, and to hold the Trade Disputes Act responsible for them is futile. It may be true that an interpretation of the law has been allowed to grow up which never should have been allowed, and we are inclined to think that if the Home Office had given the lead to local watch committees and joint standing committees in 1906 that it gave a fortnight ago much trouble would have been saved. As for section 4, something had to be done to protect the funds of the unions from being raided because of the acts of some individual member, who might be entirely ovt of sympathy with his fellows and by a wanton act land the union in bankruptcy, and we doubt whether even the most reactionary of Tory Govezpments would be willing to bring the law back le kitte R 7-1906 position. Let it be remembered that 4e House of Lords, which was actuated by no -</p>
        <p>- love for Liberal legislation in 1906, passed the bill without a murmur because it was not considered favourable ground for the Unionist Party. We believe that the great bulk of trades unionists are law abiding, even in times of unrest. The law can and ought to deal with violence, and it can do so without the necessity for a repealing Act.</p>
        <p>Sir WILLIAM RAMSAY'S WASTING THE address to the British NATIONAL Association is one of RESOURCES. those utterances calcu- lated to make men take some thought for the future. That part of the address which will attract most atten- tion deals with the coal supply of the nation, and really warns us that we are wasting what has been one of the chief sources of the nation's wealth and greatness. He calculates that if the rate of working our coal supply goes on increasing as it has done of recent years, our coal will be completely exhausted in 175 years. He sees no sign of our consumption of coal going down until such time as it will grow dearer owing to its approaching exhaustion. We had a Royal Commission on Coal Supplies in 1903, which reported in 1906, and Sir WILLIAM laments that we have done nothing in the meantime to deal with the gravity of the case as then shown to us. The usual reply of the man in the street is that before the coal-supply is exhausted some other means of supplying energy 'will have been discovered, and it is therefore interesting to see what a world-famed sciertist like Sir WILLIAM RAMSEY has to say on this point. He tells us that, having been struck by the optimism of his non-scientific friends on this subject, he suggested that a committee of the British Science Guild should be formed to investigate our available sources of energy. This committee took into consideration all the sources of energy which have been suggested—the possibility of using the tides, the internal heat of the earth, the winds, solar heat, water power, the extension of forests, and the use of woods and peat as fuels, an I lastly, the possibility of controlling the undoubted but slow disintegration of the elements, with a view of utilising their stored-up energy. One after another Sir WILLIAM rules all these out. It would be impracticable to attempt to use terrestrial heat from bore holes, the amount of energy to be obtained from wind and tide is very small in comparison with that derived from the burning of coal,nothillg can be hoped for in this uncertain climate in the way of utilising solar heat, it would be folly to look to the liberation of energy by atomic change, and so forth.</p>
        <p>What then are we to do? The period of 175 years is not long in the life of a nation, and if we are to continue to support the vast population of these islands the concentration of energy will be required in the future as in the past. First of all,Sir WILLIAM advocates that we should not waste coal as we now do. We must rely chiefly on our coal reserve for our supply of energy, and it is to scientific vconomy in this respect that we must first look if the life of the nation is to be pro- longed. He shows us many ways of econo- mising. By the substitution of turbine engines for reciprocating engines we can reduce the amount of coal required per horse power from four or five pounds to one and a-half of two pounds. By the further replacement of turbines by gas engineswe can raise the pro- portion of available energy got out of a given amount of coal, and by creating power at the pit-mouth and distributing it electrically we can still further economise. The domestic fire problem is also treated by this famous scientist. He tells us that there is probably no single remedy applicable to this great source of waste, but the introduction of central heating,of gas fires and of grates which permit of better utilisation of fuel will all help. He also recommends that greater attention should be paid to forestry and to the utilisation of our stores of peat. But his chief recommendation is one that will com- mend itself to the commonsense of the com- munity. He asks for a permanent Commis- mission whose duty it shall be to keep abreast with the diminution of the stores of natural energy and to take steps to lessen its rate. We ought, he tells us, to take stock annually, and to pro- mote legislation which will endeavour to lessen the squandering of the national resources. The problem is no doubt a big one, but it is certainly one that ought to be tackled, and not allowed to drift merely because it will not become acute in our time. A proper scheme of forestry will,for instance, take at least 100 years before it becomes a paying concern yielding an adequate return for the expense and trouble put into it. That is a matter which in the nature of things cannot be left to private initiative. It is a national matter, and should be taken up by the nation as a whole. Perhaps we may find as time goes on, that the newly-formed Development Commission will take in hand some of the problems hinted at in this very arresting address.</p>
        <p>One may reasonably THE MUSICAL express surprise at the SOCIETY, high quality of the harmony produced by the Musical Society when, for years past, it has suffered from a heart-ache which looks like becoming chronic. Season after season the Society provides the Warrington public with high-class concerts,and just as regularly the hon. treasurer presents a financial statement which shows a discouraging ' deficit. A year ago there was a loss on three concerts of £ll 4s. 7d., and this year there is a loss on four concerts of £B4 lls. Bd., which points plainly to the fact that in giving an extra concert the Society was pleasing only itself and adding unneccessarily to its liabilities. The members refuse to be dis- couraged however, and face the future with a confidence and determination beyond all praise. If possible, the public must be weaned from its taste for merely shallow entertainment, and taught to appreciate music for music's sake. Let us hope that brighter days are dawning for the Society which has played such a conspicuous part in the social and educative life of the town. With a new President at the helm in the person of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., C.V.0., there is room for optimism. He has promised to do all he can to make the Society a success in the coming year—and we have faith in his promises.</p>
        <p>One grave aspect of the A SCRAMBLE FOR Labour unrest that has PARTY CAPITAL roused. the country to</p>
        <p>a vivid realisation- of problems which had been almost ignored, is being seriously dealt with by the Railway Commission which the Government has appointed. Another aspect, perhaps even larger and graver, that is the demoralising effect of casual labour, yet awaits treatment, though the Insurance Bill offers a certain amount of inducement to the employers of casual lab3ur to decasualise it. While these problems are engaging serious attention from the Government, from experts, and the public, the Tory Party has been occupied in a ludicrous scramble to make capital out of them.</p>
        <p>The Tory Party has WASTED—AN felt itself debarred EXPLANATION. from denying the</p>
        <p>reality of the grievances underlying the labour unrest because they have conceived the imbecile notion that they can reconstruct out of admitted grievances a case for Tariff Reform. So they attack the Government because they say that the speeches of Mr. Lloyd George and Mr. Churchill have encouraged the men in the transport sarvices to strike for a relief against incontestable grievances, and they declare that Tariff Reform is the sole and the satisfactory remedy for these grievances. How the transport services are to benefit by a tariff which would reduce their employ- ment, or how higher wages are to be guaranteed to the dwindled number of employed, the resources of the most resourceful Tariff Reformer have yet failed to explain.</p>
        <p>•-77 The attitude of the AN INSIDIOUS Opposition tothe Insur- ATTACK. ance Bill is still very</p>
        <p>ambiguous. In the House of Commons they claim credit for treating a great problem in a non-Party fashion, and helping the Government to perfect and complete their scheme. Outside, they have not shrunk from any opportunity of misrepresentation to cover the scheme with unpopularity, and so capture votes from the Government. A manifesto of the Unionist Members in the Press, while dealing with matters of detail, certainly conveyed the impression that it was intended as an indict- ment, which could be used upon Tory plat- forms throughout the country The Tories tried, and ignominiously failed, after the pointed attack on the Old Age Pensions Bill was found to be quite ineffective, to pose as the champions of a much more generous measure, on the strength of an amendment intended to make the Bill impracticable. Are they" trying on" the same tactics against the Insurance Bill in a more subtle manner?</p>
        <p>If only partisan passion ULSTER WILL 133 did not blind men to the RIGHT, real bearing of their acts it would be astounding that the Tory Opposition to Home Rule should take such grotesque forms. Preparations are being made for a great Belfast demon- stration in October, when Sir E. Carson will bring the prestige of an ex-Solicitor General of the last Tory Government to encourage an organised provocation of disorder. A resolution is to be moved in favour of armed resistance against the law, if Parliament passes a law which Orangemen dislike. Why the promoters of this demonstation should imagine that the people of Great Britain would be favourably impressed by so flagrant a demonstration that Ulster loyalty depends solely upon the predominance of a small clique, whose pre- dominance, in turn, depends upon the suppression of the rights of a majority by the claims of a minority, it is mere waste of time to try to understand. No government can afford to ignore legitimate grievances, even when they are put forward by a very small section. But a grievance that a small privileged minority is no longer to be allowed to ride rough-shod over the majority of a nation, when put forward as a claim of "loyalists," who will resist by arms any attempt to establish a more equitable state of things—well, what are the terms which are appropriate to describe such a protest? However, time will smooth down such eccentricities, and "Ulster will not fight, and Ulster will be right."</p>
        <p>At an inquest at Leigh VACCINATION IN THE Workhouse on two WORKHOUSE. children aged 23 days,</p>
        <p>who died last Wednes day, it was stated that both were perfectly healthy before being vaccinated on 11th August, with ordinary calf lymph. Then one developed a rash and the other sank gradually. The verdict stated that death resulted from convulsions caused by septic poisoning arising from vaccination. These are by no means the first cases of this kind (says the "Morning Loader.") So recently as February last an inquest was held on the body of a child in Wandsworth Workhouse, vaccinated when 18 days old. If it be objected that such cases are merely the exaggerations or misrepresentations of anti- vaccinators, the simole answer is that the practice has attracted the notice and con- demnation of the Local Government Board, which in December, 1909, told the Liverpool ' Workhouse committee that "vaccination should not be offered to a child in a work- house before the age of two months." Is it any wonder, that when, even in the face of this authoritative declaration on the part of the L.G.B. itself, cases of children vaccinated in workhouses at 18 and 23 days old are brought to light, "anti-vaccinators," in the words of the " British Medical Journal," appear to thin'c that a considerable amount of compulsion takes place in work- houses? It is, of course, only the minority of cases where the child dies at once from the excessive zeal of the doctor that the public ever hears of it. We note in passing that the Vaccination Orders, by providing that the doctor shall be paid so much per head, appear to do everything possible to encourage a practice most difficult in any case to repress effectually. We have no doubt the temptation is honorably resisted ; but why should it exist at all? Why should not the insignificant c.)st and labour of workhouse vaccinations be included in a general contract in a form which would not amount to a premium on the abuse of the doctors' powers and position?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>li:j NOTES AND NOTIONS. A-.,,,,,,,.."</head>
        <p>li:j NOTES AND NOTIONS. A-.,,,,,,,.."</p>
        <p>From a list prepared by the Medical Officer of Health we learn that of 14,661 houses in the borough there are 48! unoocupied at rents varying from Is. 9d. to 17.5. 63. per week.</p>
        <p>The riotous campawign against th 3 high prices of food in France should be instructive to those Tariff Reformers who a few days ago were arguing that food would be, cheapened if only the people of this country would consent to a little tax upon all that they consumed. Clearly matters do not work out in that simple way across the Channel, and we do not suppose t'ley would here!</p>
        <p>Eighty-six patrons a the Victoria Park Bowling Green have petitioned the Town Hall Parks and Baths Committee to allow mineral waters to be sold at the green, and the committee has decided, " a 3 the season is so far advanced " to defer the matter till next year. We wonder why? Surely the question could have been decided affirmatively in five minutes, and the thirst of the bowlers reasonably assuaged. •</p>
        <p>The Warrington 'Bridge contractors have seized the vacant space at Bridge Foot, and boarded it in as a storage yard. There will consequently never be any more public meetings on this familiar site, for when the work is finished the new Bridge itself will encroach considerably on the land. Good- bye to the weekly play ground of the Tariff Reformers ! Will they deign to share the Cannons site, on different 'nights, with the Liberals and Progressive Labourites ? * * *</p>
        <p>The Borough Auditors, Messrs. B. Silc3ck, r. Pemberton, and W. J. Wilson (Mayor's Auditor) have been voluntarily granted an increased remuneration from £9O to .£l2O by the Finance Committee. For once, the work has been done thoroughly, and on pro- fessional lines. During the year ending March 31st last the auditors put in over 400 hours at their corporate duties and cannot, therefore, be considered over-paid. It rests with themselves as to how the money is divided.</p>
        <p>Some good " howlers ', are reported from a recent school examination in Brooklyn. "The cow has a pulse as well as anybody else, but you cannot f!el it at his wrist.' "The blood flows through the alimentary canal into the abdominal canopy." "Shakes- peare was a fiction and allegorical writer. His most intimate friend was rare Ben Butler, who was also a great fiction writer." This answer will not perhaps be as readily appreciated in England as in America, where Ben Butler was a once prominent, not to say notorious, politician. The best " howler" on the list is that " William the Conqueror was the first of the Mormons." • •</p>
        <p>There is a significant note under the minutes of the Water Committee for August 2l st, wherein approval is given of the action of the Chairman of the Committee in authorising the purchase of 50 tons of coal at an increased price, to provide against emerg- ency, during the recent strike. We believe it is a fact that the dislocation of the rail- way services found both the Water and Electricity Departments without any coal reserves, and there was, consequently, some scurrying about to secure enough fuel</p>
        <p>for a fortnight. It would be advisable for departments such as these,which are respon- sible for public services, to make a practice of always having enough coal in stock to tide them over such difficulties. The hand to mouth policy is too precarious in these days of labour unrest.</p>
        <p>• - The new maid had a bad habit of standing with her mouth agape, and her mistress didn't like it. "Mary ! " she exclaimed sharply, one day at dinner, "your mouth is open ! " Yessum," repliad Mary, " opened it."</p>
        <p>'*" The test of a pleasure is the memory of it. Happiness is not doing what we like, but liking what we do. I Stop watching for chances to turn up. Hustle out and turn them up. Life is a book. Read it carefully, for you can read it only once. Build castles in the air by all means—then put foundations under them.</p>
        <p>With Canon Sc Dtt liolland at Christchurch and Dr. Gore as the new bishop of the diocese, advanced Radicalism will be well represented henceforward in the councils of the Church at Oxford, and doubtless their influence will not fail to make itself felt in a wider sphere as well. Throughout the whole ranks of the Church it would probably be difficult to find two men who hold more democratic views or proclaim them more unflinchingly. • 0</p>
        <p>Here is a capital- wakes story from Oldham, which illustrates the typical humour of the cotton operative. A man and his wife were returning from their holiday after a lively week. He remarked to her : " Well, lass, ah reckon we're about spent up ?" "Ay, ah reckon we are, but we shanno' be quite beawt when we getten whoam." "Why, heaw dosta meean lass ?" "Well, ah put 39 shillin' under th' candle- stick up o' th' mantel afore we coorn away." " Ay, an' ah fun' it</p>
        <p>o - We learn that an objection has been laid by Mr. S. Gough against the Parliamentary vote of Councillor Henry Roberts, and it is likely that the case bsfore the Revising Barrister will be somewhat of a cause celebre. Mr. Roberts is not the man to lose his franchise and qualification for the Town Council, without a struggle. Nor would anyone wish to see him cast out of office for a technical irregularity wnen there is so much, in municipal affairs to-day, which requires the guidancs of expsriencsd coun- sellors like Mr. Roberts.</p>
        <p>• Here is what is said to be a schoolboy's essay on the camel :—" The cannimal is a sheep of the desert. It is called a backteria because it has a hump on its back. The cannimal is very patient, and will lie down and die without a groan, but when it is angry it gets its back up, which is called taking the hump. The shepherds of cannimals is called Arabs. When they live in towns they are called street Arabs. When the cannimal goes on a journey it drinks as much as it can to last for many days. Such animals are called acquiducts. Those that cannot carry enough are called inebriates I" * * *</p>
        <p>To-day is the 72nd anniversary of the birth of Henry George, the author of" Pro- gress and Poverty" and other famous writ- ings. The movement for the Taxation of Land Values is founded on the doctrines so ably set forth in Henry George's undying I works, and dates from the time of the publi- cation of '` Progress and Poverty" in 1879. From San Franisco and New York this movement has spread into every corner of the world. It has encountered and over- come the strenuous opposition of privileged interests. Treated at first with contempt cud scorn it has captured men's minds and is now the dominating issue in the politics of -every progressive country. 4 * *</p>
        <p>The best justifioaiion For th 3 &amp;Alan of the Government in calling out the troops is, per- haps, to be found (says the" Liverpool Post") in the failure of any attempt to work up popular feeling against the employment of the military. The demonstration which was held in Trafalgar-square on Saturday, under the auspices of the Social Democratic Party and the Transport Workers' Federa- tion, to protest against the use of the troops, was a complete fiasco. The transport workers,apparently, were otherwise engaged. At all events, the indignation with which they are supposed to be burning did not bring them as far as Trafalgar-square, and the demonstration, if it can be dignified by that title, was only attended by about one thousand persons.</p>
        <p>- Those who are in a position to foreshadow coming events in the trade union world tell me (says a London correspondent) that the next agitation of the skilled workers will be for an eight-hour day all round. Every- where there are signs that the unions are preparing for a conflict on this issue. In the shipbuilding and engineering trades the matter has been forced to the front by the boilermakers, and the executive of the eighteen federated unions who signed the Edinburgh agreement, have endorsed the action of the "Black Squad." They have asked the executive of the various unions concerned to report on ways and means of giving effect to their desires. Further, by a large 'majority of the members voting, the British Steel Smelters Society have been instructed to arrange a conference with the employers at once to demand an eight-hour day. _ _</p>
        <p>" S The appointment of Dr. Gore, Bishop of Birmingham, to the See of Oxford appears to be singularly appropriate. He was, it will be remembered, for a time Canon at West- minster Abbey, and he made his influence felt over vast masses by his powers in the pulpit. Great regret was expressed in many quarters when Dr. Gore left London. For two years he was Bishop of Worcester. It was in 1905 that he went to Birmingham. His influence there has been very marked, and it has been said by a Minister of the Crown that the Bishop, by his teaching and example, had "changed the Christianity of the Midlands." Dr. Gore does not label himself with a political description. But if the description " Socialism " means a warm interest in social questions and a fervid desire for the improvement of the masses then is he a Socialist. He has a powerful voice, a rare gift of exposition, and a deep knowledge of human nature. Dr. Gore is the author of many books on theological questions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>GREAT BRITAIN LEADS THE WAY.</head>
        <p>GREAT BRITAIN LEADS THE WAY.</p>
        <p>You need not be alarmed about the present or the future of England. She is greater thst ever she was, and a hundred years ber,ce the historian will point back to the Budget of 1909 as the turning paint in the history, not of England only, but of the world, when the great principle was first embodied in British legisla• tion, which led to the uplifting of the nations and to the emancipation of the aIavee.—STANDARD, N.S.W.</p>
        <p>" That we shout] do unto lottiers as we would have them do unto us—that wellshould tespect the rights of others as scrupulously as we would have our rights respected—is not a mere co nisei of perfection to individuals—but it is the taw to which we must con- form social institutions mud national policy—if we would secure the bleasioga of ainudauce and peace." —HENRY HEOR(.E.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gerard N. Ford, JP., of Manchester, (Preident of the Congregational Union of England and Wales) will preach at Wycliffe Congregational Church to- morrow, both morning and evening. To-morrow the new Wesleyan ministers will occupy various pulpits in the circuit. On Thursday the public recognition meeting wEI take place in Bold- street Wesleyan Church. Mr. T. It. Openshaw, of Blackburn, will preach the anniversary sermons at Brick-street Mission t.-morrow. In the afternoon a cantata entitled : " Under the palms" will be rendered by the Sunday school and church choirs combined. . . _ . . . . _</p>
        <p>It ie announced by the Btitish Women's Temper- ance Association that the Rev. R. J. Patterson L .LB., author of the "Catch my Pal " movement will be in Warrington on November lit. His Worship the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) will attend divine service at Holy Trinity Church to-morrow morning. Members of the Council are to assemble at the To.vn Ball at 10 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Mask in the Park. Messrs. Brewery Band will play in the Palk on Sunday afternoon.</p>
        <p>A few Boiled BABY CARRIAGES to be sold cheap.— Apply J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st.,-Warriugton.-Advt. Warrington Socialist Society. The Warrington Branch of the Independent Labour Party, situ,te at Artingstall's Buildings, will in future be known as the Warrington Socialist Socie•y. This Roe decided at a specially summoned mettiug of the branch on Monday last.</p>
        <p>Strike at Messrs. Rylands Bros, Ltd. Atout 100 youths employe' at Messrs. Ilylands, Bros., Ld., Church-street, left er,rk on Monday, as they considered their wages insufficient. The youths were engaged in the '' wet" wire department and they declared that they desired to be put on" piece" work inste id of on dry wage a, at preF.ent. H ppily, the strike has been amicab'y sett'ed and they returced to work co Wednesday Light.</p>
        <p>MOTOR CARS FOR Hi RR, 10Og Or short dis'socee—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st.„ 1% arrington.—Ativt. Picnic to Windermere.</p>
        <p>Thicemployecs of Mes,rs W. Winstanley and CO., held their annual picnic and jruinejed to Windermere on Saturday last. The whole of the arrargements ,ere left in the hands of Mears Dean and Davison, with exceilent results. The party consisted of 31 employees and the two principals, Mr. Winstanley and Mr.Pitcbford. They left Warrington at 7.15 a m., leaving Lakeside on the return journey at 7.30 p m , having spent a met enjoyable day.</p>
        <p>- - A Retiring Athletic Hero. li is interesting to tA:ite that Mr. John Cropper, residing at 53, China-street, Liverpool, is through medical advice ietiring fram addrtics. Cropper is nos 46 years of age anl was associated with St. Paul's School. Heathside, in early life. He thsn attended the Liverpool Icsti;nte and after studying there for several years qualified as a medical dispenser. He has always taken the keenest iater.st in athletics especially in swimming and has b:en the means of saving no less than 59 lives frmi drowning.</p>
        <p>WRINGING MACH [ES, best value obtainable. Cash price, 39/G.—J S. GARLAND, Ironmonger Bridge at., Itianiagton.—Advt. To-day's Cricket Teams. . .</p>
        <p>The. War ringtrn. senior team are due to play at Sale to-day', and N't ill be represented by the following : Measre. 11. Silcock, W. Richardson, J. Callon, S Partridge, S. Baxt. r, 11. Woodcock, W. Bennett, A. Duckworth, H. Speakman, I'. Dixon, and A. N. Other. Train leaves Arpley station at 112 p m.—The 2nd eleven will e. terta'n Sale 2cd with the following team : W. V. Turbo, R. H. I,Ave, H. Dolan, J Tlavrthlrn, H. Hawthorn, J. R. Cheaters, T. Williams, J. Br adburn, A. Gleave, R. LightfoJt and H. Partridge. Wickets will be pitched at 2.15 pm. prompt. CLEARANCE SALE OF CFCLES, all prices rrelocsd.—J S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mrs. C. E Parker. On Thursday afternoon the committee of the Women's Liberal Association were invited to meet at the residence of Mrs. Osborce, hon. secretary, for the purpose of making a preseotatioa to Mrs. C. E. Parker, hon. president, of a pair of silver t, zz is, on behalf of the officers and commttee who served under her presidency. The occasion was tha silver wedding of Mr and Mrs. Parker. After introductory remarks by Ms. Osborne, the presentation was made by Mm. Walker, who referred to the esteem and regard felt for Mrs. Parker, and gave expression ts the good wishes of all those who had canttibuted.--Mrs. Parker, to whom the glt was quite unexpected, responded in a felicitous sperch. FOLEY CHINA TEA SERVTCES, 40 pieces 12,1.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st, Warrington.—Advt. Tramway Men's Picnic. • •</p>
        <p>Last Tuesday will long be remembered as a red letter day by a poi rion of the tramway staff. Pre- parations had been proceeding for some time for this event, especially in anticipation of the bowling main. which was a prominent part of the programme, Rumour had it that some had been practising a great deal of late, and others were bolted upon as " dark horses." The party which numbered 24 left Rylands- street, at 1.20, in a chara-a-banc, fcr the Stamford Arms, Bolliegten. The weather was all that could be desired, and the drive was thou:highly enjoyed, the country looking its best. On arrival at the Stamford Arms, the party were received and welcomed by the host, Mr. Jefferson. After a brief interval, howling commenced. The green was in fine condition and some really good games were played. Some of course were novices and easily disposed of, whilst others proved adepts at the game. At six o'clock an adjournment was made for tea. This was of no mean order, the host and hostess having provided a regal repast, to which full justice was done. Later in the evening an impromptu concert was held, songs being given by Mr. Jefferson and Mimes Speed, Melia, Dunn and others. The talent was good, each item being well received. A good number of valuable *prizes bad been given by loc .1 tradesmen and these were presented to the victors. Before leaving for home the hearty thanks of all present was accorded to the donors of prizes for their great kindness, to the host and hostess for their catering, to the officials, chief of whom met .rmen W. Warburton and E. Kenwright were specially mentioned, and last but not least, the manager Mr. Teller, for his kindness in so arranging the men's duties, as to allow of their absence. Warrington was reached in safety, shortly before 11 p.m., one and all being perfectly satisfied, and voting the outing one of the best.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>BEREAVED CABINET MINISTER.</head>
        <p>BEREAVED CABINET MINISTER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Buxton's Eldest Son Dies</p>
        <p>After Operation.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of Mr. Charles I Sydney Buxton, the eldest son of the President of the Board of Trade, who succumbed at Nortiother House, I Hurst Pierpoint, near Brighton, on Thursday, following an operation for appendicitis. The deceased, who was born on May :Vith. 1884, was eiucated at Eton and then at Balliol College, Oxford, where he held an exhilition. Amongst his successes was e first in history. On leaving Oxford he was appointed Vice-principal of Ruskin College, Oxford, the working man's college. Mr. Buxton remained there till December, 190 y, keeping the infititution going with great VICCPIIII pending the appointment of a new principal. He then became private secretary to his father. In the General Election ofhanary,l9:o,be stood for the Woodbridge Division of Suffolk in the Liberal interest against Captain R. P. Peel, but was defeated. The sad news will arouse sympathetic feelings in the breasts of many Warrington people, for the Right Hon. Sydney Buxton bee twice, within recent years, visited the town. The last occasion was for the opening of the new General Post Office, Mr. Bolton being then Post- master General.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY. Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>MUSICAL SOCIETY. Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>NEXT SEASON S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Musical Society was held on Thursday evening, Sir Gilbert Greensll, Bert, C.V.0., presided over a fair attendance. He was supported by Messrs. J. P. Reynolds (hon. treasurer) and Lionel Fairclough (bon. secretary). In the Course of his report, Mr. FAIR. LOUGH mentioned that the number of members of the society was 230, made up as follows ;-80 sopranos, 73 cootraltoe, 25 tenors and 37 basses—chorus of 215—and 35 members of the orchestra. There were 35 rehearsals of the ohorus and 37 bend practices. The average attendance of the chorus was ss follows .-46 sopranos, 141 contraltos, 13 tenors and IS basses-121. Mr. REYNOLDS submitted the statement of accounts which showed that on the concert account there was a loss of .£64 its 6d, as compared with £ll Os 7d, in the previous year. The receipts included :—Members' subscriptions £59 17 64 ; concert subscriptions £126 lOs ed ; and sale of tickets and programmes £B4 Ils 84. The expenses included, professional fees .£230 7s Gd, and hire of schools, hall and music £5l 13s 2d. . . . .</p>
        <p>The balance sheet of the Opera section submitted by Mr. Henry Bond (hon trees showed that the each taken at the Theatre was £372 Ns 3d. The expenses included the following items —Costumes, wigs and make-up £56 Is 3d and proprietor Ro3al Court Theatre £s9 Ils 9d. She profit on the opera amounted to .£29 Cs 9d.</p>
        <p>r.HEVNOI.Di said there was a slight falling off in members' subscriptions. That was sot serious, but in the concert subscriptions there had been a loss of £l3 12s. 6d. due to resignations, deaths and removals. But the biggest lose of all was on the sale of ticketta and programme. In 190-10 the amount raised win E 129 Os. ld. and in 1010.11 only £ll4 16s. 3d. The difference did not seem to be very material at first sight, but last season there was an extra concert so that there was a very considerable falling off. He appealed to the members to do what they could in the obtaining of more subscribers. Mr. IL T. FAIRCLOUGH in moving the adoption of the accounts said he had been a member of the Society for 41 years and he had never known fgeir concerts in one season to pay. He thought they would do far better if they only had three concerts in one season in the future. Ssiith regard to the subscriptic ns, two years ago he suggested that certain members of the Society should take different wards and ask the people to become subscribers. In that they were very succeesful and he thought they might work en similar lines again. Mr. RErSoLDS agreed with the suggestion of Mr. ' Faircloughaod said that prior to the cenvas the concert subscriptions amounted to £76 and afterwards to £llO. The difficulty was in getting members to canvas year ef,er year. Mr. A. FIELDI-10tE seconded the motion that the accounts be adopted. He mentioned that the com- mittee bad decided to have only three concerts next seaeon. . . .</p>
        <p>The accounts were adopted. SIR GILBERT AND MUSIC.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOCKII proposed that Sir Gilbert Greenell be asked to become president again. He said be had taken a great interest in the society for a good many year,. lie remembered the t ime when Sir Gilbert used to sing in the society. His sisters wtre also members for some years. (Applause). Mr. W. E. ATKIN seconded the motion. He hoped that at the end of the season they would have a better balance sheet. (Applause.) The resolution was carried enthusiastically. Sir GILBERT GREENALL thanked the meeting for re-electing him as president. He hoped that at the next annual meeting the society would he in a more flourish- ing condition. Ile thought that three concerts were sufficient for one season. There were so many other attractions—picturedromes and music halls—tuat he thought ccncerts could perhaps te overdone. It was much better to have three concerts end make them a success than to have four and make a failure. They could rely on him to do all he could to make the society a success during the coming year. (Applause). The officers were re-elected and thanked for their services. The vice-presidents were re-elected and also the committee en bloc except Mr. W. H. Wallington who through ill-health sent in his rtsignation. dr. W. [tinkles was elected as his successor. Mr. PAYTON moved a vote of thanks and sympathy to Mr. Wallington and Mr. It. T. FAirtctouurr in seconding said Mr. liVallingtoo lad been s member of the society for neatly 40 years ad he had worked very hard.</p>
        <p>The motion was carried unanimously. NEXT SEASON'S PROGRANIME.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSSLEY said he would give an idea of what the Society intended doing during the forthcoming season. After thanking the officials and members for assisting him in his work as conductor, he said that the balance sheet was a great dissppeintment to them all, because last reason they really did try to make a good programme. (Hear, hear.) On the whole, he thought it was the fluent season, musically speaking, that they had attempted in his time,l which extended over 20 years. However, if they had not had success financially, they could take ' comfort from the fact that there was hardly a single musical society in the kingdom which gained anything last season, They knew that with the Halle concerts the loss was £1,200 and all the societies in ' Yorkshire hod a loss. Still the Warrington society was going on in the hope of having less troubla in the future and they were doing some good musically. He thought the educational work which they were doing would receive the support of all. They all got a great deal of enjoyment. out of the work and really the last concert—when they gave Berlioz's " Faust "—was delightful, although he thought they did not allow sufficient time to get it op. With regard to next season it would be better to curtail expenses and that element had been considered in the drawing up of the programme. Three concerts bad teen decided upon by the committee and they were all to take place on Wednesdays—November 22nd, lanuiry 3lel, and March 20th. Looking at the work of the past season they found that the concert on which they lost least was on the miscel- laneous and orchestral concert, sod he proposed that they should have two miscellaneous concerts this season. lathe first concert the principd work for the chorus would ha the c mtats Tne Blattlt Knight," by Sir Edward Eiger. There were no solos in it and it wee a short wcrk which would take about 40 minutes. Then the orchestra would SIB 3 have some things to do. Arrangements had been made for a local pianist, Mr. W. Pearce—who was making a naniefor himself—to play Griees Concerto in A minor. (Applause). A lady vocalist would also be engaged, The second concert would also be m!swilaneors with a little more prominence attached to the orchestra. They suggested having an instrumental soloist and also one vocalist. At the third conceit they intended to give Dvorak's " Stalilt Mater "—a work which bad never been done in Warrington yet. It was a most delightful work and could be done either in Latin or in Englishlle wanted the members to choose. It was a little aw:eward to sing in Latin and some people did not Me it. That piece would take nearly the whole of the evening and the rest could be occupied by a vocalist. From the outline he had given it would he seen that they were having fewer profeesional vocalists, which meant a consider. able saving in fees. He announced that the first choral preemie* would be held on September 19th. Sir Gilbert was thanked for presiding</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>FATHER BERNARD VAUGHAN REPUDIATES</head>
        <p>FATHER BERNARD VAUGHAN REPUDIATES</p>
        <p>SYNDICALISM.</p>
        <p>Father Itrrnarti Vanghao, speaking. n Monday at St. Mary's School, Inverness, avid that England had been invaded by Syndicalism, and be thought it might to meet with the same firm handling there as it had met with in France from M. Briand. In France Syndicalist!' had paralysed the industrial life of the country, and during its short reign of terror Lad cost France £12,000,000. Syndicalism in England, which was a euphonistic name for trade unionism tyranny, Lad for its objective the triumph of what Mr. lieir Ilardie called the revolutionary spirit"—anarchy. How long asked Father Vaughan, were law-abiding citizens to be exposed to outbursts of irresponsible strike agitators? Even German Socialists had set themselves against S)ndicslism, which if tolerated would mean the disruption of their Emgire ? It was high time for England to pass such legislation as would prevent the repetition of these displays of anarchy and reigns of tenor which had so recently menaced the life itself of the nation. Syndicating should be given short shrift. G44%111)009 there were in plenty, but lawleesnen and anarchy were *no remedy. Penury was not cured by starvation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>CAMPAIGN FOR STATE RAILWAYS.</head>
        <p>CAMPAIGN FOR STATE RAILWAYS.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. C. Anderson, chairman of the Inds pendent Labour Party, announces that the party is beginning a campaign in favour of railway nationalisation. Tbia is the second campaign under the mi.., system adopted by tte I.L.P. The first, which has been running through the summer, is in favour of a minimum wage of 130 s. a week for all workers.</p>
        <p>It is intend.' that every meeting held under 1.1.. P. 'Lupins diaing.the nest few mostbs shall deal with nine phase of the railway questioe, leading up to the purchase of the railway eystems of tb.s coorcry by the State.</p>
        <p>OPERATION ON 1-IR. BARNES, M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. N. Barnes, M.P., who has been taken ill at his residence at Streatham, vras yesterday removed to Guy's Hospital for en operation, which was performed last evening. and, in the opinion of the surgeon, was quite:satisfactory BO far.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FOUNDER OF BRIGHTER</head>
        <p>FOUNDER OF BRIGHTER</p>
        <p>SUNDAYS.</p>
        <p>MR. R. M. MORRELL ON THE LEAGUE S EARLY STRUGGLES.</p>
        <p>In a pretty little garden in Tollington-p ark a silver- haired old man eat on Thursday in the sunshine. receiving by wire, by post, and in person, congnstnla- tions from all sorts of peop'e upon his eighty-eighth birthday. . . .</p>
        <p>This 'cid man, who has helped to brighten tl lives of thousands uprs thousands of his fellow coun rymen was Mr. It. M. Morrell, the founder of the N iortal Sunday 'Argue. Just 58 years ago Mr. Morr 'l, on his thirtieth birthday, being then a goldsmith's ore- man, determined to do what he could to bring joys of pure air, music, art, science and harmle recretttion into the people's Sunday. Now, after one nal( a csu.tuiy, he finds himself the " father " of a huge organisation, whcse beneficence is accepted nationally and officially. "From that time forward we struggled for oNer 30 years against calumny, vilification, and even threats, before we got our petition sanctioned by Parliament for the opening of the museum.. We were called atheists, Sabhatb-breakers, and I dcn't know what besides.</p>
        <p>n Almost from the first we had some loyal friends even in official quarters but for a I ng time they were powerlelt. Lord Palmerston was one of these, and never shall I forget the outcry when, quite on his own responsibility, he started a military baud in the Green Park, which had immediately to be discontinued. ‘` One of our earliest friends was Dickens, who had written a pamphlet advocating a brighter Sunday even before the league was started. Dr. Furnival was another of our champions. When Mr. Balfour was Prime Minister I wrote to him asking him if he would receive a deputation. is replied 'There is no need. I am doing all I can for you already.' Fr orn the first the newspapers were kind to us—cotably Lloyd's News, under the editorship of Douglas Jerrold and my old friend Mr. Thomas Oatling. "The opposition came not from officials or leading men, but from local clergy. There was a time when I hardly ever could come home without findings clergy- man waiting for me, who generally began by taking for granted that I was a lost soul. "It is one of my plementest thoughts that I have never taken a penny for my work in connection sea the National Sunday League, though I was again and again asked to become a paid official."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>ALLEGED HOME OFFiCE SCANDAL. _ The Bubble Pricked.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED HOME OFFiCE SCANDAL. _ The Bubble Pricked.</p>
        <p>MR. WINSTON CHURCHILL JUSTIFIED.</p>
        <p>The Home “ffice los issued a statement giving prompt denial of the allegation made in the Weat- , minster Police Court on Wednesday that a criminal lunatic named James Garfield Craik had been dis- charged by Mr. Winston Churchill '' against the wishes of the medical superintendent." The case serves as a merited reprimand for section of the Preen which inconsiderately betrays ils bitter animosity against eel tam n Cabinet Ministers on every possible opportunity without Eecuring con- firmation of the accusations. Here, aide by side, are the statements made in court and the official denial :</p>
        <p>Police Court Statement. Offi Ad Account. The Magistrate : How did The patient's conduct he come to be released? has been exemplary, and Mr. Reginald Newton his mental stite is such (solicitor)) Under the order that I do not think his of the Hone Secretary, I continued detention is undertand, and against the essential. . . If you wishes of the medical could eee your way to superintendent, who thought allow him to live under it would hen criminal thing the supervision of his to release the man. who was brother is London, absolutely imbecile aud think te Conld hg ttnlittl dangerous. . . It is a not to attempt to tom - most disgraceful ca:o, municate with or annoy in any way Mr. or Mut Egan-Newcomb...Medical . .</p>
        <p>Superintendent s report to Home Office.</p>
        <p>Creik, a tall man of line physique, was !emended on Wednesday on a charge of assaulting Mrs. Florence Egan-Newcomb, of Rutland-gate, W., by throwing his arms round her and kissing her at her own door. It was a similar act four and a half years ago that led to his confinement in Banstead Asylum. Craik was released," the Home Office statement continues, " in accordance with the recommendation of the medical superintendent, after his brother had given an undertaking to keep watch over him and to report at once any symptoms of insanity or any attempt to molest or to see or communicate with Mrs. Egan-Newcomb. As he had fai!ed to observe the conditions under which he was released, the order cf discharge has been revoked aml the lunatic has Inta sent back to Bauetesd Asylum."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>LORD NIDLETON FALLS INTO A TRAP.</head>
        <p>LORD NIDLETON FALLS INTO A TRAP.</p>
        <p>Viscount Ifidleton, speaking at Lyme Regis on Thursday night, said Mr. Churchill's constant inter- ference with the sentence of magistrates hail been pointedly brought out by the case which appeared that day of the release of a lunatic by his order against the wishes of the medical superintendent who declared him to be' absolutely imbecile—dangerous." Public opinion would not long tolerate the use of the Home Office for sell-advertisement, and of the Exchequer for punitive raids on political opponents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>CHESHIRE PEER S ANCESTRAL</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE PEER S ANCESTRAL</p>
        <p>HOtAE.</p>
        <p>Youthful Lord Who Has Decided</p>
        <p>to Eel!.</p>
        <p>Lord V, moo, the youthful peer who CSIPP of age two years ago, has decided to sell hia ancestral home at Poynton, in Cheshire, together with the exclusive estates and shooting,. This is the second sale which Lord Vernon Las announced in recent months. Ifis principal home is at Sudbury, in Derby hire, where he bay 5,0(4) acres and last Novernl.lr he informed hie tenant. there of hie intention to sell his Derbyshire land, retaining the Hall. The tenants were given the option of purchaaiog their holding., on easy term,. The reaaon for breaking op the estate was given as" the present instability of landed property," and not a question of" personal finance." Poynton Hall has been in the possession of the Vernon family for generation', and included in the estate are extensive collieries and 30 farms. The Dan was erected in the 17th century, and replaced one built in the !firm of Edward VI.</p>
        <p>The %ernons are. very rich fainiLy, es wady all of them married beires,es. Tbe prelvnt peer's father married a rieh American, and Lord Vernon married. niece of Mr. Pierpoint Nlorgan. lllordnblp is perhaps best known to the public an a motorist who has bees many times lined.</p>
      </div>
