      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>THEFI'S OF BRASS.</head>
        <p>THEFI'S OF BRASS.</p>
        <p>RECEIVER AND THIEF BOTH PUNISHED. A case which has aroused considerable interest in the district was decided at the Borough Court, on Thursday before Mr. A. J. Jolley (presiding), and other magis- trates, when Richard Hassell, a lodgingthonse habitu4, wad charged with stealing lithe of steps, 171 be of scrap brass on the 20th inst, and 20 lbs of brass bearings on the 21st inst. the property of Messrs. Mania, Hall and Co., while Jonathan Broadhurst, greengrocer, Bostock-street, was charged with receiving the same well knowing them to have been stolen. A further charge against Broadhuaat for having received a hammer the property of Messrs. Croaneld's and Soo, well knowing it to have been stolen, was dismiss id. The cases were dealt with separately. In the case of Hassall, the Chief Constable said that the prisoner was arrested the previous week on a charge of loitering in the vicinity of Mowers. Monks hull and Co., with intent to commit a felony. Inquiries were then teing made with regard to the theft or some brass steps front the 4 inch mill at the above works, and from information received the officers engaged in the work came to the conclusion that the prisoner was concerned in the affair. He watt charged and admitted the theft. At 6.30 on the 21st inst. prisoner was seen by a watch- man acting in a suspicious manner in the vicinity of the inch mill. The prisoner disappeared and the watchman failed to find him. The following morning the workrren in the above mill were unable to work on account of the missing steps. Four previous convic- tions were proved against the prisoner, who was sentenced to three months' imprisonment. Mr. Steel appeared for the prisoner Broadhurst, and put in a plea of guilty. The Chief Constable said that the prisoner who had just been sentenced by the court, appeared to have taken the property and handed it to another individual, and that person to have taken them to the premises of the prisoner and sold the property there. The prisoner paid for it at the rate of twopence a lb. A description of the missing property was circulated by the police and Detective Metcalfe called upon the prisoner and showed him one, and asked him if he had received any brass lately, to which he replied that he did not deal in it. The police obtained a march warrant to search the prisoner's premises. On the 24th inst., the prisoner was seen in Liverpool road and was taken to the l'olice Station where he was questioned by Inepector Williamson. Ile replied that he knew absolutely rothing about them. The Inspector then told him that they were going to search his premises and asked him if he had anything to any before they went to which be replied " No, you eau go but you will and nothing there." The officers discovered a bird of workshop on the prisoner's premises. A new board had been nailed lathe rafters and upon this being removed the property was discovered. The property was taken to the l'olice Station and placed before the prisoner and he immediately replied lam dose now I'll give is; I bought the brass from Sammy Warburton and gave him 2d. a pound for it. I bought the hammer from a man I didn't know in town, for Is. I didn't see Croarleld'e name on it. I knew Warburton had no right with the stuff and afterwards when I knew it was wrong I planted it to save the monkey that took it." When searched the prisoner had a police circular with the description of the property in his possession. Mr. Steel said the prisoner found himself in that position with being an easy going man and with placing too much confidence in another man. 'the man Warburton had worked for the prisoner during the whole of last winter, and the pi isoner found him a straight and honest man and had no reason to suspect him whatever. When Warburton took the brass he told the prisoner that it belonged to a workman who was entitled to pick it from the scrap heap. It was well-known that it was the custom for these men to take the scrap pickings and there was no reasan for suspicion on the part of Broadhnrst. Broadhurst did not take particular notice at the time but he examined the property later and at once ear that it Could not have been boneslty got. He however got into a state of fright and didn't know what to do. lie talked it ever with his wife but she being in a nervous condition would not help him. Being in such a "funk "he wa afraid to remove it from the premises and he said that if he reported it to the police he would be charged with receiving. He bad been carrying on business in War, ington for forty years and was a married man with eleven children. The Chief Constable said that the point of the men being able to pick scrap was absolutely untrue. This was corroborated by,Mr. Mold a foreman at Messrs. Monks Hall and Company's who stated that during his 13 )ears of service they had never had that right. Mr. Edwin Woods (from the Bench) : If the men bad that privilege there would be no works left. Mr. Steel : I said that Mr. Broadhurst was under that impression. The Bench retired, sod upon their return the Chair- man said that after careful consideration of the circum- stances, the gravity of the offence was so grist that the Bench felt bound to pass a sentence of three months on each charge the sentences to run concurrently. The Chief Constable then asked that a number of other charges might be wlthdrawn,and this was granted A sequel to the above was heard at the Borough Court yesterday, when Simonet Warburton was charged with stealing a quantity of brass bearings from ti ewe. Monks. Hall and Co. The Chief Constable said that the prisoner was con- cerned with stealing the brass in which two men were sentenced the previous day. The prisoner left the town when he knew the police were in search of the brass, and he had not been heard of until yesterday when he was arrimted. He asked that the case might stand a tiourned until next Thursday, in order that further i.quiries might Le made.—This request was granted. Borough Police Court. SATURDAY.—B. tore Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair), Aldermen Tinnii n and Bennett, Mears. Thomas Pemberton, R. T. Fairclough and A. J. Jolley. THEFT or Brues.—Edwaid O'Toole, of no settled abode, was charged with stealing a pair of roles value us. fal. from the stable of Maginn Bras, on the 2lth insL—The Chief C salable said that at 7.30 p.m. on the day in qi eation t'e prisoner went to a Mr. Wilcox, who was a fish dealer,- std offered the reins for sale. He named is. as the price, and Mated in reply to Mr. Wilcox that he had come by them quite honestly. Mr. Wilcox was not satisfied with the statement and decided . to advance the prisoner Is. Information was given to the police and Detective Holland made inquiries. He went round to Mr. Houghton, the manager for Magian Bros., who identified the reins and stated that the prisoner had no rir.lit with them. Prisoner was subse- quently arrested and at first denied all knowledge of the theft, but upon being further questioned he admitted it. Prisoner was Bentenoed to 14 days' imprisonment. ALLEORD MEAL:LT.—AN Akini.—Peter Murray, 55, Oliver-street, were cherged on remand with assaulting Edward Stokes, 37, Warburton-street, at midnight on the 21st inst.—Mr. Steel represented the defendant.— Edward Stokes stated that he had a row with the prisoner shout three months ago and on the night in question the prisoner went to him and said that he was " going to get his own back." Before the com- plainant could say anything the prisoner knocked him to the ground, kicked him in the ribs and struck him several times. A number of witnesses were called who testified that the prisoner was in bed at the time of the alleged assault and the Bench dismissed the case. MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst, Bolton, James Evans,Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. S. Wright, W. Roberts, H. Thornton, G. Pilling and T. Locker. " 510 ST BRUTAL AND UNJUSTIFIABLE."—John Longshasi, 1, Garnett's-yard. was charged with assault- ing Ms wife, Agnes Longshaw, by striking her with a stick on the 19th inst.—The Chief Constable said that about ten minutes to twelve on the 19th inst. the prisoner reached home and in reply to his wife asking</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>him if he wanted his supper be ordered her to hed, lie afterwards followed her upstairs and brought her down when he struck her with the stick. The complainant had to be taken to the Infirmary and six stitches were required.—Mr. Steel said that although he admitted the assault he thought that it was acne in which the Bench might feel inclined in the interests of the parties to adjourn it for • while. They had been married for 2X years and he did not want to increase the breach between them alter such a period. Prisoner tutted that his wife was drunk on the night in question and when she was drunk she had an awful temper. When drunk she would face guns or even lions. When be went upstairs the complainant threw a bucketful of filthy refuse over him. Ile did not vtrike her with a stick, but he lifted a chair up and in the dark it fell on her. Complainant denied throwing the refuse or of giving prisoner any provocation at all. The Bench sentenced prisoner to a mouth's hard labour the Chair- man remarking that the assault was most brutal and unjustifiable and the magistrates had been very lenient in their sentence.</p>
        <p>Diensosv.—Before Mr A J Jolley (in the chair), Councillor Wilkinson, blessra W Roberta, W T Williams, Edwin Woods and Dr Nadeo. Dour ESTIC SERVANT IN TROtßLE.—Ethel Green, a domestic servant, aged 18, a native of Grappenhall, was charged a ith falsely obtaining food and lodgings value Os from Catherine Smethurst, 61, Cneeter New-road. She pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable said that about 10 o'clock on Sunday last the prisoner went to the house of the prosecutrix and asked for lodgings. She stated that she had a box of clothing in the cloak room at the station. She was taken in and the following morning said that her money was in the box at the station. She stayed the following day and then went out and returned and said that her box had been taken on U Manchester. The prosecutrix became auspicious and the police were informed and the statement was found to be false. They had no desire to I)3k the prisoner up as Mr. Firth, the police conrt missionary, had interested himself in the case, hut unfortunately they had to look her up pending tha finding of a home. Prisoner was bound over for twelve months. "SENT BY TUE LA NOLORD ."—Walter Booth, 113, Wilderspool Causeway, was charged with obtaining a white-wash brush and twelve mantels from Alice Marsh with intent to defraud.—The Chief Constable said that on June 24th, the prisoner went to the shop of Mrs. Marsh, 155, Slarsh-house-lane, and represented that be had been sent by the landlord, of the King and Queen Hotel (51r. Starkey), for a whitewash brush. He was given the brush and called later in the day and stated again that Mr. Starkey bad sent him for twelve mantels. There were given to him An account was sent to Mr. Starkey who did not know I anything about it. Information was given to the 'police and the prisoner was arrested. When charged Ihe stated that he intended to pay for the articles. He sold the mantels the some day for 3s. 6d.—lle was I fined 40s. including casts with the alternative of a montlfri imprisonment. WORKING HORSE IN UNFIT STATE.—Joseph Hesketh, a native of St. Helens, was summoned for ill.tresting a horse by working it lass unfit state on the 23rd iost Inspector Dee of the R.S.P.C.A. said that at 8.45 on the day in question he was in the vegetable market and there saw defendant leading the horse. Ile examined it and found it very lame and suffering from leminitis. He advised the defendant to take the animal home in a conveyance but in spite of that he walked the animal. The roles of its feet were dropped very much owing to the long standing of the infisnimation. Defendant stated that the horse was all right when be took it out of the stable, but it went lame when begot into Warring- ton. He was fined 40s including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" The Dandy Doctor."</p>
        <p>"The Dandy Doctor" is the title cf an excellent little musical comedy presented by Mr. Tom 11. Taylor's company at the Royal Glint Theatre this week. The author is Mr. Edward Mars, who has a thorough knowledge of human nature : he knows what's what, and in this piece we get the benefit of his keen insight, fine humour and wit to the full. The chang- ing of positions betseen mistress nod maid, the delusions of the tin-meat millionaire, the embarrass- ment of the wooing doctor, the "restoring to health" in the surgery and the accidents at the carnival—all contribute to the hilarity and success of the comedy. Miss Marie Ault, who takes the character of Jane (the general), is the embodiment of humour itself •, her very make-up almost convulses one. Her interludes with Mr. C. T. Chapman are admirably done, especially "The trip t, Blackpool," in which all the eccentricities and enthusiasms of a Lancashire couple are brought out to perfection. Mr. Chapman, as Brtie Bolingbroke (the Dandy Doctor), is a distinct success aided as he is by a fine physique, and an , apparently natural aptitude for indulging in parading on the pier. As the tinned meat millionaire Mr. Tom lE. Sinclair gives a capital represensatfon oi the Yankee " Pork-packer," infusing his part with becoming vivacity and humour. Mr. Herbert I Pearce is a typical Colonel Pendred—an old officer whose only object seems to be to capture a man of ample means who will marry his daughter, I Ivy Pendred. Miss May Livesey gains considerable favour in the popular role of Ivy, her singing being particularly sweet, while her dancing is extremely graceful. Mr. Cecil W. Parke has the difficult part of Arthur Vandyke, M.P., entrusted to him and he responds in excellent style. He is a frequent contributor of baritone solos and fully deser Tea the plaudits of the audience. A dozen or more chorus girls besides other prominent characters assist in presenting a most entertsiningperf,rrnance, in which i comedy and music are blended n the happiest manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>"WHEN KNIGHTS WERE BOLD."</head>
        <p>"WHEN KNIGHTS WERE BOLD."</p>
        <p>Ur. James Welch's principal company in" When 'knights were bold will be the great attraction at the Royal Court Theatre next week. This play has beaten all theatrical records in having had the longest run of the century at West End Theatres, first at Wyndham's and afterwards at the Criterion. Queen Alexandra saw this farce three times, and in a recent magazise interview Lords Roberts declared " When knights were bold" to be his favourite play. "Bob" has seen it eight times. Although in its fifth year of unbruken tour this will be the first visit of this delightful farcical comedy to Warrington. The author, Charles Marlow, has succeeded in writing an entirely original story. It is in three acts, Act I. being Sir Guy's sleep. Having been ridiculed before his guests for not being as brave and bold as his ancestors, he has an extra glass of hot whisky and falls asleep before the hall fire and dreams. In Act 11. we see this dream realised, rand Sir Guy living in the year 1196 instead of 1906. The element of the incongruous is the chief characteristic of this Act. We are assisting at the wildest exhibition of farce; the duel scene alone would make the fortune of a mus:c hall turn. It is so absolutely in the picture and yet so grotesquely ludicrous that one can only give oneself up to the spirit of the moment and laugh unrestrainedly. In the third act we see Sir Guy awake again. Mr. Arthur Stanley, a clever and rising actor from the Haymarket and Duke of York's Theatre, London, has been specially eogaged to play "Sir Guy de Vere." He will be ably supported by Mies Madeline Du Val as " Lady Rowena.' The scenery, costumes and effects have been expressly built for this tour and the company has been rehearsed at the Criterion Theatre, London, under the personal direction of Mr. James Welch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WHAT'S TO BE DONE?</head>
        <p>WHAT'S TO BE DONE?</p>
        <p>By Mr. H. Whitham..</p>
        <p>I have been very much interested d rring the pest few weeks in the contents ef the British papers I have received. Two questions have been to the fore, and no doubt the Britisher has been interested in the solution of them. I refer to the Veto and strike questions. One thing is very clear to the reader. The man from Oxford can demonstrate the fact that he is as big and as mean a rough as the most ignorant , d cker, and cmat be as guilty of just as raw Hughliganism as the imported Malay seaman. Judging from the epeechee made by Mr. Asquith's lopponents, and the levers to the press propheaying the utter and • immediate co'lapse of the British Empire. I can only think one or two things; they are either hypoerites, and do not believe what tl.ey say, or they are not sane and are therefore unfitted for leg'slative work. My mail to-day brought me my bi-weekly batch of London papers, " Public Opinion ' and the' London Times." In the latter, July 27th I find a lelter ' signed Frederick Milner, in which the writer, pleading for unity " among the Unionists says : " For ad's sake do not let us play into our opponents hands by causing a split in the only party that can save the state." It would be interesting to know just what F. M. means by " the State." DO9B he moon, and lois party, does it mean, that their sole and only object is to have and to hold the land of Britain and of Ireland for the Astors of New York, the Hoe ards and the Stanley% and their kind. If this is what the Unionist policy ia, and we know it is, why, to use the words of Carnegie" the jig is up." The Tory party were in power quite long enough to prove their incapability, as the f 'Bowing will show. I quote from "Public Opinion" July 2Sth : —"ln many parts of Ireland the farmer has ceased to till his land, not out of choice, but of necessity. The hands that would give the needful help in the sowing and saving of his crop are not available." Have the Smiths sad the Cecil, done anything that would encourage the Irish labourer to ,work oa the sod he loves ? "Public Opinion " of April 28th gives us the storyl of poor bleeding Scotland. I remember long years ago writing a series of articles touching the Cotters Bill. At thet time the Cotters question was a burning one. The Highlands were being cleared. The only saviours of the State were getting their horrid work in at that time. May I take up a little of the" Examiner's " space by giving a story of that time. It will illustrate the " bleeding method "as pursued to-day. In the history of David. King of Israel, may be found a story that was told by Nathan to the King, about " one ewe lamb." Up in the Highlands of Scotland a rich man—not one of ancient chiefs of the glens, not even a Scotchman, but a foreigner, acquired for his own selfish purposes alone three hundred and fifty square n.iles of the soil of the island. This man required for the satisfaction of his land lust a tract of country as large as all Middlesex and the metropolitan portion of Surrey, and he stated openly that it was his intention to clear off the whole of the population he found there. On one part of the land which was not owned but held on lease by Mr. Winans there were thirteen cotters, who, according to the evidence of Mr. Mackenzie, the owner of the land, had always been accustomed to graze a sheep or a cow. One of these cotters hal a pet lamb which ran in and out like a dog. The American millionaire proceeded to the sheriff's court at Dingwall for an interdict against the cottar'a pet lamb. Winans' statement to the court was "I cannot get the full benefit of the forest while the cotters are allowed to remain there. What I desire is to get rid of the cottagers and their inhabitan,s. I shan't leave a stone unturned until I get rid of them." About the time the above episode was passing into the history of the "bleeding of Sco•land," Mr. Thoro'd Rogers made the statement that of the casual poor in Southwark the majority were agricultural labmrers who could not find work upon the land. The proper use of the land is to grow food, and not to amuse the rich. I think a quotation from Mr. J. Chamberlain's speech before the Eighty Club at the time he was President of the Board of Trade, will fitly close this letter. "I hold that every man who comes into the world, has a natural right to life, and a fair enjoyment of life, and that is a right you ought to secure by law— (cbf era) - and if you don't hold that view, if you think it is Utopian, then you ought to carry your principles out, and you ought to adopt the Chinese view and poison the children when they come into the world without giving them any chance of life." IT. WHIM AIL</p>
        <p>San Francisco. August 14th, 1911</p>
      </div>
