      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>POLITICAL LIBELS. Tory Plaintiffs Fare Better than Liberals.</head>
        <p>POLITICAL LIBELS. Tory Plaintiffs Fare Better than Liberals.</p>
        <p>---- --- An instructive tabulated statement of political libel actions tried since the last General Election has been compiled be the London " Star." This statement is published with the object of bringing out in its proper perspective " the striking differences in the results of that litigation when Liberals were the plaintiffs as com- pared with the actions in which Tories were plaintiffs." As our contemporary point. out, when the actions were against the Liberals, the plaintiffs were successful in In out of 17, but in only six cases out of 13 did the Liberal plaintiff obtain a verdict. An analysis of the damages awarded provides an evcn more striking comparison. "In 17 actions spinet Liberals the plaintiffs secured from juries a total sum of £16,130, while in 13 actions against Conservatives the plaintiffs secured only £550 On. Old. In the first group the damages of success- ful Conservative plaintiffs average £1,958. The six successful Liberal plaintiffs only ireceived an average of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>ASTOR Hair-dressing and Shaving Saloon</head>
        <p>THE ASTOR Hair-dressing and Shaving Saloon</p>
        <p>Photo long,</p>
        <p>......, , 1 • 1 1 P"F41411111 1 I 5,7 , . ~..,., ~. .4., , , ! ,0 . . . , ..., .„ . _ _ ~„„,........... , , : ~..,,, ~_ , , i, / --, I _ ... , , .. , . .. "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Horsemarket St., Warrington. Now op.zisT.</head>
        <p>Horsemarket St., Warrington. Now op.zisT.</p>
        <p>A HAIR-DRESSING SALOON DE LUXE.</p>
        <p>The Astor Hair-dressing and Shaving Saloon which has just been opened over one of the new shoos in Horeemarket-street, ought to create acauewbat of a furore in Warrington, for it introduces to the local public all the latest meth do and appliances of the barber's art as practised in up-to-date America. There are lusuliously upholstered automatic tip-up chairs, soap-powdera " on tap," compressed air for spraying, and an ingenious electrical hand device which gives the operator and the customer the choice of either a hot or cold blast for the scalp after shampooing or other treatment. Eveiytbing is done on the antiseptic principle, all inetrumenta being sterilised before use, no 'het the danger of infection is nil. The employees are specialists in the scientific treatment of the hair and after haircutting, a shampoo, with medicated washes,</p>
        <p>Cr a dry shampoo, accomplished, on the most up-lo- date lines gives a most exh,larating sensation The experier ce of shaving is ((malty pleasant. Each custom( r is furni,hed with a clean towel and after being shaved instead of the usual puff powder being dabbed on the Lce an antiseptic spray dtiven by com- pressed air is utilised. A rub with a hot moist towel greatly refreshes the skin and completes the prccess. Another speciality is head and face massage, while chiropody and manicure are also carried on. The Saloon itself is splendidly lit and upholstered in the most luxurious fashion. Plush-covered chairs and a handsome divan spell comfort so plainly that the familiar " next please " is an almost unwelcome sound. In short, t e Astor Saloon is the acme of comfort, tefinement, and scientific application, and should prove a great boon to the unshorn and unshaven public of Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>METHODIST FLITTINGS.</head>
        <p>METHODIST FLITTINGS.</p>
        <p>OVER EIGHT HUNDRED MINISTERS CHANGING CIRCUITS.</p>
        <p>Tuesday and Wednesday were the annual removal days or the Wesleyan Methodist minim, y. From the time of John Wesley the ministeta of the Wesleyan Methodist Church have been under the itinerant system which necessitates, as e rule, a change of station every three years. As an official at the new Wesleyan Church House explained ton representative of the'• Wesminster Gazette' this week, over 800 men will be moving into fresh circuits. Ct..] ar ordinary circumstances they remain there three years, but conditions may arise that make it advisable for the minister only to stay a year. On the other hand the minister may receive a pressing Invitation to stay a fourth year in order that he may complete some special enterprise in which the circuit is engaged. 'lbis has to be sanctioned by the Stationing Committee and by Confareuce. For a man to remain the fourth year ira an ordinary circuit is considered a special tribute to Ilia value as n past lie system, it was further explained, by which these pastoral arrangements proceed, are on lines settled by the Wesleyan deed poll, and to alter them would necessitate an Act of Parliament. Much discussion has followed in the Wesleyan Conference on the subject, and many proposals have been made from time to time in order to modify the itinerant pastorate, but for the present the Connexion clings to its old says, though,as I explained later, some elasticity has taken place with regard to ths great central missions. Every time the Stationing Committee meets to plan out tha removals, a number of ministers, who m ry be consiiered the best organisers and preachers, usually receive in advance invitations from the larger and more important circuits, but these can only ha accepted provisionally by the ministers no invited. In every case they must be endorsed by the Stationing Committee and also by Conference. These invitations are allowed to stand in the majority of instances, but cases arise in which it is found that older men of long experience are over- looked by those who desire younger niiiiiinkra of • preaching ability, and then readjustments have to be made. Disappointments ensue, and protests are sent to Conference on the subject, but on the whole the object of the Stationing Committee, whose task is onerous and frequently invidions, is to provide that the stationing of ministers works out fairly an] squarely all round.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>CIRCUIT ARRANGEMENTS.</head>
        <p>CIRCUIT ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>--- Another impoitant duty cast upon the Stationing Committee is the flying up of the necaosa-y arrange- ments for circuits that h Iva not exercised the right of molt ing a minister. There are many such who leave to the committee such sslection ant agree to accept the minister sent tnem. In this way it is possible to provide for the whole of the available ministers. A special paper called " Stations of the Ministers," consisting of twelve large foolscap pages and published at tvopence, was given to the " Westminster Gazette " representative embodying the whole of the arrange- ments sanctioned by the Wesleyan Conference at Cardiff and taking effect this week. Ministers are being moved to circuits which will involve much travelling. For instance Scottish ministerc are cell, into Yorkshire and those in Yorkshire to Wales, and so on. At times a man in Cornwall may be stationed in the fur North or rice rersa. Those in charge of the great central missions of Methodism do not come under the itinerant system. Their tenure is from year to year, but under normal 'circumstances it is not disturbed. The Rev S F Collier has been in charge of the Manchester Central Mission for 20 years; the Rev J Ernest Rattentury, MA, remains superintendent of the West London Mission, for which, at length, he has secured fine new premises in Great New-striet ; the Rev .1 Gregory Mantle is still at the Deptford and Greenwich mlssion, where he has proved exceptionally successful ; and the Rev J Scott Lidgett, M. A, D. D, is still Warden of Ber- mondsey Settlement. The most notable change in this respect is the appointment of the Rev J li Wakerley, from one or the London missions to superintend the work at West- minster, when the Centenary Hall is opened. Again, also, there is no change in many of the departmental offices. Dr. Henry Pope is still general secretary of the Home Minions ; Rev. Simpson Johnson, secretary of the Conference ; Rev .) Williams Butcher, secretory of the Suodsy School Department; Rev John Telford, 8.A., editor, and Dr Arthur E Gregory, sec nary of the National Children's Home ; whilst the Rev. Fred P 7 Mazdoetsle, uncle of Rudyard Kipling, is hon. secretary of the Wesleyan Missionary Society. NURNISUED HOUSES. Every mon who moves this week has, according to Wesleyan Methodist custom, a furnished house waiting for his accommodation. For a single man lodgings are usually provided. It is not, therefore, necessary for the minister to take any household furniture or equip- ment. All this is ready for him in his new sphere. He will take his books and household geode, but nothing more. Beds, chairs, pots and pans, knives and forks ac., are all is order after inspection by the society steward, who welccmes the incoming minister and his wife. In course of tine Wesleyan Methodism has developed • really remarkable system, which has not Banos' out • side Methodism, and probably is not so perfect in any other Methodist Church. With a touch of imagination one can read into the departure from familiar scenes much that will rouse mixed feelings. Many circuits Rill regret the loss of ministers. Sume even may rejoice. The United Methodist Church and the Primitive Methodists also adopt the itinerant system, but with more elastic arrangements. In the United Methodist Connexion the rule o trains that all ministers changing their circuits shall be due is their new siphons on the fiuorsday before the last Sunday in August, no that I their ecclesiastical year c.im-nenzes a week eorlier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
