      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Colborne-street Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Colborne-street Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>The lieu% Bible Class in connection with Oolborne- street Church, held their anniversary services on Sundry, when large congregations assembled at all the servicss. The preacher both morning and evening was the Bev. H. Ogden, et Oldham, who has recently</p>
        <p>accepted the pastorate of this church. A speclal musical service, presided over by tie president of the class, Mr. Wynne Jones, was held in the arternoon,and an excellent programme of music was gone through. Solos were rendered by Misses Hilda Hipkiss and Ads Hsmer and Mr E Walker, while a pianoforte solo by Mr J Barrett and a violin solo by Mr W tievaine were nicely executed. The choir gave a tasteful rendering of the anthem "0 Taste and see." The Rev. H. Ogden gave an interesting address. Mr. Ben Few proposed a vote of thanks to all who bad helped to make the service such a pronounced success, and this was heartily carried. Madame Fairhurst wax the principle roloist at the ' evening service. Mr. S. flickers presided at the orgaa and Mr. Atkinson was the conductor of the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church</p>
        <p>Preaohing at Si. John's on S inday evening last on the subject of" Gideon and the angel," Jangee vii., 22 and 23, the Ray. William Reid sail thsy had in the pessage read the story of a man living in the greatest obscurity, into whose life a greai, light brake, and he received a call tram G iii which wan sudden and unexpected. Teat often happened in the lives of men. Light sometimes came to them from a quarter directly opposite to that from which they had expected it would come, and the great teat Vrll9 whether they would accept the light from the direction from which it, Came, Or whether they would rej,ci. it, and pursue the Course previously marked out by themselves. Sometimes a men was brought face to face with the angel of God, and the call was one of trouble, or it might be a call which changed ths whale course of one's life, not the great question was whether the call Iran responded to or whether it was rejected, the lie afterwards becoming parched and stunted. At the evening rerSi 43 Miss Christiana, of the Royal Academy of Music, Llnden, gave a beautiful rendering of the solo Shepherd of souls," the singing of this gifted and highly trained vocalist bring much appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>British and Foreign Bible Society Garden</head>
        <p>British and Foreign Bible Society Garden</p>
        <p>Party. By the kind invitation of Mrs. Garnett, of Betaketh, the collectors sod a few friends of tie .British and</p>
        <p>Foreign Bible Society (a'arrington Auxiliary) were present at a garden party at Hall Nook on Saturday. The weather, thong' a little unsettled at timer, did not at all interfere with the enjoyment of the visitors. After an inspection of the beautiful grounds, together with the extensive vineries, all of which presented a charming oppearnace, the visitors engaged in various games, viz., croquet, bowling, &amp;a., all of which were much enjoyed. Tea was served abut 4.30, after which the Rev. J. 0. Houghton, B.A. (of Liverpool), the district secretary, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs. Garnett, 'referred to her long association with the Society sat trusted that their experience that afternoon would be an incentive to others to take up the work an heartily. The ltev. William Reid, the Rev. John Guznell, and Mr. J. C. Holland also voiced their appreciation of Mrs. Garnett's hospitality. The party returned home about 7.30, having spent a most enjo3able time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>SORE FEET AFTER HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>SORE FEET AFTER HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>Torturing Pain from Running Sores</p>
        <p>ANuTilEa GLAND LAII-DUK ,CLE,</p>
        <p>Attu- his holidays, little Wilfred Wiight arrived home with his feet very more. "They were in a shocking state," said his mother, Mrs. F. Wright, of 14, Crab Lane, Aroaley, Leeds, to a reporter, " and if it hadn't been hr Zam-Buk I really don't know what we should have done.</p>
        <p>•` Wilfred's feet were all inflamed and nwollen, al blieers formed b•tireen ail the toes. These blisters burst into sores, which discharged thick matter. The poor child was so tormented with the itcLiog and pain that he eoulain't wear his hoots. I had to dress Wilfred's feet when he was asleep, for be couldn't bear them touching ...len awake, and often it took me two hours at a time to dress and bandage them up. . . .</p>
        <p>You can imsgiae my disappointaient, therefore, after all my trouble, to sea the sores getting bigger, and discharning more matter. I had often beard Zam-Buk talked abcut, and decided at last to test it for myself. I mean just what I Fay when I tell ytu that Zan-&amp;c cried like magi. We were surprised at its soothing effect, while it ' drew ' the sores so nicely that I was able to dress Wilfred's feet while he was awake. The swelling and inflammation went down. and all the matter was drawn from the sores. Zatn•lluk then grew neir skis over the old sore places, and Wilfred's feet were tQuad end healthy onc6 more." -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BOY SCOUT DROW- NRD IN FRANCE.</head>
        <p>BOY SCOUT DROW- NRD IN FRANCE.</p>
        <p>1 A touching ceremoay was performed at Hard*lot, near Boulogne on Thursday, when the body of Robert Utley, the toy scout drowned on Monday, was removed for conves (Ace to Reading with full honoors. The cotta was cove-ed with a Clion Jack. the deceased's hat, and will floral tributes. It was ft•st. placed on the transport cart by the Breotford troop, end the cart was drawn by Soar Reading szouta. The procession following the body WM beaded by Sit Francis Vane and Mr. John Whitley, their host, an the !chief astur.Ars Then came the entire force of boy somas headed by muffled drums and bugles. Nest canoe the village school boss beaded by Cure Pere Condette. The Duke of Argyll, who was present a: the ceremony, re-eived a message from the Pf iDC937I Louise expressing regret at the sad set de t.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>MUSEUM AND LIBRARY.</head>
        <p>THE MUSEUM AND LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>Annual Report.</p>
        <p>Mr Charles Madeley, director and librarian, has issued his annual report to the Museum Committee. From it. we make the following extracts :- 1 MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>The number of specimens of all kinds added to the collections during the year was 1,518 (as against 2,436 during the p-evions twelve months). Of these 1,329 were seceived se gifts, and the remainder, 219 were purchased. In addition 4,117 specimens of wild tl zwers were receyed for temporary exhibition. One of the most notable additions to the natural history side of the Museum was made by Mr. LiDEM,PI Oreening' who presented a cabinet, containing 390 coloured lentern slides, illustratiog the structure, life history, and affinities of spiders,hatrachiane, and snakes. It is intended that these slides shall be lent to local applicants competent to make good us) of them. A collection of insects (30 species arranged and mounted to illustrate life history, protective resemblance and mimicry was presented by Dr. Maybe). Captain Waring made further contributions to the fauna of the Ship Canal, presenting several species of fresh-water fish, and nost notably,a number of star-fish, Asterias tubers, which were found climbiog on the walls on the upper side of Latchford Locks, in September, 1910. To the series of English weapons were added a Snap- branch. the earliest form of the that-lock, ends Prod- he form of cross-bow used for rook shooting, the latter n ade by theism, of Warrington, In the 19th century. He most interesting eddition in the section of Domestic Arts was the gift by Mcii Stone. F.L.S., of a he d-spindle and distaff, which Le saw in use at Amain, near Naples, in January last. Few opportunities arose of adding to the collection of English pottery. The chief accessions were a dessert dish of Chelsea pi rzelain and a cup and saucer of Bristol porcelain, printed in Line under glaze. Important additions were made to the section of Japanese decorative art, including a number of iron sword.guards with a short-sword, a knife, and a few of the smeller sword-ornaments, which serve to suggest the artistic taste and ,kill which were lavished upon what was the chief possession of a Japanese gentleman under the old r4gime. For the moment this is better shown by the collection of sword-guards lent by Mr.C. P. Peak. A very interesting addition to the local antiquities was a prise medal of the Loyal Warrington Volunteers of 1798. The medal is of silver, inscribed on the reverse," For skill at arms Captain James Leigh's Third Company," and was previously unknown to on. To the Mayor (Dr. Joseph), the Museum is indebted for the gilt of a copy, in gold, of the pecial medal which he had struck to commemorate the Coronation of Ring George. The following paintings were purchased Oil paint- ing," Riser scene, evening." by Robert Colliuson (first master of the Wartington School of Art). Oil paintin "The rescue," by Frank Brangwyn, A.R.A. Design in water colour, "Going to school," by Mrs. E. E. Houghton. Visrrons.—A chsnge in the rules came into force Is the beginning of the year under report, by which the Exhibition Rooms were opened six days, instead of four days, weekly, the hours being at the same time al ered to that the moms are now closed alB p.m.</p>
        <p>instead of 9 p.m. The change hss doubtless been t convenience to many persons, although it has no resulted in a great increase in the attendalce. The total numLer of visitors counted during the year was 48,389 giving an average of 137 per day for the 308 days open. as against an average of 190 per day for the 208 dap+ on which the rooms were open in the previous year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>LIBRARY.</head>
        <p>LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>AccsyitoNs.—Tbe total number of accessions to the library of all kinds registered during the year was 2,123. Allowing for withdrawals the total stock on the shelves on 30th June, 1911, was :i3.910, including about 19,320 pamphletn and broadsides. Colonel .1. D. Buckton presented three manuscripts of very great local interest, being minute-Isaoks and papers relating to the following institutions :—Werringtou Pitt Club 1814-29 : Committee for the erection of new church (St Paurs), 1821-31 ; Warrington Recreative Evening Schools Association, 1888-92. Books and pamphlets for the local collection were also presented by Alderman Bennett. Mr. Walter Crompton, Mrs. Madely, Mr. P. W. Monks, Mr. Arthur Monnfield, Mr. Albert Nicholson, Mr. W. A. Proud, and the Accrington Public Library. CincubiTiox.—The number of boobs honed from the Lending Library was 89,247. including 70,397 from the Generdl Library and 12,830 from the Juvenile Department. It is gratifying to be able to report an increase, if only amounting to 3 per cent, after three Tears of " slight decrease." The issue is fluctuating, the classes of literature In which there was an increase last year now show a decrease, and vice versa. The average number of books "out" was 2,341. The number of ticket-holders at the cud of the year was 3,954. DELIV FRY STATION,—On March Isl, 1011, three additional delivery stations for the issue of hooks from the Lending Library were opened at the Sub-Put Offices in Bewsay-road, Orford-lane, and Latchford (Thelwall-lane).</p>
        <p>Total Average lure. Books Out. Reesey Poet Office Church-street Poet Office Greet-street Poet Office 2,858 54</p>
        <p>Latchford Post Office Orford-lane Post Office Wine ick-street Post Office</p>
        <p>12,G69</p>
        <p>Previous year (three italicise c nly) *Open during four mouth, only. BRANCH READING ROONL—A MID at Silver-street schools was opened, as beton, from 0.30 to 9.30 p.m. on 300 evenings as a public reading.room. The list of periodicals supplied included denim 15 weeklies. and 4 monthlies. The average number of reader, per evening was 22, last year 21. SCHOOL LIBRARY.—AI indicated in the last report the School Library was re-organized at the beginning of the year under review. Instead of keeping a general stock of books from which each school was at liberty to borrow a certain number, it was decided to place a permanent library in each school. The new arrange- ment was inaugurated with practically a new stock of books, amounting to 1,920 volumes, divided amongst 2i school department.. 'I he number cf issues to scholars from the Schools daring the nine months since the new amazement was made was 17,997,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
