      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LOST MASTERPIECE.</head>
        <p>THE LOST MASTERPIECE.</p>
        <p>LEONARDO DA VINCI'S FAMOUS PICTURE.</p>
        <p>Da Vinci's great picture " Monna Li 81" has been stolen from the Louvre gallery and Paris sits in the sackcloth of shame. To the plain man it nmy seem that amid the acreage of painted canvas in the Louvre a few square feet can hardly matter. But he, of course, has never seen the " Monna Lisa" and can- not be expected to understand. It may be taken for granted that every ono who ever felt the soh inn witchery of that face will feel a sense of deepest personal loss. We are among the mourners and must mourn awhile. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LEONARDO THE ELUSIVE.</head>
        <p>LEONARDO THE ELUSIVE.</p>
        <p>There bangs over Leonardo Da Vinci the same mystery that hangs over this woman's face in his picture. 1-14-re are inscrutable eyes, yet they look the beholder through and through. Such was Monna Lisa's creator; inscrutable to every biographer, yet himself with eyes of flame. A writer, mathematician, engineer, botanist, scientist, musician, architect, sculptor, mechanic, he took up painting as an incidental and less than secondary thing. He was "impatient of pain ang " and did but one known portrait, yet it is hailed as the world's masterpiece. Who was this Leonardo who so bewilders; us? His first biographer, Antonio Billi, who knew him, closed .his sketch with a note of despair. " ills spirit was iii:"Ter st rest, his mind was ever .devising new things." lie tried to track the workings of his mind and utterly failed. Nor has any future biographer brought us to an understanding. He was the most versatile genius of the age of the Renaissame,and like a star he dwelt apart. Leonardo was born at Vinci, a village not far from Flor.mc3 in 1452, and showed as a child tie aptltude for geometry and arith- metic which marked him through life. When about 18 year; old his father happily placed</p>
        <p>him in the studio of Andrea del Verrocchio from whom he learned modelling and paint- ing. So swiftly did his mind rise to the work that in two years he was an independent artist whose name appears upon the roll of the Guild of Painters.</p>
        <p>But Leonardo WKS devoted to engineering and as an engineer went to the East whin about 28 years old in the service oi the Sultan of Cairo.</p>
        <p>HIS PAINTINGS.</p>
        <p>The year 1498 (though the work may have occupied several previous years) witnessed the work with which his name will always be associated' the famous " Last Supper," painted on the wall of the convent of St. Maria delle Grazie at Milan, in hours rescued from his business as engineer. Two years later he was in Florence as the engineer and architect of Cmsar Borgia, and here painted the famous " Monna Lisa." For four years the board was upon the easel (the painting is upon a panel of wood) and at the end was declared unfinished. And who was this Monna Lisa? Our only knowledga is that she was the beautiful wife of his friend del Francesco Giocondo. But the picture never belonged to Giocondo. King Francis I. of France, saw it and bought it from the artist for the incredible sum of 4,000 gold florins, in English equivalent £20,000. The work brought Leonardo a fortune; it also took him to France. Francis I gave him an allowance and a studio and here he died fifteen years later on May 2nd, 1519. Of his skill as an artist a mere novice can say little, but there is in his work a mastery of light and shad', a sublety of exoression, a reality which declares itself even to the uninitiated, and challenges all the world.</p>
        <p>Who that has stood before " Monna Lisa" can ever forget the sensation of over- powering reality?</p>
        <p>-, 11, / ert° itirw.</p>
        <p>LA JOCONDE : MONNA LISA LEONARDO DA VINCI (Louvre).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>HISTORY OF THE PICTURE.</head>
        <p>THE HISTORY OF THE PICTURE.</p>
        <p>Until the present hour this wooden panel —the most precious bit of timber in the world—has had an uneventful history. In 1545 Francis I. hung it in his cabinet of pictures. This collection centred round Leonardo's picture, which, in the mind of the King, was to form a Royal collection. Out of it grew the gallery all the world has visited—is the peculiar glory of Paris and indeed of France. "Monna Lisa" never left the Royal collection. It has hung at Versailles and at Fontainebleau and in 1793 was sent by the Republic to the Louvre. And now let 118 listen to the critics. We cmy first Walter Pater's description as given in his "Renaissance Studies." WALTER PATER ON " MONNA LISA." "La Giocondo is, in the truest sense, Leonardo's masterpiece, the revealing instance of his mode of thought and work. . . . We all know the face and hands of the figure, set in its marble chair, with that circle of fantastic rocks, as in some faint light under sea. Perhaps of all ancient pictures time has chilled it least. . . Besides the picture is a portrait. But for express historical testimony, we might fancy that this was but his ideal lady embodied and beheld at last. Was it in four Years and by renewed labour never really completed, or in four months and as by stroke of magic that the image was projected?</p>
        <p>—"-The presence that rose thus so strangely beside the waters, is expressive, of what in the ways of a thousand years men had come to desire. Her's is the head upon which' all the ends of the world are come,' and the eye-lids are a little weary. It is a beauty wrought out from within upon the flesh, the deposit, little cell by cell or strange thoughts or fantastic reveries and exquisite passions. Set it for a moment beside one of those Greek Goddesses or beautiful women of antiquity and how would they be troubled by this beauty, into</p>
        <p>which the soul with all its maladies has passed ! and all the thought and experience of the world have etched and moulded there, in that which they have power to refine and make expressive the out- ward form, the animalism of Greece, the lust of Rome, the mysticism of the middle age with its spiritual ambition and imaginative loves, the return of the Pagan world, the sins of the dorgias. She is older than the rocks among which she sits; like the vampire she has been dead many times, and learned the secrets of the grave; And has been a diver in deep seas, and kelps their fallen day about her; and trafficed for strange webs with Eastern merchants; and, as Leda, was the mother of Helen of Troy, and, as St. Anne, the mother of Mary ; and all this has been to her but as the sound of lyres and flutes, and lived only in the delicacy with which it has moulded the hanging linaments, and tinged the eye- lids and the hands. The fancy of a perpetual life, sweeping together ten thousand experi- ences is an old one; and modern philosophy has conceived the idea of humanity as wrought upon by, and summing up in itself, all modes of thought and life. Certainly Ltdy Lisa might stand as the emboiiment of the whole fancy, the symbol of the modern idea."</p>
        <p>We frankly confess we never 3aw half that in " Monna Lisa," but such gifts of seeing don't belong to every man. The most striking thing we ever found was her power to look into the beholder. Hours spent in gazing cannot read what is behind those eyes; yet they discern at a glance every secret of our being. If it is not impertinent, to add to such a critic as Pater, the background is full of pure romance. Water winds rounds rocks; hills tremble like mirage and hang in the air like smoke. It is simply uncanny.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>VASAReS ACCOUNT OF MONNA LISA.</head>
        <p>VASAReS ACCOUNT OF MONNA LISA.</p>
        <p>Now for a more distant critic who saw the picture in the days of its fresh and early splendour. We quote from Vasari : "For rancesco del Giocondo, Leonardo undertook to paint the portrait of Monna Lisa, his wife, but, after loitering over it for four years he finally left it unfinished. This work is now in the possession of Bing Francis of France, and is at Fontainebleau. Whoever shall desire to see how far art can imitate I Nature may do so to perfection in this head, I wherein every peculiarity that could be depicted by the utmost subtility of the pencil has been faithfully reproduced. The eyes have the lustrous brightness and moisture which is seen in life, and around them are those pale, red, Lod slightly livid circles also proper to nature with the lashes, which can only be copied, as these are, with he greatest difficulty ; the eye-brows are also represented with the closest exactitude, where fuller and where more thinly set, with the separate hairs delineated as they issue from the skin, every turn being followed, and all the pores exhibited in a manner that could not be more natueal than it is; the nose, with its beautiful and delicately roseate nostrils, might be easily believed to be alive; the I mouth, admirable in its outline, has lips I uniting the rose tints of their colour with 1 that of the face, in the utmost perfection, and the carnation of the cheek does not appear to be painted, but truly of flesh and blood; he who looks earnestly at the pit of the throat cannot but believe that he sees the beating of the pulses, and it may be truly said that this work is painted in a manner well calculated to make the boldest master tremble, and astonishes all who behold it, however well accustomed to the marvels of art. " Monna Lisa was exceedingly beautiful, and while Leonardo was painting her por- trait, he took the precaution to keep someone constantly near her, to sing or play on instruments, or to jest and otherwise amuse her, to the end that she might continue cheerful, and so that her face might not exhibit the melancholy expression often imparted by painters to the likenes3 they paint. In this portrait of Leonardo's, on the contrary, there is so pleasing an expression, and a smile so sweet, that while looking at it one thinks it rather divine than human, and it has ever been esteemed a wonderful work, since life itself could exhibit no other appearance."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>JAMES RUSSELL LOWELL'S VERSES.</head>
        <p>JAMES RUSSELL LOWELL'S VERSES.</p>
        <p>That is simpler and we can understand it. Now for the poet Lowell, though his verses on the picture have been quoted almost threadbare.</p>
        <p>Sho.gave me all that women cm,</p>
        <p>Nor her sourn nunnery forgo, A confiden-e that man t., man</p>
        <p>Without remorse can never eliew. R we art, that c in the sense refine, Till not a pulse rebellious wire, And, since she never can be mine, Makes it seem sweeter to be hers.</p>
        <p>Our last word will be the most poignant. England very narrowly missed the possession of Leonardo's masterpiece. When the Duke of Buckingham went to France to bring back Henrietta Maria as bride of our Charles 1., the Duke expressed a strong admiration for Monna Lisa "and would have liked to carry it away. The French King was on the point of giving it as a present with the bride, but was prevented by the remonstrance of those about him, who argued that he would be sending away the most precious picture France possessed. Cassiand dell Pozzo wrote this in 1625, and since he was on the spot was a likely man_ to know.</p>
        <p>And now, surely. France will regret that " Monne Lisa" did not come. An English policeman at the door and she would have been safe for ever.</p>
        <p>Poor France! The gem of all the ages lifted from the wall. What stewardship of art ! A. M.</p>
        <p>SORE HANDS FOR FOURTEEN YEARS " For fourteen years I suffered with my bands. The skin came off in pa.ches, leaving my bands raw. They itched terribly and if I rubbed them, water oozed out, leaving them worse than before. My thumbs and first fingers were the worst, he skin came off, leaving a red, smooth surface. They itched and burned terribly. Other psrts of my hands became affected, the skin first drying up and peeling off. They were drawn together and 1 could not grasp anything. "I tried various ointments, but they did me no good. I went to five different doctors, having medicine and ointment (tom each, but all to no purpose. One of the doctors told me they would never be cured, another said the oil was all dried up out of the skin. Mx one gave me any hope of ever being cured. A friend thought one of my fingers was withering away. I tried various ointments recommended by friends, but they all failed to cure. After some time, a friend told me about the Cuticura Remedies, having tried them with SUCCeS3 for bands similar to mine. I tried them, and I found that nothing bad such a soothing and cooling effect. They acted wonderfully, and I noticed the soothing effect from the first. My hands were completely cured by the Cuticura Soap and Ointment. It has stood the test of a dozen years. It was wonderful, I never saw anything like it. Other members of the household have benefitted by it, and we are never without a box." (Signed) Mrs. Alice Bromley, 129, Durham St., Rochdale, Jan. 3, 1911. Post free, from F. Newbery &amp; Bons, 27, Charterbouse London, samples of Cuticura Soap sad Ointment, with 32-p. boot on skin treatment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>CLEOPATRA'S NEEDLE.</head>
        <p>CLEOPATRA'S NEEDLE.</p>
        <p>Cleopatra's Needle on the Thames Embankment is, for the first time since its erection, In the hands of workmen, who are cleaning it preparatory to the work of preservation which is necessary owing to the action of the London air on the stone. It is interesting to note that in the base of the monument are placed a varied collection of articles which may enable coming generations to form an idea of London to-day. Amongst the articles are coins, portraits, Whitaker's Almanac, London Directory, Bradshaw. corm-. ct oars our, pipes hairpins, toys, and an account of the removal of the Obelisk.</p>
        <p>of arbitration. Mr. Will Crooks has introduced a Bill into the House of Commons which makes proposals on the lines of the Canadian model. This Canadian system would probably be more suitable for adoption in this country than either the New Zealand or Australian system. It does not go so far towards compulsion, and leaves the work- men the liberty to strike if they are not satisfied with the result of the arbitration' award. This Canadian Act proceeds on the policy of "arbitrate before you fight." It makes it a very serious offence for an employer to lock out his men, or for the men to strike, before the Board of Arbitration has considered the dispute. If there is a I dispute either of the parties may demand that the Board of Arbitration shall consider the case. The findings of the Board are published, and this is a very important point in the system. If either party refuses to abide by the decision of the Board and enters upon a strike, it does so at a very serious disadvantage and with public opinion against it. The Board is so constituted as to carry public confidence, con- sisting of one representative of the employers, one of the workmen, and a third person who is nominated by the Minister of Labour. In the practical working of the system the expected result has followed, and in almost all cases the parties have accepted the finding of the Board. Our Government are evidently contemplating something in the way of compulsory arbitration. On the last day of the sitting of Parliament the Prime Minister announced that information had been sought from the Australian Government about the working of the Arbitration laws. The Trades Union Congress meets next week at Newcastle. and 1 it will be interesting to see if recent events in the labour world have increased or lessened the support for compulsory arbitra- tion among trade unionists. About three years ago the vote was proctically equal, but the following year the support fell off con- siderably. The miners are the great opponents of State arbitration. They think that their Conciliation scheme gives them more than they would gain under a compulsory scheme. The cotton operatives, who have evolved voluntary conciliation to a high state of perfection, are likewise opposed to State compulsion. They believe that on a series of years the operatives get a higher average wage under the present arrangement than they would do if they were regu- lated by some State Arbitration Board. But that is not the point of the argument for compulsory arbitration. There would be no case at all for State inter- ference if the employers and the workpeople could always settle their differences by arrangement. It is because they do not always do that, but sometimes resort to a strike or lockout, and thereby cause loss and inconvenience tuthe whole community, that the necessity for some power of interference by the State has arisen.</p>
