      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RIGHT TO STRIKE.</head>
        <p>THE RIGHT TO STRIKE.</p>
        <p>The right to strike as last resort is one which the British workman is not likely to quietly relinquish. It is not proposed to deprive him of that right. But the State, as representing the public interests has a right to make both parties present their case to an impartial tribunal, so that the public may know the merits of the dispute. The recent railway strike could not have taken place if we had had such a law as they have in Canada. The Commission of Enquiry which the Government has set up is just such a tribunal as would have been called upon to deal with such a dispute in Canada before a strike could have taken place. The railway strike has turned public attention to another means by which such troubles may be avoided in the future, namely, by the nationalisation of the rail- ways. Support for this proposal is coming from unexpected quarters. It does not follow that the transfer of the railways to the State would make railway strikes 'impossible. Some years ago a very serious strike took place on the Australian State railways, and the State on that occasion acted with as much rigour as any railway directors could have done. But it is a right instinct which assumes that there would be less trouble with the employees on our rail- ways if they were under State control, for the Government would not, like the railway directors have done, refuse to meet the representatives of the men's union. They do this now with their Post Office servants, and successive Postmasters have testified to the way in which this recognition promotes the harmonious workings of the Post Office. The recent railway strike has given an impetus to the demand for railway nationalisation. This will be seen more plainly when the smoke at present around the field of conflict has cleared away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, NP. CAN STRIKES AND LOCKOUTS BE PREVENTED.</p>
        <p>Now that the epidemic of strikes seems for the present to have subsided, attention will no doubt be directed to the considera- tion of methods for preventing a recurrence of such serious troubles, or at least for deal- ing with them more effectively than we are able to do by any machinery we have at present. Nothing certainly has stood out more prominently in the recent disputes than the utter helplessness of the State to assert itself in practical and effective way. It tried to handle the railway strike with what it considered to be firmness, with such terrible ill-success that nothing but the early settlement of the trouble saved the country from civil war. The State, as representing the public interest, certainly has much at stake in every labour dispute, but the most disastrous way in which it can assert its rights and try to protect the public interests is calling upon the armed forces of the nation to intervene. Whatever lesson the recent strikes have left with us, surely the most painful and serious one is that the State will have to assert its authority land protect the public. interests by civil and not by martial law.</p>
        <p>Every one of the strikes has been settled by resorting to conciliation. There is no other way by which such a dispute can be settled. Strike first and then arbitrate has been the policy. Surely the more sensible way would be to arbitrate first and then strike if agreement is impossible. But if the two parties would first come together with a real desire to settle the matter in dispute, in nine cases out of ten there would never be a I strike. The recent experiences will have greatly strengthened the public demand for 1 compulsory State Arbitration. It is rather a singular thing that the London dockers, who have just won such remarkable I successes by the strike are the men who for many years back have been strongly urging I the Trades Union Congress to call for State, Compulsory Arbitration on the Australian model. But there is nothing inconsistent in those who believe in Compulsory Arbitration resorting to the strike in the absence of Compulsory Arbitration. No trade union officials would prefer to get what they want by a strike rather than by a mutual agreement. VOLUNTARY CONCILIATION.</p>
        <p>There is no power vested in the Board of Trade at present to interfere in a Labour dispute. Its intervention must be sought by the one party or the other. It can only advise, it cannot enforce. Its advice naturally carries weight, but as recent experience has shown the differences between the two parties to the disputes are often so great that friendly advice cannot prevail. There are hundreds of voluntary Conciliation Com- mittees in existence in the country, and the outside public has no idea of the amount of work these Boards do and the number of disputes they prevent developing into strikes or lockouts. In 1909 the permanent boards settled over a thousand cases, and the Board of Trade under the Conciliation Acts settled 23 disputes. The Arbitration laws of this country will have to be amended. They are neither one thing nor another at present. We have a good deal of material for considering the subject in the experience of our colonies— 'Australia, New Zealand, and Canada. Opinions differ very widely as to the success of the New Zealand and Australian Acts. But there is one fact which is more conclusive than all the opinions of critics, and that is that no attempt has been made in any of the colonies to repeal any of the Arbitration laws, lthough many efforts at amendment have been made. The New Zealand scheme works wholly through the trade unions. It does not recognise unorganised workmen. In the early years of the working of the Actl there was a good deal of violation of it, and strikes were by no means uncommon occurrences in spite of their illegality. But in 1908 an amending Act was passed, and since then the law has been more generally observed. Strikes and lock-outs in breach of an award are unlawful, and the wages of workmen may be attached in payment of penalties. Other strikes, however, are legal, although workmen in certain occupations, such as public lighting services, must..give fourteen days' notice. THE CANADIAN SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Attention has recently been directed rather prominently to the Canadian system</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>OPEN-MK POLITIS.</head>
        <p>OPEN-MK POLITIS.</p>
        <p>WHAT IS RAW MATERIAL?</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting held on Monday at Budge Foot under the auspices of the Warrington Progressive Labour League was again fraught with enthusiasm, the great crowd which assembled following the excellent speeches very intently. Mr. W. H. Sherhern presided, end along with him in the van were Mr. Herbert Wilson (of Liverpeol), Mr. E. Wynne-Jones and Mr. Harry Nev. The CD AIRM As: said that by the parsing of the Veto Bill the foundations had been laid for reform for a long time in the future. They had previously laid down a foundation of land reform which had cost them a general election. The land income taxes looked very small, but they were big enough to cause perturbation in certain quarters. What seemed to be more of a plague than the taxes was the valuation scheme which was included in the 1909 budget. Though those things did causes general election, they did not involve a change of Government. (Applause.) Continuing, Mr. Sherburn said that Mr. Morrell, the Liberal member for Burnley, had raid that when Liberal. showed themselves unsympathetic with Labour then it would be a had day for Liberalism. He fully agreed with that. (Applause.) Referring to payment of members of Parliament the speaker said it was strange bow sensitive same people became when income from the Government misspoken of. It was all right when it was going in PENSIONS OF £1,200 AND MOOR to those who had already plenty, but when it was going in any other direction such as for payment of members then they beard the old, old cry that when people were paid they would not works, well. He wondered whether they said that in business ; be wondered whether the directors of industrial concerns would prefer to put the management of their works into the hands of people who have plenty of money and time because they would do it for nothing. (Laughter.) It would have been better if there had been payment of members when legislation each as that for enclosing Wilk lands was passed. If working- men had been in Parliament then we should have had different history of England to have understood. By the payment of members men with ability though of moderate means would be enabled to share in the Government of the country and we should be much bettor for that. (Applause). Mr. HERBERT WILSON delivered an excellent speech characterised by much humour, He said that as a work- ingman be owed almost everything that was wor:h having to Liberaliem.Liberalism meant giving the people a chance and trusting them—Conservatism meant thrust- ing the people and he much preferred to be trusted than thrusted. (Laughter.) They often came across people who said they were not interested in politics, hut they could bet their bottom dollar that those were the people who voted Tory, for if they did think about politics they would vote otherwise. (Laughter end I applause.) Of late years there were thousands of works ingmen who had wakened up to the fact that COO. servatiam meant nothing for them, for the Conservative was the man who was prepared to stay as he was. Of course if he (the speaker) were Lord Derby or a poor Duke be would prefer to stay as he was —(lauglater)—but could they imagine workingmen, who I worked almost every hour that God sent, saying they were Conservatives and therefore preferred to remain where they were—the stick in the mud attitude. (Laughter.) Rich landowners talked a lot ebout unemployment. They told the people that they were sorry for the poor and that it wee more work they wanted. But unemployment was not a bad thing stall when they had an income of 30s. a minute as a certain duke had. They wanted more of that kind of unemployment among workingmen. What the workingmen wanted really was not more work but more pay and shorter hours. Vt'hat bed the Conservative party ever done for the workingman ? They had done him at every turn. (Laughter.) As Liberals they stood for the pe.tple and by the people they stood.</p>
        <p>. . LIIIKRAL LE-OISLATION WAS FOR Tilt PEOPLE as history proved. For workingmen to oppose the Liberal or labour programme was only to deteat their own ends and they aere helping those who had always been their foes. What had the Conservative party done in recent years for the workingmen ? They had refused to take the tax off paper, because they said that I if they did he would be able to have a cheap newspaper and soon know as much as the rich. Vell, he wonld not have much to learn, commented Mr. Wilson amidst laughter. It had now been suggested by the Tory party that Tariff Reform would help the workingman. It was A WONDERFUL STORY AN!) A SPLENDID CATCHPENNY (Applausa.) Mr. Balfour speaking at York in January, 1910, said that he verily believed a 2s. tax put upon corn would reduce the price of bread—(laughter)— and there was not another man oatside Rainhill who believed that. (Renewed laughter). The very next day I after Mr. Balfour mode that statement, the Liverpool Millers' Association sent out a circular in which they I said that in the event of a tax being put upon wheat I flour would be raised in price to the HMO extent. He I(the speaker) wanted to know if a Sr. tax on corn would I reduce the price of bread why should they not put a 2fis. tax on and get their bread free? (Laughter end applauae.) The tariff reformers said they were not going to tax raw material hot only manufactured goods. In St. Helens the Conservative'. said, " There shall be a tax upon glass," but when they were talking to the Builders' Association they raid, " There shall never ba a Lax upon glass because it is raw material of the Luilder.' In Bermondsey where a good deal of leather was made Tariff Reformers said " We will put a tax upon leather " but when they went to Leicester and Nottingham " We will never put a tax upon leather, because it is the raw material of the boot makers." In Sheffield they said, "We shall put a heavy tax upon steelplates," but when they went to Birkenhead they said," There shall never be a tax upon steel plates, because they are the raw material for ship- builders." (Laughter and applause.) Tariff Reform meant loving the workingman— (laughter)—but if it were a good thing for the workingman they would not find bug landed proprietors running the show. The greatest landowner in Yorkshire had said that if we had Tariff Reform his rent roll would go up ,£250,000 per annum, and he would be able to give more to charity. They did not want charity, they wanted thei r righta, exclaimed Mr. Wilson to the aocomprniment of loud applause. In conclusion the speaker dealt with Home Rule. lit said that as the Tories could not gull the people with Tariff Reform they said they would give them a run for their money OD Home Rule. (Laughter.) The Isle of Man, Australia and Canada had all got self-government and yet they seemed to thrive, bat the Lords raid "We must not give it to Ireland " and then they whispered among themselves "The land is ours." (laughter and hear, hear) If Home Rule was a bad thing how WAS it that an opposite policy had nearly emptied Ireland? (Applause.) Mr. E, WYNNII JONES said he was glad to ficd that one of the works in the town had advanced some of the men's wages 2s. a week. (Hear hear.) That was far better then any Tariff Reform picnic for now the men would be able to take the children themselves Everyone of them must be glad that the labour trouble' bad come to a close and that there was now a chance of arriving at a lasting settlement. He was glad to find that</p>
        <p>MR. RYLANDS- TIM A BLEST MAN IN THE LOCAL TORY</p>
        <p>had made a splendid speec—h— -regarding strikes. Some- thing wool.) have to be done to see that the railway trouble did not occur. What was required was compel/wry arbitration in all disputes. (Applause). There was wanted something else alto—the nationalisa- tion of railways, because they were a national meet and convenience and should belong to the State and not to any company. He was very glad that the ' Warrington railway men acted no splendidly. Perhaps itrwas because members of the Old Academy, impelled by the statue of Oliver Cromwell, had volunteered to act as constables. Alderman Bennett was a member of the Old Academy and he had been besting the big Imperialistic drum for a long time. He (Mr. Joliet; could fancy him saying that it was no good him going on beating it any longer because Canada had gone and put her foot through it. He could also fancy the Alderman, in view of that fact, moving the resolution that they should take an intercat in local affairs and become constables. (Laughter.) Perhaps that was the reason everything bad been quiet here. (Laughter.; Continuing, Mr. Jones said that in the " Monthly Not of the Tariff Reform League it was stated that they did rot want to keep goods oat of the country, because if they did it would mean en increase in price. That was what Free Traders had been saying all along—they could not interfere with the fiscal system withcut increasing the price of goods. (Applause). He did not believe that men were going to be led away by the fallacies of the Tariff Reformers. The " Monthly Notes " admitted that foreign countries were in revolt against the Tariff system and that the reason was cruised by the high prices of food Bluffs. Tariff Reforrcers were continually giving their own case away. (Applause). In Iti92 Professor Ifewins—aoha got such a beating the other day in the Middleton division—said the old system of Protestion in England "appealed to the worst prejudices of the time. It played into the hands of the landed classes and led to the mischievous corn Mora . . . It meant that English industry and commerce should hecramped and distorted by protected tariffs which were imposed, not in the interests of the country as a whole, not to rupply the financial needs of the government, but in response to the demands of the ignorant and self-interested cliques of merchants and manufacturers who feared without reason the competition of foreign rivals." "The history of the Trading Companies is not without its warningn at a time when certain powerful • interests' have undue irfluence in English representation. Nor is the history of that time favourable to a close association of the Government with t•ade. Mtn isrmtri:nENcs WITH TRUCE HAS C KWIC 11.1. Y</p>
        <p>NI VA VT ,I.ASS 1,60 181.ATION</p>
        <p>and favour thown to one section of the community at the expense of others." (Applause.) That wax what Professor Ilewins said in 1591 Ile might change his opinions but the facts remained. (Applome)• Mr. HARRY tiy., also addremed the meeting. We w,id that when the Plural Voting went Harrington, as far as Toryism wee concerned, went with it. (A Voice : Never.) Ile knew this, be replied, that whereas now at an election men come Into War-</p>
        <p>rington from Stockton Heath, Altrincham, Rontaford Lymm and other quarters to record their votes, that at the neat election they we-e going to put a barrier along the Ship Canal and say " Go back, you have got a vote somewhere else." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ICHILDREN'S CORNER, Ccnducted by UNCLE CALEB -,,--x--,...",„--,\,w,,,,--,.--v--•"%es</head>
        <p>ICHILDREN'S CORNER, Ccnducted by UNCLE CALEB -,,--x--,...",„--,\,w,,,,--,.--v--•"%es</p>
        <p>THE JUMPING BEAN.</p>
        <p>I have often in the big cities seen men selling those curiosities called jumping beans. ordinary looking beans which jump and turn in most unexpected ways. Perhaps my readers have also seen them and would like to know what they are. The bean comes from a bush, closely resembling the coffee- bush, that grows in the Yaqui River country of western and northern Mexico. The bush attains a height of from three to four feet, and the hundreds of blossoms that it puts forth are of an olive-green tint, which, after ripening, turns to a buff gray. Within each blossom are two " fertile, ' seeds, and a third, which is the home of a small, exceedingly active worm, whose performances are responsible for the queer conduct of the bean. When this worm emerges from its prison it becomes a beautifully coloured moth. _</p>
        <p>The seeds of the jumping bean blossom in the month of May. Then the female moth deposits one egg on the pollen of the flower. As the flower develops it forms a triangular- shaped shell on two sides, with a convex shape on the other. Within this the chrysalis develops into a grayish-brown worm about one-tenth of an inch in diameter and about half an inch in length. This worm lives inside its cell for a period of six months, or until the middle of November. Then, climatic conditions being favourable, it bores a hole through the end of its shell and flies a way.as a moth. _ _ _</p>
        <p>If,' however, it is brought in the bean to a colder climate, the worm will die in the shell. Cut a small hole in the end of a jump- ing bean about an eighth of an inch in diameter, and in less than one hour the worm will cover the break with a white fibre-like substance from its stomach, work- ing the mouth around the opening until the hole in the shell becomes completely closed. Keep in a moderately warm place, and not packed too tightly, the jumping beans will live for five or six months to mystify and amuse. The jumping is caused by the worm's curling itself into a ball and then springing out to full length against the side of the shell, thus making the bean jump from the band ' into the air.</p>
        <p>TILE SF:OREL'. It isn't in doing your work, my boy. It isn't in doing the thing you must, That you win the honour and gain the j y. Oe claim the profit or earn the trust. It isn't in labouring I mg and late, And it isn't the hurry and noise that tell ; The smallest thing that you do is great, If you do it well.</p>
        <p>It isn't in getting it done, my boy, It isn't in getting it out of the way ; It is not in the methods you may employ, And it's not in the price thst your masters pay. It isn't in squandering precious time, And it isn't in rushing ahead poll tnell. If the thing that you do is to In sublime, You must do it well. E. KISER, THIS WEEK'S STORY. OUTWITTED. When travellers pass from one country to another they have to pay taxes upon certain articles such as wines, tobacco or lace. Two Germans were passing into France, and they thought they would have a joke with the Customs officer. "We have with us three bottles of red wine each. How much is there to pay?" "Where is it ? " was asked. " Well, inside us." The official pretended to look at his tariff book and read: " ' Wine in casks, twenty shillings; in bottles, forty-eight shillings; ,1 in donkeys' hides, free.' I " Gentlemen, "he added, looking up, "You can go." And they wished they .hadn't tried to be so funny.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>AN OBSTINATE PARENT. Unusual School Attendance Prosecution.</head>
        <p>AN OBSTINATE PARENT. Unusual School Attendance Prosecution.</p>
        <p>A case of considerable interest to parents was heard at the Borough Court on Tuesday before Councillor H. Roberts (presiding), Councillor C. Jackson-Ifolmee, Mr. W. Richardson and Colonel Edelsten, when Mr. T. H. Williams, an excise officer, residing at 110, Beweey- road, was summoned for neglecting to send his child to school without a reasonable excuse. Ile pleaded guilty to the neglect, but stated that he had an explana- tion to offer.</p>
        <p>The Deputy Town Clerk (Mr. Hallaway) ssid that defendant's child named Olive was live years of age on February 5.11 of this year. The child did not go to school at that time and a summons was taken out against the defendant but wan withdrawn on payment of coats and the production of a medical certificate. That certificate stated that the child would be able to return in four months' time. A month after the expiration of that certificate a school attendance officer called on the defendant and asked if the child was better and fit to return to school. The defendant replied ' What has it got to d, with you. I'm not going to tell you." A letter was then sent by Mr. Murray (the director of education) to the defendant, calling his sttention to the expiration of the term or the certificate, but the defendant returned this letter torn in two. _ .</p>
        <p>The defendant : I object to this. I admit neglect and I ho' eon excuse. It seems these ststements are being made to produce prejudice. Mr. Holloway said what they objected to sou that they bad not had an opportunity of learning what the excuse of the defindant was and they were forced to come into court in order to hear for the first time the excuse. Whether the case be a reasonable one or not he asked that they ohould decide to give them the costa of the summons as they bad been treated in a manner which was most discourteous. They bad been forced to bring the wetter before the coon in a way that would not be expected from a gentleman occupying the defendant's position. Evidence was tendered by Mr. Ferrer, school attendance officer, as to coiling at the defendant's house and being given no reply. The Clerk (to the defendant) _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Did you receive the</p>
        <p>letter from Mr. Moore Murray' The Defendant I decline to answer. That has ; nothing to do with the case. The defendant said the child when five years of •ge was suffering from an illness which necessitated an operation sod when partly recovered be rent her away for three or four months. The medical certificate expired on July sth and es the school was on the point of bresking•up be did not send the child. The date of the summons was August 21st. As the school was than closed he could not possibly send her, but be had done RO the first day the school MO open (Monday of this week). The Bench dismissed the CIIB9. Councillor RoJerts said that as Chairman of the Bdrication Committee he should like to express eery strongly that Mr Williams had not osly treated the court but the Education Committee with contempt and sue man of his position he ought to have shown more courtesy. The Defendent : You ought not to maks that state- ment from the Bench.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>PRESSURE OF POVERTY,</head>
        <p>PRESSURE OF POVERTY,</p>
        <p>At St. Helens on Wednesday, Robert Corner, of 23, Beetinok-street, was dealt with flr steanog • piece of copper cable, video IS.. 6d.1 from the Corporation electricity works, Cropper's-hill. Constable Marlow env Um man crossing Kootenai-street cith the cable on Friday evening. the 25th inst. When arrested he mid, "It's a fair cop. I stole it from the electric worlib."</p>
        <p>Chief Inspector Goodall said that on December 7th last, Corner mu injured in the bead at one of the local works. He was piid compensation, and when he went back be was offered a job in another part of the works, which he aaid he could not do, and be was then pot on the calling list, lie had been obliged to sell part of bin furniture doting the pant month, lie was a •ery respectable man, and had ale ays been a good husband land father. The magistrates Slesers. B. B. Glover and P. Grundy) said they were sorry for the man's misfortunes, and sentenced him to one day's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>OUR SERIAL' STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL' STORY.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIOTITS RESERVT.O.</p>
        <p>THE FOUNTAIN OF BEAUTY</p>
        <p>BY L. T, MEADE,</p>
        <p>Author o The Rebellion of Lit Carrington," "A Sweet Girl Graduate," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XV.</p>
        <p>IT IT, BECULDE, AND BRING IT BACK TO ME. About nine o'clock on the evening of that same day, Lonsdale's hansom drew up out- side the Carlton Hotel. Ile sent in his card, made a certain inquiry, and was presently asked by one of the servants to follow him up a long, low flight of stairs. On reaching the top, he was taken down a corridor; then a door was opened. The servant announced: "Mr. Gerard Lonsdale," and Lonsdale found himself in the presence of the great Persian Minister. Mirza Kara Yusuf was dressed as a Euro- pean, and, except for the fact that he wore a fez on his head, which he instantly removed when Lonsdale appeared, he might have been an Englishman. He was a tall, powerful made man with massive features and a look at once of strength and cunning about his face. His voice was smooth and melodious as was the voice of that other Persian, Nadir Jule-</p>
        <p>ver. Not a trace of emotion or surprise passed over his features as he rose slowly with extreme dignity and made the slightest possible bow to Lonsdale. The servant withdrew, closing the door be- hind him. When he did so, Mirsa touched a small silver gong which stood on the table by his side. Instantly two men in the native dress of Persia appeared. "Draw the curtains to the door; stay out- side in case you are wanted," was the Mirza's command. _ _ .</p>
        <p>The men salaamed, and immediately with- drew. • • • . •</p>
        <p>Lonsdale felt a certain tightening about his heart. Nevertheless, he knew that he was safe. lie had the diamond, the "Foun- tain of Beauty," on his person. The Mires looked at him from head to foot. We have never met face to face before," he said. "No,' said Lonsdale. "You have very nearly cost me my life," said the Persian.</p>
        <p>"Unwittingly," replied Lonsdale. That matters nothing," replied the Per- sian. "A life for a life—l could take yours now if I pleased; not a soul would know." Lonsdale gathered courage from the immi- nence of the peril. "In a great hotel like this," he said, the man who murders another must remember that he is in England, and that however great his genius he cannot possibly conceal this fact from the world at large.' The Mirza gave the faintest ghost of a sar- donic smile. Then he said slowly: "We of Persia carry out methods with us. But I have no intention of murdering you, Mr. Lansdale, if you have brought me back my diamond." "I have, sir," said Lonsdale. Lansdale had put the diamond into a little case which he wore fastened to his gold chain. He was about to put his hand into his pocket, but withdrew it when the Mirza in. terrupted him. "One moment," said the Persian. have seen Nadir Julaver?"</p>
        <p>" You</p>
        <p>"A long time ago," replied Lonsdale. "I am well aware that it is years ago," said the Mirza, "but that matters little. With us in Persia time is of no moment. What we mean to do to-day we do to-morrow, or years hence; it is all the same; we invariably do it. Yon gave Nadir Julaver certain informa- tion when you were in prison." "It is of little moment what information I</p>
        <p>gave him," replied Lonsdale, recovering more and more of his spirit as he recalled the fact that his feet were on English ground. "I hold the diamond now in my possession, and I am prepared to give it back to you." "Ah!" said Mirza Kara Yusuf. "But something rather strange has happened. ,One of my servants will perhaps enlighten you. Something has occurred which puzzles me not a little." Again he touched his silver gong, and a third Persian, in magnificent white embroi- dery garments, appeared. "Seleucus," said his master, "will you in- form this gentleman exactly what you have heard with regard CI Nadir Julaver!' Seleucus turned his soft black eyes just for one moment in Lonsdale's direction. Then he dropped them, allowing the long silky lashes to rest on his olive cheeks. His words . _</p>
        <p>came slowly, but with great distinctness "His Excellency, the great Nadir Julaver, visited London, became acquainted with an Englishman, had an interview with him, had rooms in a part of the City called Peacock Buildings; sent his servant—one. Abbas—to follow the said Englishman into a den of thieves in a low part of the City. Later on, that same evening, Nadir Julaver, com- pletely disguised, wearing a long white beard, and in the tattered robes of a beggar, went to the said court and came away. The next day he sailed from Tilbury, and is now on his way back to Persia, accompanied by his servant—one, Abbas." "Thank you," said the Mirza, "you can go. Seleucus." The man noiselessly withdrew, letting the velvet curtain drop behind him. Lonadale found the great dark eyes of Mirza Kara Yuen( fixed upon him. "Now," he said, "I will confide in you. You know much, but you may as well know all. My life depends on the recovery of the diamond. lam by nature a man of keen ambitions. I hold a position in Teheran second to the Shah himself in importance. There are possibilities with regard to my future which need not concern you, sir, but suffice it to say that it is one of my whims to order my palv-e in all respects as the Shah orders his. My amusements, when I do con- descend to amuse myself, are the same as those of the Shah, and it is my great delight to fill my palace with treasures almost as great as "those which are contained in hie palace of the Shah. "The Shah has what is railed his Brilliant Room. So have I, Mr. Lonsdale. The Shah's room is full of mirrors—its floors have silken carpets. The chairs, tables, even the wains- cotting of the room are of gold. My room is similarly arranged, Mr. Lonsdale. the FPuth has what is called the Peacock Throne Room, and it is my pleasure to have a Grits Throne Room, for I dare not use the exact same name in my own palace. The Griffin Room is my secret delight. Only those thoroughly initiated into my life and plans are allowed to enter it. In the centre of it I have a throne, just as the...ll:bah has a throne in his room. At the bin of the Shah's Throne, which resembles what you in Eng- land would call a camp bed, is • star of brilliants which make the eyes blink. I also have my star of brilliants. 1. "The Shah knows nothing of my imitation of his grandeur. It is my secret pleasure to look as like him as I dare, to follow in all respects his life, his way, his magnificence— so dear to us of the East. I am his favourite Mirza. In short, I stand to him much as your Prime Minister in England stands to the King. Daily lam admitted to his pre- sence, and he trusts me as he would trust himself. He does not know that once when he was absent from the Peacock Throne Room I managed to possess myself of the greatest diamond, perhaps, in the world— that one which is called the 'Fountain of Beauty.' I put an inferior stone, but a real one, in it, place, and to this day the Shah is unaware of the fact that the real stone haa been removed. The other stone is also of great value, but there is a flaw in it which only the keenest expert would notice. I secreted the atone I had stolen, knowing well that if my theft were discovered I should be arrested nrvil beheaded on tb• spot</p>
        <p>"A day or two alter I had secured it, it was missing—taken by a minion of my palace. That happened seven years ago, and all this time I have been searching for it. I have many spies—hundreds, in fact—who are all in my pay, and who tell me everything that is going on. I believe these men to be faithful, because the smallest action of betrayal would</p>
        <p>mean their immediate death.</p>
        <p>"Strange to relate, the Shah has not yet missed the most famous diamond in the world, but believes it to glitter at the back ot his throne. Now, however, a terrible qnd serious difficulty arises. There is not the slightest doubt that for some time Nadir Julaver—a rival of mine, and one of the Shah's personal friends— has discovered my secret, and that he is using it to my ruin. His kindness to you, his liberat- ing you from prison, proves it all too well. Can you swear, sir, that you told him nothing, that you gave him no information which he could go upon with regard to that stone which you purchased for a beggarly sum from ithe wretched man who stole it from</p>
        <p>me?" "I can swear it, your Excellency," said Lone - dale.</p>
        <p>The Mirza was now so excited that he rose from his seat and began to pace up and down the room. Ills steps resembled those of a panther, so swift were they, so restless, and withal so silent. His lithe body seemed to turn without the least effort on his part. His strange, deep, unfathomable eyes sought the ground as though in thought. He held his hands behind his back. On his fingers glit- tered gems of dazzling brilliancy. He suddenly turned to Lonsdale. "The recovery of the stone, the great stone, the 'Fountain of Beauty,' matters little to me," he said. "But if, before I have time to put it back, my real master discovers that I stole it from the Peacock Throne Room, no- thing could possibly appease his wrath at such an act, and my death would be certain. I should die by slow torture, which is his way of avenging himself on his enemies. Now, sir, can you tell me why Nadir Julaver came to London? Wily did he see an Englishman? Why did he go in disguise to a den of thieves? Why has he left London so swiftly and sud- denly? Can you answer any of these questions, Mr. Lonsdale?" .</p>
        <p>"None," replied Lonsdale. "I have never laid eyes on Nadir inlayer since the day when he kindly interfered to have me liberated from prison." "May the curse of the Prophet rest upon him!' said the Mirza. "If you lay rotting now in that dungeon where I threw you. I should be in less danger than I am at the present</p>
        <p>moment."</p>
        <p>"Sir," said Lonsdale, "forgive me, the matter lies henceforth in your own hands. You have but to be first at Teheran, and all will be well. You have but to restore the stolen property, to put it back in its place in the Peacock Throne Room, and no words on the part of Nadir Julaver will injure you." "True," said the Mina. "But how is it to I be done?" _</p>
        <p>-- - "That, sir, is your part; I have got you back the stone."</p>
        <p>The Mirza held out his hand. "Give it to me," he said. Lonsdale instantly took the precious gem from its case. The Mirza's hand closed over the stone. He then laid it on a piece of velvet which was on the centre table. Lights , innumerable leaped from the gem. The dia- mond seemed immediately to dominate the entire room. "I have to prove whether this stone is genuine or not," said the Mirza. Lonsdale threw back his head proudly. "The stone is genuine," he replied. "It was kept for me all these years by a nian whose honour is irreproachable. Please remember, Mirza Kara Yusuf, that the stone you call the Fountain of Beauty' is in reality no more yours than r tolir ofmyi not.. o;asvier, 1 rletaumrneldotitgofolooto and lost the chance of securing a large sum of money. You stole it, sir. I bought it from a poor Persian, being a young man at the time and absolutely ignorant of its true value. All I ask of you now, sir, is to remember that I am PP Englishman in my own country, and to allow me to wish you good evening.'' These words, strange to say, did not in the least annoy the Mirza. He was silent for a minute. Theo he said in an equable voice: _</p>
        <p>"If the stone proves to be real, I will never trouble you again, Mr. Lonedale. I don't even now ask for your address, for I have means of knowing all about those people who are of interest to me. You can go, Mr. Lonedale, but if you have played inn a trick, please under- stand that no power on earth, no guardian- ship, no care, can save your life. Please also understand that before you retire to bed to- night, the name of your hotel and the very room you occupy therein will be in my pos- session. I shall further ascertain the nature of the keys with which you lock your door and the sort of window which gives light to your bedchamber. You may move your lodging to- morrow, but I shall still follow you—unseen, unknown. You will imagine that you are free as air; but you will never for a single instant be out of my cognisance. If you have played me false, I strike you down. There will be no commotion. Your coroner will give a :er- dirt of Death from natural causes.' Your life will be swept out. So is it with my enemies. I warn you, sir. Now _v.." - .</p>
        <p>As the Mirza uttered' the last words, the velvet curtains were swept back from the door, and two Persians, in their native dress, held it open for Lonsdale to pass through. As soon as ever he had gone, the Mirza desired the two men to come into his presence. "Both of you follow that gentleman. Bring me the particulars I require. Go at once." They salaamed, and departed. The Mirza ' then took up the stone and looked at it. He flung it with some petulance on the velvet cushion. He touched his little silver gong. The Persian whose name was Seleucue entered, and salaamed to his master. Ile was a glitter- ing object, sparkling all over with heavy gold embroidery on his white dress, The Mirza took the gem, and tossed it into the servant's hand. "Test it, Seleueus, and bring it back to me," he said. Without the slightest movement of surprise the man withdrew. While be was absent, the Mirza continued to pace up and down the room. His movements resembled those of a panther more and more each moment. Seleucus premmtly returned. He salaamed twice. "Well?" said the Mirza. "Very well done indeed, sir," was the Oriental's reply. "No Western could have accomplished this piece of work. The gem has been imitated by a son of the East." "Ah!" said the Mina, not showing a trace of anger or the faintest touch of surprise. "Nadir Julaver—no doubt." "That is my opinion, Your High Excellency," said Seleucus.</p>
        <p>The Mince sat down by a table, drew the atone towards him, and looked at it for a minute, an evil glitter in his eyes. "Then our course is quite clear," he said. "There is not the faintest shadow of doubt that Nadir inlayer has got the real gem—the real ' Fountain of Beauty.'" The Persian was silent, standing still as a statue, with folded hands. "When did he sail, Seleucus? " asked his master.</p>
        <p>"The day before yegterday. He went from Tilbury in a merchant vessel called the Amaryllis, lie may, and probably will, change to one of the great liners at Mar- seilles, but he cannot get there for several days."</p>
        <p>• "Good," said Mirza Fara Yusef. " Seleucus, must meet Nadir Julaver at Mar- seilles. Go on board whatever vessel he sails in for Persia, and get the diamond. If necessary to accomplish this job, you must even scuttle the ship. rause at nothing. A</p>
        <p>high reward is yours if you succeed; death by torture lies before you if you fail. Leave London to-night; go to Marseilles and— wait. Take off that dress. Disguise your-</p>
        <p>self as you know how. Go on bo-ard in any form. Here's money for you. I shall wait in England for a day or two until I have settled the Englishman, Lonsdale, and will then fol- low you. We shall meet at Teheran in five weeks from now." . . . .</p>
        <p>As the Mirza spoke, he thrust his band into hie pocket, produced come notes and gold, and gave them to the Persian who, silently and without uttering a word, left him.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XVI. traz CENTLEMAN FROM PERSIA. Rebecca Henshall slept soundly that night. A load was removed from her life. She felt</p>
        <p>light-hearted and like a young girl once again. She was interest i eld, too, n .Lonsdale. and hoped he would call and finish his she should see him next. She liked his face, his manner,</p>
        <p>. - - his upright bearing. The fact, too, that her father had taken a fancy to. him, .could not . .</p>
        <p>but prejudice her enorM'ously in his favour. She was a girl who, hitherto, had taken no notice whatever of men, as men. She had</p>
        <p>been so placed in life that she made but few friends. Her father was the idol of her life.</p>
        <p>He was all sufficient to her. What she did, she did for him.</p>
        <p>She tolerated Mary Gray until she found her out, but she never cordially cared for her. Mrs. Lorrimer, who was only a few years older than Rebecca, had taken a fancy to her and endeavoured to introduce her into society. The girl was glad to go out occa- sionally. She was bright and pleasant in the presence of all those men and women whom she suet, but she made no very special friends. Lady Jane Halliday was also anxi- ous to be good to the young heiress. She came to see Rebecca on the afternoon follow-</p>
        <p>ing that on which Lonsdale had called. She noticed the girl's improved appearance and congratulated her. "By the way, my dear," she said, "you re- member that young woman, Mrs. Deepe?' "Of course I do," said Rebecca. _ .</p>
        <p>"Well, you know, I took a sort of fancy to her; I thought she would prove useful to me." _</p>
        <p>—"She is quite deter enough to be useful tc anybody," said Rebecca. "''You never liked her."</p>
        <p>"But that fact has nothing to do with your finding her useful," replied the girl. Well, no; but I agree with you about her." "Why?' said Rebecca. "What has she done?' . .</p>
        <p>"She wrote to me, accepting my situation I offered it to her immediately after I saw you. It was arranged that she was to cow on a certain day and take up her work. Well, she never turned up at all. I hays heard nothing of her from that day till now.,, .</p>
        <p>It was Rebecca's turn to look astonished. "How strange!" she said. "But surely. you sent her to me requiring a written testi- monial?" . .</p>
        <p>"I sent her to you requiring a written testimonial!" cried Lady Jane. "My deal Rebecca—what do you mean? I did nothing of the kind. Your word when I saw you wat all-sufficient. What could the young woman have meant?"</p>
        <p>"I don't know," said Rebecca. She felt a queer sense of doubt stealing over her. "Cep tainly Mrs. Deepe was admitted into my pre. sence—or rather, she forced herself into this room against my will—and pleaded so hard for a letter that I was forced to give it tc her. It was just a very ordinary letter, stat- ing that she possessed the ordinary qualifica- tions for your post, Lady Jane. f remembez I got ill immediately after signing the letter or rather, after directing the envelope. When I recovered, Mrs. Deepe had gone." "I am glad I never had her," was Lady Jane's rejoinder. " How queer of her to gc to you, and why did you get ill, my dear Did she do anything to frighten you? " "Oh, no," said Rebecca, 'nothing at all. but I have taken an antipathy to her." Just at that moment Lonsdale was an- nounced. Rebecca felt herself colouring as she rose to greet him, and she further knew that Lady Jane was cognisant of the tact. "how do you do?" she said to the young min. Then she turned to Lady Jane. "This gentleman, Mr. Lonsdale, is an old friend of my father's. They met seven years ago at Teheran."</p>
        <p>"Dear me!" said Lady Jane. "I am very glad indeed to see you, Mr. Lonsdale; and have you just come from Persia? It must be a most interesting place." "I find England far more delightful," said Lonsdale. He was at that moment in as high spirits as Rebecca herself. Lady Jane looked at him approvingly. "And RO you are an old friend of dear Mr. Henshall?" she said, softly. "It is most kind of Miss Henshall to put it in that way," answered the young man. "As a matter of fact, Mr. Henshall, when in Teheran, helped me round a tight corner, put me on my legs again; and, by no doing, earned my undying gratitude." "Won't you have some tea?" said -Rebecca, into whose pale cheeks a lovely rose bloom had crept. "And I have done my tea, and am forget- ting that time flies," said Lady Jane. "Well, Rebecca, what you have told me with regard i to Mrs. Deeps s most extraordinary. What a mercy I never got the young woman into my house! But now, my dear, before I leave,, I shall insist on your coming to dine with use. Have you an engagement for to-morrow evening?" "Oh, I don't go out yet," said Rebecca. "But I shall be alone, quite alone—and unless you specially object to Mr. Lonsdale's presence, I meant to ask him to come also. I am so much interested in Teheran. I once had a nephew there—a scapegrace young fellow enough, but very 800 d-looking. You will come, of course you will, Rebecca. Mr. Lonsdale, aid me in pursuading her." "I will come," said Rebecca in a grave voice. _ _ .</p>
        <p>"And you—Mr. Lonsdale?" "Thank you," he replied. Lady Jane left the house, mentally rub- bing her hands. She was a born match- maker. She felt certain that Rebecca liked Lonsdale already. The young man had a good face. Even if he were poor, it did not greatly matter; Rebecca herself was rich. "I shall be doing a kindness to my old friend, Robert llenshall," she said to herself, "if I bring a match about between those two. , What a mercy that I never got that woman, , Deepe, into my family! Really, dear Mrs. Lorrimer is right, and I am not half careful enough." Meanwhile, Lonsdale acceptrd the cosy chair which Rebecca offered him. "You look much better to-day," said the girl, speaking in her frank tone. "I am," he replied. "Like yourself, I have a load off my mind." "Did you, Mr. Lonsdale, give the diamond back to that fearful personage, Mires Kara Yusuft" "I gave it to him last night." , "You saw him personally," said the girl. "Yes; I had an interview with him." "Was that the first time you ever spoke to him?" "Yes; a great dirza like Kara Yuanf never condescends to speak to ordinary people. I have seen him driving by in his carriage surrounded by his retinue. You can have very little idea, Miss Denali*ll, what magnificent state is kept up in the East." "I have read about it," said Rebecca, her eyes glistening. "It seems to me like the 'Arabian Nights Entertainments' over again. lhe account fascinates me. I hope, Mr. Lonsdale, you will tell me plenty of stories of the magnificence of the East." "I will, with pleasure, if you will give me the opportunity, said the young man. "This Mirza ranks next to the Shah in magnifi- cence. Well, he has got the diamond. It is a good—a very good thing that you were able to protect the real stone, for he assured me that if I had played him any trick, my life wou:d not be worth a minutes purchase." . "Ah, yes; my father and I took care of the stone for you," said Rebecca with a smile. They talked together a little longer, find- ing many subjects of natural interest; but for some reason Lonsdale did not care to de- say rai" —Of his Peraian eAxiseismaia</p>
        <p>,ist day. US said - • • "It rests me to talk of the things that con- cern old England. It is indescribably sooth- ing to be in my native land agaid.," Rebecca felt that his presence aiso incithed her. She asked him many questions about his mether. He described her patient, self- sacrificing, noble life. He took a little photo- graph of her out of his pocket and showed it to the girl He spoke with sadness of her death, and described to Rebecca how it had been the great dream of his life to come back to her well off and to give her every comfort. You have lost your mother, and I my father," said the girl. "I can sympathise with you, and I do." He looked long into her lovely eyes, which were full of pathos at that moment. Sud- denly he rose. "I must go," he said. "I shall meet you to-morrow evening at Lady Jane Halliday's." "Yes, I shall look forward to it," said Re- becca. ' She did not omit to ask him where he was staying. He left her, ana'. she sat on by the fire ali,orbed in a day ,ream. She had no thought of falling in love with Lonsdale;no thought of love of any sort had entered her mind; but she was -attracted by him. She felt that she had found a frienf in him, and that her father was right in his appreciation of the young man. Rebecca was just rising from her chair preparatory to going upstairs to change her dress for her solitary dinner, when Lucy came into the room bearing a few letters which had arrived by the last post. Rebecca took them in her hand. She was tired of letters. She had read so many since her father's death. Her bead ached at the mass of correspondence which her lawyers were always showering upon her. She was about to lay these letters aaide in- tending to look at them on the following day, when the handwriting on one of the envelopes attracted her attention. It was from Mrs. Deepe. Rebecca stood hesitatin i g with this letter n her hand. She had laid the others on the table. She felt a great loathing of this woman, and a fierce determi- nation to have nothing whatever to do with her—to banish her once and for all from her life. Should she read the letter, or should she burn it; . .</p>
        <p>Her first indignation at Mary's writing to her cooled down, and curiosity got the upper hand. Of contse, Mary Deepe was already beginning to bag for money. What else cculd Rebecca expect from such a woman— a woman who had united herself to so con- temptible a being as Arnold Deepe. She tore open the envelope and read its contents. Mary Deepe's letter was not very long, but it was sufficiently astounding to cause the girl to gasp once or twice for breath, and then slowly, with a long shiver, to sink into the nearest chair. 'lle letter lay open on her lap. Her wide, frightened eyes seemed for a short time to be looking at some awful vision. They had the expression which inay be seen in the eyes of those who have received a swift, sudden, and awful blow. Then, recovering herself, shaking herself, as it were, out of her deadly torpor, Rebecca Hen.hall took up the letter and read it once more. Its brief contents were as follows: "Rebecca Ifenshall,—l, Mary Deepe, who lived in your house for five long years as Mary Gray, and who served you and your father well according to my lights, am now about to leave England for ever. I go; my husband goes also. You will not find us, search as you may. We are neither of us fools, and have taken pains to secure secrecy in our future movements. Besides, we are under powerful protection. Under these cir- cumstances, therefore, Rebecca, I feel little fear in writing you a last letter of farewell. Whcm you are reading it I shall be far away, for it will not be posted for several hours after I leave London. You will, indeed, never sec my face again, nor be worried by my presence. Nevertheless, I doubt very much if you will forget sue. On the con- trary, it is my impression that you will be haunted by the memory of Mary Deepe and by the knowledge of that which she has done. Confession is good for the soul, and I do not</p>
        <p>„ scruple to confess to von. "Rebecca, on the night that your father died, with the aid of Arnold Deepe, who was then my lover, and is now my husband. I crept into the cupboard in his room, getting there through an entrance which Arnold made for me on the landing. I waited in ambush, and in 'such a position that I could hear every word that you and your father spoke. I was an eavesdropper ; I confess to niv iniquity. Now for my second crime. "I remember how coldly and with what ap- parent cruelty you turned me from your house. You thought two hundred pounds would make tip to me for your deed. Little you knew. When I parted from you I vowed revenge. You will recall the day when I forced myself into your presence and de. mended a letter from you to take to Lady Jane Halliday. I forced you to write the letter, for, after all—you may not acknow- ledge it, but it is true—my will is stronger than yours. I presented you with an enve- lope which in all respects was identical with one of your own. There was no difficulty about that, for I had taken a few of your envelopes away with me. On the gum of the envelope was a certain preparation which caused you when you licked it to become im- mediately- unconscious. It was of the nature of a poison, but was not, as events proved, sufficient to kill you. It caused you, how- ever, to faint, or appear to faint. During your faint, Rebecca Henshall, I took the d;a- mond, the 'Fountain of Beauty,' out of its case. It was lying against your heart, and I removed it, substituting for it a marvellous imitation, which was made by a great eastern magnate, who held the secret of how to manufacture false gems so perfectly that only certain tests could prove that they were not real. This great man knew the whole story of the ' Fountain of Beauty,' and could imitate it as no one else could. When you awoke from your trance, or faint, you were satisfied that you still possessed the real stone. You have doubtless, ere now, met with Gerard Lonsdale, and given him the false stone, believing it to be the real. "I have nothing more to say, only that my revenge is complete. "Mawr bzarE.” (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
