      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>OYSTER SHELLS FOR FOWLS.</head>
        <p>OYSTER SHELLS FOR FOWLS.</p>
        <p>The best method of preparing oyster shells for ponitry is to place them in lime, niece to born them simply converts them into carbonate of lime, which is equivalent to chalk. Some unslaked lime should be placed in a galvanised iron receptacle, and after the shells have, been deposited in the centre of it, sufficient water bhould be poured on the lime to &amp;lairs it., If SD old sack then be placed over the receptacle, the heat generated in slaking We lime will destroy any decomposing animal matter that may he adhering to the oy,ter shells, and will purify them wi,hout volatiliaing the valuable chemicals they contain.— Agricultural Economist and Ifortlcui rut /Z.:rut tr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>POACHING. The charge of poaching in Walton Park, or, which two men stand remanded in custody at Haresbuly Petty Sessions, reminded me of an experience of my own as a youthful reporter for a weekly paper in a mining dietrict. Having made something of a hit by writing a sensational account of a somewhat perilous adventure in an old coal pit, I conceived the brilliant idea that if! could contrive to spend a night with a gang of poachers, good "copy" might be made of it. I managed to get into touch with the genuine article, land an expedition was planned. I kept my appoint- ment, but the members of the party made such , elaborate not to say formidable preparations for all possible developments that I wan speedily reduced to a condition of' blue funk," and prayed them to have ,me excused. It occurred to me that, in case of an encounter with the representatives of the law, my plea that I was merely seeking to enhance nay reputation as a journalist might avail me little, and that altogether the game was hardly worth the candle. 0, it was pitiful—so my companions thought ; besides (I may as well finish the story), the night's adventure proved quite profitable from their point of view, and no such evil as I had feared betel any of the I company. EVENING SCHOOLS. To get back to respectability, I note that the even- ing classes in connection with the Technical and Commercial Institutes are shortly tore-open for the winter months. I take the opportunity to press upon the young folk concerned and their parents the importance of making the best of these chances of self-improvement. It ought not to be necessary to do more than . mention the fact, but that it has again and again to be emphasised and that appeals in relation to such matters too often fall upon deaf ears is well known to local authorities and to all who are actively interested in the subject. I may not enlarge upon it here, but as one who has seen how many who have been shrewd and persever- ing and self-sacrificing enough to thus continue their education after leaving the ordinary school have benefited in after life, I do urge boy and girl readers to forthwith study for themselves the current sylla- buses, copies of which are now obtainable (vide advt.) and determine upon adetinite course of study under the auspices of our enterprising local education authority. GENERAL BOOTH. If the veteran chief of the Salvation Army had carried out his original intention and included War- rington in his present itinerary, I might have written more respecting that very remarkable old man than in the altered circumstances is called for. When I first made the acquaintance of the Rev. W. Booth, as he then was, he was an obscure Methodist New Connexion minister stationed at Brighouse, Yorkshire. The last time I was in his company, he was on a summer tour similar to the one now in question. Able ugh f ostriches in years and suffering sorely on account of his eye affiictim, he spoke at considerable Ilength with unabated vigour and energy. Immedi- ately after his retirement from the platform, I inter- viewed him in an ante-room. Au attendant helped him to change his upper garments, and the General was in his shirt sleeves as we talked. I noticed that his arms were frightfully thin. IA LOCAL CENTENARY CELEBRATION. The commemoration of the hundredth anniversary of the Warrington auxiliary to the Bible Society or, to be precise, the preliminary announcement respecting that exceedingly interesting event—recalls Ithe memorable address read by the Archbishop when, on the occasion of the recent tercentenary celebration of the authorised venion, representatives of the churches made their presentation to the King. In his short review of the 300 years that have passed since the famous revision ordered by James 1., the Archbishop said of our English Bible, "It has I sweetened home life ; it has set a standard of pure speech ; it has permeated literature and art ; it has helped to remove social wrongs, and to ameliorate conditions of labour ; it has modified the laws of the realm and shaped the national character." The British and Foreign Bible Society was founded in , 1804 for the purpose of issuing the simple text of Holy Scripture in all the languages of the world. I always make a oint of reading all about it that comes my way, but I have not yet any information with reference to the doings of the past year beyond last week's newspaper summaries. I remember, however, that the previous year's issues reached the then unprecedented number of 6,620,021 including 813,18 k Bibles, 1,198,226 New Testaments, and 4,578,011 smaller portions of Scripture. This result was 685,000 in excess of the output in the previous year, and constituted a record in the history of the Society. Throe times in the last seven years the total has exceeded 5,900,000. The immense growth in the Society's issues has been a growth of portions, for the most part of Gospels and Psalters which circulate mainly in the mission field, and the marked increase is chiefly due to the advance of Christianity in the Far East. The expel iences of the noble band of devoted colporteurs who carry these to dark places to which regular missionaries have not penetrated, go to show that truth is indeed stranger than fiction. So much for the parent society; the story of Warrington's five score years' effort in asiiociation therewith will be much better told on the occasion to which these remarks are intended to direct public attention. THE DISTRICT NURSES. I should like alter to commend the work and needs of the Warrington District Nursing Association. The question has been raised whether constant attendance on sick and suffering persons results in callousness. The, thinks the " Nursing Mirror," depends upon individual character, and-here comes in the import- ant point whether the nurse considers her work merely ass means of earning a livelihood, or whether she regards it as avocation. In the former rase there is a serious danger that the constata sight of pain may result in indifference, but in the latter the effect must be to deepen the nurse's sympathies, because she learns toexhibit them in a practical and self-restrained mode which excludes all mere surface-emotionalism. I believe readers generally will agree that Warring- ton is and has been very fortunate indeed in this regard. I happen to enjoy the personal friendship of several nurses. I need not explain how that has come about ; suffice it that some of these are now fulfilling the duties of their calling in various distant countries. One of them who went to Siam for a few years boa lately married a police official in Shanghai. As a correspondent recently wrote, many brave women leave England every year to take up nursing appoint. meats in the Colonies, China and India. Quiet, self- contained, resourceful and clever as are our nurses, they do not always take into consideration the risks that attach to their calling. In England the danger is minimised, and certainly it would go ill with any person in Warrington who meddled with one of these courageous women to her hurt—but why pursue the theme, for the thing is really quite out of the question. They and the institution to which they are so devotedly attached and the interests of which they so admirably serve will surely not be allowed to suffer through lack of adequate support.</p>
        <p>, THE FLOWER SHOW. - - " As a love of flowers grows, the practice of crime will cease, for certainly flowers and criminals are never seen together " ; " lovers of flowers are not only not criminal, but they are not quarrelsome " the work of spreading a knowledge of flowers is worthy of the best efforts of philanthropy." These were among many noteworthy ppints made by a lecturer upon the subject a while ago, and one could write at length in amplification and multiplication thereof. I reproduce the samples for the encourage- ment of those who have exhibited at the recent flower shows in this part of the country. Elsewhere I have seen many once highly successful and widely popular institutions like the Ilatton Society come to grief and disaster owing to the strange indifference of once warm supporters, and one would naturally be sorry and ashamed were the like spirit ever to prevail in this locality. TEST PIECE. In the case of the habitual drunkard before the Borough Police Court here last week, the complainant was the wife of the accused. She had a sad story to tell. Occasionally, a suspicious wife turns out to be happily mistaken. Thus: The hour was I am. Inside the dimly-lighted balLwav stool Mrs. Dorking with a grim smile on her face. The front door was bolted. 'Jobs," she said, in cutting extents, "you have been dissipating at the club again!" " Maria," spoke a voice outside, rapidly, clearly, and distinctly, he blew lugubriously on the blooming bugle' Instantly she unfastened and opened the door. Mr. Dorkins had not been dissipating.</p>
        <p>COMMIRIAL FAlLURY,.—According to Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the onmber of Commercial Failures published in Great Britain and Ireland daring the four weeks ending Saturday, August 26th, was 529. The number in the corresponding four weeks of last year was US4, showing a decrease of Ks, being s net [decrease in 11,11, to data, of 54'8.</p>
      </div>
