      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>l'; DISTRICT tipXls 1</head>
        <p>l'; DISTRICT tipXls 1</p>
        <p>Orford.</p>
        <p>SERMON, "1. ORFORD CA URC annual Sunday school and choir sermons, in connection with Orford Church, were held on Sunday, the preacher at the morning and evening services being the Vicar, the Rev. G. Vale Owen. Sp,cial music was rendered by the choir, includinf the anthems "We bow in prayer," and "Daughter of Zion." The collections throughout the .... day were divided between the Sunday School and Choir Funds. A flower service was held in the afternoon when Mr. Wratislaw, of St. Peter's, addressed the children. The service was conducted by the Vicar. A large number of the children brought bunches of flowera and plants which were afterwards taken down to the Infirmary. Stockton Heath. Sr. To..tt ts' MEN'S CI.ASS.—At the annual slaking day of St. Thomas' church a few days ago a good number of the above look part in the precession headed by a very handsome banner which was xery much admired and was entirely the work of the members. Encircling a large cross were the words: "St. Thomas' Men's Class Section B.' The banner was designed and painted by Mr. Frank Bliley, while the joinery and iron work and painting was done by Measre. W. Cleaton A. Bailey, 11. B,xter and J. Vernon. The class which has a membership of 120 is a very hive of workers the object of the leader being not only to keep them in touch with God and his church but as a means of assist- ing each other socially during the week, and with this object in view amongst other branches they have a guild, a saving hank, a helping hand fund, a bicycle and fishing club, the latest addition being an association football club.</p>
        <p>THE SWIMMING GALA. EXCITING CONTESTS.</p>
        <p>A swimming gala under the auspices of the Stockton Heath and District Swimming, Angling, and Boating Association took place in the lake at Stockton Heath on Saturday. A large number of people assembled and the event proved in every way a success. Some excellent swimming was witnessed and many exciting races were contested. The most important item was the race for the championship of the lake which drew five entries, the prize being a gold medal. After a bard fought race H. Beaman was returned victor, he being closely followed by J. Whitehouse and with F. Hartree a good third. Much amusement ass caused in the comic and obstacle races. In the former the com- petitors had to swim a distance of about 5 yards enter • boat and put one pair of socks, then swim to the line anddivest themselves of the socks on their return. J. Whitehouse was the first home. In the obstacle race the competitors after squeezing through a life- buoy had to dive under a boat, much amusement being caused by the inability of savant of the competitors to manage the latter by not takings complete dive and consequently coming up at the wrong side of the boat. The handicaps were followed by a polo match between teams representing Stockton Heath and Warrington, cap,..iined by F. Hartree and FL Beaman respectively. Some good play was witnessed on both sides and after a hard fought game Stockton Heath were returned winners by three goals to two. The prizes were distri- buted at the close by turn. Henry (treenail. The officials were as follows :—Starter, Mr S Beeman ; judges Mr J Coy, Mr J It Sutton, Mr Newton ; stewards, Mr C E B Sutton, Mr G Bayley, Mr E Downs, Mr E Melbourne, Mr J F Melbourne ; committee, Messrs H Beaman, J Cox, W Davenport, F Hartree, C Sacker, J It Sutton, .1 Whitehouse, J Potter, J F Melbourne. The results were as follow :- 50 Yards Ladies' Handicap.-1, Miss Warburton; 2, Miss Kineley. Championship of the Lake.-1, H Beaman ;2, .1 Whitehouse ; 3, F Hartree. 50 Yards Boys' Handicap.—Heat winners : 1, W Hindley ; 2, A F White. 1, H Hartree; 2, C Swinton. 1. J Venn ; 2, W Jackson. Final : I, C Swinton ; 2, W Hindley ; 3,11 Hartree. Diving Competition.-1, W Sutton. 50 Yards Handicap.—Hest winners: 1, W Langley ; 2,J Whitehouse. 1, H Seaman; 2, C Seiner. 1, J It Sutton; 2,0 Geniis ; Final 1,3 5 Sutton, 2, W Langley, 3, H Beaman. Obstacle Race.-1, II Beaman ;2, J Whitehouse. Veterans' Race.-1, F J Hartree. Comic Race,-3 Whitehouse. 50 Yards Novice Rsce.-4, H Hartree ; 2. A Moores. Polo Match.—Stockton Heath, 3 goats ; Warrington, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>GENERAL BOOTR.—On Monday morning some of the residents of Booth's Hill had the pleasure of seeing the grand old man" General Booth " as he passed through the district on hie way to Altrincham on his preaching tour. He looked quite cheery and waved his hand as he went by in his motor Car. CONG REG ATIONAL CHRISTIAN SERVICE BAN D.—A good number of young people attended this meeting on Monday evening. A very homely address was given by Mr. Roberts, of Stockton Heath. to the young folks. kir Percy Leather presided. ike meeting was con- cluded with several short prayers. DEATH OF AN OLD RESIDENT.—It Is with deep regret we tare to announce the death of Mr. Isaac Maim at the age of 82 years. Mr. flair, was gardener for the late Dr. Brabason and had been ailing for some time—having a bad fall some weeks ago which hastened his death. The residents of Lymm will miss Mr. Maim sitting at his shop door where he had sword for everyone and was recognised as a village worthy.</p>
        <p>ANNIVER,ARY SERVICES AT CHERRY-LANE.—On Sunday last the congregation of the little chapel in Cherry-lane cel. hrated their anniversary services. Mr. W. Brimelow, J.P., of Bolton, occupied the pulpit. In the morning he preached from the text " What is man ? and in the evening from the text "As I have loved you so ye ought to love cue another." The sermons were most interesting and appreciated by all the people 'rho attended in good numbers at both services. The choir are to be praised for their effective rendering of the hymns during the day. The collections were in aid of chapel funds. Misr. G. M. Greenman presided at the organ. CON'C. REG tTION tL Mu_ rtf R.—On Sunday last, the services at this church were conducted by the Rev. Herbert Dunnico of Liverpool. In the morning he preached from the text "if any man will come after Me, let him take up his cross daily and follow Me." In the evening he took for his discourse the parable of the woman putting the leaven into the hrerd. Good congregations attended both services and fully appre- ciated Mr. Dunnico's excellent sermons. During the evening rervice Mr W. Tynan of Warrington, rendered a solo " The Man of Sorrows," in a moat creditable manner. The services throughout the day paned off in a most satisfactory manner. URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the shove wee held in the Council offices on Thursday. Mr 0 G Smith presided, and there were also present :—MesersW G Groves, j Houk, F Lockyer, R H Gotohed, S Davies, T H Warburton, W Mullard (clerk), D Mort (surveyor), W R Donaldson (gas manager), and Dr De Jung (medical officer of health.)</p>
        <p>I.ROPOSED iVIDENIO OF CA MSLEY BRIDGE. At a meeting of the Roads Committee held on the 24th inst. the Surveyor to the Council submitted a plan showing the proposed widening of Camsley Bridge and he reported that the assistant County Surveyor recommended a road of sft. wide as shown on the plan. It wee proposed by Councillor Davies and seconded by Councillor Lockyer and carried unanimously that the plan be approved and a copy be sent to the County Council requesting their support in an application to be made to the Road Board for a grant in order to carry out Lr.ogo,saclospcyurto.iltif Velpnl:nhetPolath% Nita nwcteesatelBr° decided to p Casl Company calling theta. attention to thee xist:ngdangerot s conditions and ashing for their support in the propoEed improvecrnt. _</p>
        <p>1m1141104 ItOSPITAL OPENED.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Sanitary Committee was held cn the 24th inst., when the Medical Officer of Health reported that two cases of amulet fever had broken out in the district and that he had opeied the hospital an:: engaged a nurse at Its, a week. It was proposed by Councillor Davies seconded by Councillor Gotobed and carried unanimously that the action of the Medical Officer be confirmed.</p>
        <p>THE CONFERENCR AT LONDON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gotobed reported tbat be and -Mr. Mallard u</p>
        <p>delegatee attended the annual conference of the District Council's ASV dation held at London in July. They were received by the Lord Mayor of the City wi o gave them a hearty welcome. The programme, how- ever, wee interesting although the subjects dealt with were not of much interest to districts like Lymm. A hearty vote of thanks 77.18 passed to Mr. Crotched end Mr. Mullard for attending the ccoferetce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Culcheth.</head>
        <p>Culcheth.</p>
        <p>I,I,EPENDENT Micri!!!Diax CITA l'FL—The anniversnry sermons in connect m with this place were held on Sunday last, the preacher at the morning and evening services being Aldermen J. Evans of Warrington. Special hymns and anthems were sung at both services by the choir. In the afternoon a !service of song entitled " Little Minnie' was ably rendered by the choir. The various solo parts were sling by members of the choir and on the whole very efficient renderings were given.—Mr W Broadhurst the village postmaster was the reader. Mr II Griffiths of Manchester officiated BD organist and choirmaster. Collections were taken at all the services in aid of the trust funds.</p>
        <p>Padgate. REV. T. GLADDIN(..3 F —The Rev. T Cladding preached his farewell sermon to his Padgatr. friends on Tuesday evening laat, and the unusually large congregation who assembled to hear him was personal tribute showing the love and esteem in which he had been held during his three years' ministry at the Wesleyan Church at Padgate. After the service a purse of gold was presented to Mr. Cladding by the senior steward (Mr. VV. Bennett) again showing ftl a practical degree the manner in which Mr. Cladding most have wound him- self round the hearts of his parishioners. Speeches were made by Messrs W Bennett, J Duckworth, H Cottrell, F Wright, Dr Bennett and Alderman Bennett, and in every case ther spoke very highly of the work done by Mr and Mrs Cladding and family,knowing that after they had gone there would be vacancies which in every case it would be diflimit to fill. Both Mr and Mrs Gladding in replying to the numerous aedresses felt keenly the parting from the numerous friends they had made during the soj awn at Padgate and Mrs. Cladding in her remarks mentioned that the rest of the family had never been made to feel so much at home and made so many friends as they had done in the Warrington Circuit. Both Mr and Mrs Gladdin g have done a noble work in sit of the new Sniiday school and it is expected that Mr Cladding will take a part in the opening ceremony on the 25th of September.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>GARDEN PARTY.-0a Saturday afternoon a garden party was held under the auspices of the Fenketh Women's Labour League on the lawn at" Willow Bank,' the residence of Mr. W. and Mrs. Shaw who had kindly lent the same for the occrwion. This league was only formed a few months ago but has made great strides in this short time. The party numbered about 60 amongst them being Mr. and Mrs. J. A. Seddon, Mr. W. B. Macfarlane, who is on a visit to this country as fraternal delegate from the American Federation of labour to the British trades union congress, and Mrs. Dodds. Alter tea had been served on the lawn Mr. Seddon was called upon to speak. He said it gave him greet pleasure to see no many happy faces. l'onketh labour party was a credit to any party of the divisien. In speaking of the recent strike he said it had been a victory woo by the working classes, and if any one wan to blame for the prolonging of the fight it was the employers themselves for bringing in black leg labour to take the place of the rues on strike. Mr. Macfarlane also spokes few words in which be said he could assure them that they in America watched the doings of the workers in this country very clossly. There was a great spirit of fraternity growing amongst the workers throughout the world. The rest of the time was spent in games which were under the care of Miss E. Cooke (secretary of the Women's league.) The party was brought to a close at 8 o'clock when Mr. E. Marshall moved a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Shaw for the use of the lawn, and also to all who had helped to make the party such a success. This was seconded by Mr. Gill of Sankey.—Mr. Shaw said if they wished they could hold the party annually.—Airs. Cooke, president of the Women's League, Mrs. Bell and Miss Cooke also spoke and said the best way they could show their appreciation would be to get new members for the League.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Winwick. FLOWER SHOW._</head>
        <p>Winwick. FLOWER SHOW._</p>
        <p>The annual show of the Winwick Horticultural Soc.ety sae held on Saturday in the schoolroom, and was a great success, the exhibits being of a very high class. Mr G McAllister, head gardenerat the County Asylum, had a fine chow of fruit, plants and vegetables, (not for Competition). PRIZE LIST. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Foliage plants : Canon Penrbyn. Flowering plants : Mrs Brat. Cottage window plants : J Holmes. I Ling- ing basket T Rutter. Geranium, Thomas Potter. Fuchsia A Peters, One banging basket : Thomas Potter. Roses : 1, J K Fattener; 2, Mrs Bent. Double dabliss: 1, W Hodgson ; 2, J Johnson. Pompon dahlias : 1, W Hodgson ' • 2, T Johnson. Cactus dahlias : I,T Johnson ;2, W Hodgson ;3. J Johnson. Astern : 1,0 Hatton ; 2, T Rutter ; 3, W Britt:M. Stocks special : T Rutter. Gladioli : 1, %V Butch; 2, J Cooper. Chrysanthemums, special : R Copple. S meet peso: 1, Mre Best; 2, P Lonaton ;3, J Cooper. Twelve distinct cut blooms :1, .1 E Pettener ; 2, J Cooper; 8, T Rutter. Bouquet of out door blooms 1, J Cooper ;2, T Rutter. Culinary apples : 1, J W Knowles; 2, Mns E Carter ;3, J E Fattener. Dessert apple. : 1, Cam n Penrhyn ; 2, RCopple ; 3, Mrs Naylor Five pears : R Robinson. Red or white cnions : I, W Hodgson; 2..3 Johnson. Pickling onions: Special, A Peters. Leeks : I,T Rutter ; 2, A Peters. Celery: I, W Hodgson ;2, T Rutter. White cabbage : I, W Hodgson. Red cabbages : 1, J Johnson : 2, Mrs Lightfoot • 3, A Petors. Savoys r Special, W Itritch. Carrots 1, Carioca Penrhyn ;2, 0 Hatton. Parsnips : 1, A Peter.; 2, Canon Penrhyn. Beetroots : I, J Johnson ; 2, Mrs Lightfoot ; 3, .1 Cooper. Tomatoes : 1, T Rutter ; 2, Canon Penrhyo. White turnips : special, lths Lightfoot. Yellow turnips : V Dean. Round potatoes : 1, 0 Hatton ; 2, T Bowles ; 3, T Johnson. Flat potatoes : 1, V Dean; 2, Mrs Light- foot ;3, T Rowles. Peas 1, Canon Penrhyn ;2, J Gnsworth. French beans : 1, Mrs Lightfoot ; 2, W Hodgson ; 3, T Rutter. Runner beans : 1, R Coppie ; 2, G Baker ; 3, Canon Penrhyn. Vegetable marrows : I, GI Baker •, 2, T Johnson ; 3, .1 Walker. Curled greens: Special, A Peters, Broad teens : Specinl, .1 Johnson. Shallots 1, Mrs Lightfoot ;2, T Rowles. Collection of vegetables : IT T Rutter ; 2, W Hodgson. FARMERS' CLASSES. to cd potatoes 1, J Blackledge ;2, .1 Shallcross : 3, C Robinson. Flat potatoes : I, .1 Inackledge ; 2, P Longton ; 3, C Robinson. Swede turnips : 1, C E Brocklehurst ; 2, J W Knowles 3, C Robinson. Mangold. (yellow globes) ; 1, A Alker ; 2, W Fairclough ; 3, C Robinson.</p>
        <p>OPEN SECTION. Sweet peas: 1, F Fazakerly, ; 2, J Cooper. Roses, (special) : Mrs Ntild. Asters F Fazakerly. Stocks (special) : R Copple. Dahlias (cactus) : 1. FFezakerly ; 2, T Johnson. Porn-posts: 1, F Fazakerly ; 2, T Johnson. Gladioli (special): R Copple. Tomatoes: 1, T Rut.er ; 2, J E Pettener ; 3, T Johnson. Mr J W Knowles' special prizes for best cottage or farm flower garden. Cottage garden : 1, T Rutter; 2, G Hatton. Cottage or farm flower garden : 1, A Peters : 2, G Baker. NVindow plant, cottage or tuns : 1, T Po tter ; 2. GI Ilatton. Mr 0 Starkey's prize for peas J Enswortb. Mr W G Wallace's special .crize for ben eggs : Mrs. Bent. Children's prized for window plants and wild flowers E Derbyshire, E Baker, F Wallace, M Darbyshire, A Waldo, J Foden and L Dean.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>OCTOGENARIAN LADY'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>/el rat, Thomas, of Cowley Bank, St. Helens, a well- known resident, passed away at her residence on Satur- day. Deceased'. busband was the late fe:r. Robert Thomas, solicitor, and she leaves three sons and three daughters, the soon being Colonel R. W. H. Thomas, commending the St. Helens Rifles; Dr. A. Thomas, of Walton ; and Mr. C. R. Tbomas, solicitor, of Maiden- head. Although eighty-three veers of age, Mrs. Thomas as always active and youthful in her ways till her last illness a month ago. A good horsewoman sbe took • delight in the country end in all out-of-door-exercises. At home she was most charitable, and was beloved by all who knew her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>BLACKBERRYING TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>BLACKBERRYING TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>A verdict of Found drowned" was returned at an inquest held at St. Helens on Saturday on Margaret Ann Cropper (is), of 5, Grace's-square, Sutton, who was found drowned in • pond near Slicklehead Orem The girl had alsysys been healthy and cheerful. She set out on Tuesday afternoon to go with a letter to Rainhill, but never arrived, and her body was after- wards recovered in the water. It was stated that there were a large number of blackberry bushes round the water side, and 149 she was going on her way the girl was seen to be picking blackberries. The Coroner said it apreared most likely that the girl bad accidently fallen into the water while picking blackberries.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd-Georp has arrive-1 at his Welsh home, Brynawelos, Criccieth. lie will, according to present arrangements, remain at Criccieth at least a fortnight. Golfing, motoring and river fibbing are the Chancellor's chief holiday pastimes at home. The King motored on Saturday from Ittoy IhII to Tulchan Lodge, Advie, where he will remain until next Monday as the pest of Mr. sad Mrs. Sassoon The house party includes Lord end Lady Wolverton. Lord Derby, and Lord Herbert Vane-Tempest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ARRESI OF ARMED SHIPS.</head>
        <p>ARRESI OF ARMED SHIPS.</p>
        <p>Much excitement has been caused by the arrest of three vessels, one in the Thames and two at Barrow, with cargoes of arms and ammunition. The Foam Queen, the vessel seized in the Thames, is a two-masted steamer of SOO tons, and, it is stated, was being fitted up with mountings for four machine guns, with racks for several hundred rifles, maga- zines for powder and shell and provision for tinned and other goods for a considerable time. She was also fitted with " wireless " apparatus. Her destination is unknown, and the officers on board did not know where they were going. Our uhototranh shows the Foam Queen. off Gravesend.</p>
        <p>FOU MAKING CONTINU9C6 CONCUETZ</p>
        <p>to a flywheel provided with a crank handle. no that by tee oFeration of the crank handle the worm will be turned. The up- ward frame extension of the machine has a caws-piece serving as a handle for holding the machine with one hand while the other operates the crank handle. For machines of large capacity, the motive power may be supplied by an engine or motor of any design required. The shaft is also geared to the front wheel, to cause forward trac- tion of tire machine when the crank is operated. Ent rpm FOR Snips.</p>
        <p>Lieut.-Com. Allen, of the U.S.A. Navy, estimates that, weight for weight, fuel oil will carry his ship one-third further than coal, and at much less expense, the saving, of course, varying with the local market price of the oil. In his ship, one thousand tons of oil can be carried in the double bottom, over and above that which could be stowed in the bunker spaces, and this one thousand tons would be sufficient to carry the vessel about five thousand rules.</p>
        <p>A RAPES INVENTION. A. Swiss watchmaker has invented a chronometer whose hands move from right to left instead of the reverse way. It has been designed to meet the demand created in Tnrkey and Japan and other Oriental countries for a watch that works in a manner consistent with their methods of reading. If, as he states, the inventor has succeeded in obtaining patent rights which preclude the manufacture of left-handed watches by anyone else, his fortune is made.</p>
        <p>a • s , . . s . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>rOENTAIN INVENTION.</head>
        <p>A rOENTAIN INVENTION.</p>
        <p>A fountain adapted for use in schools and other public places has been invented which is so arranged as to form a jet that will make the use of a cup unnecessary. As shown in the sketch, the invention consists of a series of tubes which may be bent to any orna- mental design, and which at their upper end</p>
        <p>ENG FOENTA</p>
        <p>are trained to deliver the water to a common centre. The impact of the water at this central point produces a geyser-like jet over which the drinker can apply his mouth, while the unused water falls to the base of the drinking fountain. Thus one is assured of having pure, fresh water that has not been contaminated by the lips of anyone else.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RETIRED POLICE INSPECTOR'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>The death took place on Monday of en-Inspector Miles Burton, of the Lancashire County Constabulary. Deceased, who had retired only a few months ago, joined the force thirty 3ears ago, and was latterly in Widnes, where he was very popular. He leaves a widow and a large family. SUNDAY TRADING.</p>
        <p>A number of shopkeepers were summoned at Runcor on Monday for Sunday trading. Ike Chairman said a petition had been received from two shopkeepers, but it really had nothing to do with the summonses. It would be sent on to the Chief Constable.</p>
        <p>While the CUES were proceeding the Chairman said it had been mentioned, Irom time to time, that shops remained open in Widnes on Sundays. Runcorn people would not like to see Runcorn reduced to the level of Widnes. (Laughter). All the defendants were fined ss. COLLAPSE OF PUBLIC-HOUSE ROOF.</p>
        <p>The roof of the Derby Arms Hotel, Runcorn, fell in on Saturday morning, several tons of old fashioned flag roofing missing by sir inches the bed on which Larry Rees, the lanHord. and his wife were sleeping. The rest of the roof landed i 3 an adjoining unoccupied bed- room, and the roof of the servant's bedroom was cracked.</p>
        <p>_ The humorous feature was that late on Friday night the landlord herd mysterious noises, and celled in the police, wha searched the house without result.</p>
        <p>WORKMAN'S FATAL FALL.</p>
        <p>Coroner Brighouse held an inquiry at the Widnes Accident Hospital on Monday evening, touching the death of Albert Walker, aged fifty-one years, who lived at Brook-street, Widnes, and was a boiler fireman at Bolton's copperworks, where he tad been employed close on twenty-five years. John Lloyd said deceased and himself were engaged tarring a roof of the depositing shed, which was thirty yards in width. To do the work they bad two cat- ladders and stage plank with brackets, which were placed at an angle of 35 degrees. The work want on all right till 10.20, when the plank slipped. Witness slid down the shed, having fallen forward, while Walker eppeared to have gone backwards. In doing no he fell through a skylight on to a depositing tank, which caused a fracture of the base of the shall. The drop would be about 21 feet. Asked by his Majesty's inspector whether the ladders provided by the first were quite satisfactory, be replied " Yea." He had taken particular care to fix the ladders right. Harriet Walker, his wife, said that recently he had been unwell, and only resumed work the day before his death, after a few weeks' absence. He asked his employers to provide him with light work. He never regained consciousness after the accident, and died at 3 p.m. on the day of the accident. The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death." RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above was held at Dutton work- house on Tuesday. Mr T Wright presided and the officers present were Messrs G F Ashton (clerk), WE flough (assistant clerk), W Diggle (surveyor), J Farrington (inspector) and Dr Adams (medical officer of health).</p>
        <p>PREVALENT DIARRHEA IYIDFM IC.</p>
        <p>...... ...... The Clerk read 6 circular letter from the Local Government Board stating that they had bad under consideration the excessive child mortality, especially from diarrhea and enteritis which was accompanying the hot and dry summer. They all realised that some excess of mortality over that occurring in cool and wet summers wan inevi table, but they desired to impress upon the CLuncil the importance of taking such steps as were practiceable with a view to minimising the excess.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams (medical officer of health), said that the epidemic bad been very prevalent there being no lees than 892 cases in one week in London alone. In the Runcorn district up to the present there had been eighteen cases compared with practically nil in previous years. This went to show what shot summer had been experienced. . . .</p>
        <p>It was decided to have pamphlets printed and distri- buted advisiog parents upon the necessity of boiling milk before use and other matters which tended to alleviate this disuse°.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>TERRIBLE DISCOVERY NEAR WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE DISCOVERY NEAR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Paris Resident's Tragic End.</p>
        <p>INQUEST.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held by the district coroner (Mr. S. Brighouse), at the Institute, Moore, on Wednesday, on the body of Donald Smith, aged forty-four, a steno- grapher, employed in Pens, whom body was found in • trrribly mutilated condition on the London and North.' Western Railway at Keckwick, neon Moore on Monday afternoon. . _ . . . . _ . .</p>
        <p>a John Smith, brother of the deceased, a clerk residing at 109, Kent-road, Glasgow, gave evidence of identifica- tion. He stated that for the past fifteen years the ' &amp;ceased had worked in P iris, and he last saw him alive two years ago. On Monday witness received a telegram from his brother to the effect that be would arrive in Glasgow by the six o'clock train. He met three trains that night, and later was informed by a railway official that some luggage had been left in the train which arrived at Glasgow at 6.15. James Antrolons, a sub-ganger stationed at Moore, slated that about three o'cicck on Monday afternoon he received an intimation from the driver of the 1119 from Carlisle to Huston that a body was lying in the aix-foot-way. He found the body Is ing face downwards, practically naked, in a terribly mutilated condition. linsyeator Bell, of the London and North-Western Railwsy, said that from the marks in the ballast of the four-foot way, the deceased had evidently been underneath the train. James Persian, the attendant on the ten o'clock train! from Euston to Carl'sle, said the deceased's tourist ticket had been punched at Kristen asd Rugby. No report was made to him by any of the passengers that anything had happened 03 the journey from Crewe to Carlisle. Percy Edward Lewis, the guard of the train, ssid that he found the luggage in a third-class comp rrtment in the corridor portion of the train. It would have been impossible, owing to the high speed of the train, to abut the door of the compartment on the side of the train in which the deceased was supposed to have fallen out. The Corcne- said it was evidently clear from the disturbed track in the four-foot way that deceased MIS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>NOVEL URI</head>
        <p>A NOVEL URI</p>
        <p>TIME BY WIRELESS,</p>
        <p>The Observatory of Paris, with the help of the Eiffel Tower wireless station, has for some time past been signalling the time at midnight by wireless telegraphy, for the benefit of Alps at sea. These signals were given, one at midnight and the others at intervals of two minutes. It has now begun a similar service during the day. The first signal is wired at eleven o'clock, the second at 11.2. and the third at 11.4. So far it is not stated whether captains of vessels at sea provided with wireless apparatus have been taking notice of these signals, but there is no doubt that this plan may be of great use in the future, once it comes to be generally known, A GIAVICYARD IDEA.</p>
        <p>A curious apparatus has been invented by Professor Jules Mauler, head of the School of Mechanics at Neuchatel, to lower coffins automatically into the grave without the aiif of men and ropes. The invention is a kind of "lift," resembling a stretcher, on which the coffin is placed, and by its weight des- cends slowly into the grave, aided by wires. The professor predicts that in a few years every country will employ his invention, which is easily handled, is cheap, and does away with ropes and men "during a sad ceremony, where the presence of strangers is often resented." Tun Nzw SPONGE.</p>
        <p>An artificial sponge, as made by a Ger- man process, is chiefly a result of the action of zinc chloride on pure cellulose, which iv obtained from vegetable matter. The pasty, sticky mass produced by this action is mixed with coarsely ground rock salt, and then, in a press-mould armed with pins, it is pierced with a multitude of little canals, imitating the pores of a natural sponge. After the necessary porosity is obtained, the material is given a prolonged washing in dilute alcohol, to remove the excess of salts. The artificial sponge has the same action as the natural in absorbing and retaining water, and hardening on drying, and is especially recommended for the sanitary and industrial filtering of water, while it is adapted for all purposes to which the genuine product is usually assigned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>SCOTTISH BY-ELECTION.</head>
        <p>SCOTTISH BY-ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone's Grandson Invited to Contest Kilmarnock.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Glynne Charles Gladstone, grandson of the late Mr. Gladstone, has been invited to become Liberal candidate for the Kilmarnock Burghs, where a vacancy has been created by the death of Dr. Rolland Rainy. lhe seat is a safe Liberal one, though Cdlonel Denny was returned as a Unionist through his own and his family's well-deserved local popularity. The young squire of Hasarden has family associa- tions with the West of Scotland. Ilia maternal grand- I father, the last Lord Blantyre, was an extensive landed proprietor in Clydesdale, his anoestrial seat, Erskine /19.25 e looking over the Clyde to Dumbarton. . .</p>
        <p>The group of burghs Inch composes the constituency includes Kilmarnock, Dumbarton, Renfrew, Rutberglen, and Pun Gla.gow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>VOTES AND SPEECHES IN PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>VOTES AND SPEECHES IN PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>The recent Parliamentary session was a rather heavy one no fare, divisions were concerned, no fewer than 359 divisions being recorded, against 159 in the previous session. The members of the Cabinet recorded the following totals in the division lobby:</p>
