      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Parental Control</head>
        <p>Parental Control</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln this neighbourhood one cons.antly seesl bya and gi.ls rambling about in prim, after the fashion of persons "keeping company " as it is called. The girls are frequently mere children, and are sometimes no absurdly overdressed as to present veritable figures of fun. I do not know what ycur correspondents' opinion may be, but my own impression is that behaviour of this sort would have been punished in former days, with some severity. Is it that present-day parents do not know of there goings on, or that they do not care? The matter is surely too serious to be dismissed with the usual thoughtless remark that " boys will be boys."—Yours Ace . September Ist, 1911</p>
        <p>INQL'IRER.</p>
        <p>trers I_c to the ' I - A EDITOR.. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Alter-Thoughts on the Strike</head>
        <p>Alter-Thoughts on the Strike</p>
        <p>Sir, -lie would be: bold writer indeed who would dare to predict the extent of the dire results which this recent great national strike has left in its trail. There is no denying the fact that it has been the most s upendously organized strike that has ever taken place in the history of the railway world. Let us earoesrly hope, for the welfare of our beloved nation, that a recurrence of such an appalling catastrophy, between employers and employees, will, by rrompt legislative measures, be made an impossibility in the future. I readily admit that many of the men who went out on strike had real grievances, some o f them of long standing. What the general public are asking now is have the men taken the b at aeons in their power to air their gi ievances, and to win public opinion in their favour, which generally carries some weight M such eases. Many of the men will say that they were forced to adopt the methods they pursued in order to bring the masters to their knees. That may Pe to some e xtent true as regards "genuine" peaceful picketing. But the general lublic naturally ask the question, What god purpose is going to be nerved by holding up cur food supplies aid forcing up the cost of living in many eases to almost famine prices thereby causing empty cupboards in the hi mes of poor peot le who have a bard struggle to taint even when food is at normal prices ? Many of the strikers themselves will feel the pinch caused by the extra cost e f living, and no doubt lots of them will also feel the shortagesof supplies for some time to come. The married men especidly, must know from past experience that when a general rise in the price of food takes place it is a long time before, if ever, we get prices back to their former level lam neither prophet nor pessimist, but the outlook ahead is black indeed for any fall in prices in the near future. We are infe rmed that the Uovernment have proneised the Railway Companies a mandate empowering them to raise their rates or charges to the pubic, to such an extent as sill enable the Rail- way Companies to recoup themselves for concessions made or shout to be made to their employees. As to the wisdom or otherwise of granting such powers to ' the Railway Companies I shall leave till some future occasion, but by the very fact of traders having to pay an enhanced price on the carriage (Albeit goada, tiey will naturally, in turn, recoup themselves from their customers. It does take not a magician to foretell whose pockets will be touched most in the end of this great struggle. It seems to me thod after all the misery and suffer- ings endured and yet to be endured by the wives and families of many of the strikers, that financially the men will be no better off, and the public in general who had no connection n ith the serikes will be a great deal worse cff.---Yeura etc.</p>
        <p>CACKLER.</p>
        <p>Aquatic Reflections. Stimulated at Stockton Heath Lake. Sir,—As an eye witness of the events of last Saturday at the Stockton Heath Lake I should like to record my impressions. There is no doubt that this aquatic institution for the promotion of open-air swimming in the district has come to stay, and that here is a pioneer effort bearing a rich harvest of physical and moral well-being to the commuoity already, and that, too, within the brief space of only two seasons. I have long felt and now state my belief publicly that if a system of open-air swimming baths, situated amid natural surround- ings of field and shrubbery, were established, and made accessible to the inhabitants of all our centres of population for we in the warmer months of the year, also, that, if in addition there were a municipal stock of canvas roofs or open shelters purchased, and erected in some adjoining common or open space, whither the school urchins of our towns could be marched off to prosecute their intellectual labours in the open air, by the Power s ! the arrest of national decay would be immediate, the improvement in the physique and character of the people assured, and that at, comparatively, so beggarly a rate that the ratepayers would scarcely feel it on the rates. Such at any rate is my opinion ; but to the proceedings of Saturday, as I stood upon the bank and looked around upon the tiers of crowded seats, as I cast my eyes upon the arena, not a tract of terra firma this one, hut a sheet of lake water. " Ha!" thinks I," this is as I expected, this is as it should be ,for this is in accordance with nature herself. The law of affinity, of I ke seeking like, is it not Lore in operation, even though they may be, every son accl daughter of them, uroonscioue ot the far', I;'.c.ttse in the int placz, as scientists affirm, the far greats r proportion of their constitutional makt-up consists of water, and that in itself could account for so much water streaming to the lake— like, as I soy, seeking I he. Impression, number one, then, why there was such a gathering on Saturday at the lake. But there were others. The thrice renowned Darwin has told us that the progenitors of the exalted order Man was a tadpole, and I declare myself, on the strength of my own observation, exercised on the very epot, that I would have sworn the some myself, which WFS for me impression No. 2, and, still further, explained the gathering at the lake; for, here again, I saw that like instinctively was seeking like, that nature must continue nature,frogs continue frogs Here were the greatest celebrities and beauties in the district settled down for a whole afternoon in the galleries around, and the] e down in the chilly water were such frog like capers of human legs and arms as no frog could ever rival. Truly, as Horace says "You can't drive Nature out with a pitchfork." Consequently the impression No. 3 :—Raise a finger to injure the Stockton Heath Lake and you raise Cain. Immutably convinced, then, in the presence of such proofs, as the Darwinian aid such like theories afford, that the Stockton Heath Lake is not all my e 3 e and Betty Martin," but the life and soul of me and all my tribe, who are something more and better than a rag tag and bobta 1 of a remnant, as you might have seen on Saturday, let me, without unnecessary detail, make some reflections upon certain features ol that great day's aquatic sport. The Com- mittee responsible for the character and order of the events did well. That I think would be the general opiuion. The ladies' 50 yards open handicap was a race which revealed an interest on the part of the spectators so unexpected that, there is no doubt, in future galas, they will be accorded their fair share of the events. In this race, the graceful, smooth, quiet style of the young lady winner of the first prize. using the breast stroke, easily secured her the post of honour. This is the real Siren stroke, which I commend to the ladies as being the most bewitchirg for them to acquire, and so disport them- selves to the utmost advairtage in the water. As to , the youths and mens' competitions, we brew what to expect; nor were the spectators disappointed in any single instance of their anticipations. The contest for the championship of the lake was viewed with strained delight. The neat dives were a running eeries of masterpieces, but the veterans' race was a climax And here I must specially commend the com- mittee ; for, to my mind, in this event, if any. they revealed a genius. Now a veteran is a man who Comes through an ordeal well, and a chief veteran one who comes through it hist of all. Eight of this veteran class then stood proudly forth, and ranged themeelvesalcng the edge of the stage. The sight of course was one to stir the heart, as might he expected of a line of warriors thus drawn out in battle array. From their costume it was not to be expected rin should make dednctions. If one was to go by stripes as proofs of valorous deeds, none wore a stripe upon hid coetume to my recollection, sava me, and his was barred from throat ti thigh; but for the matter of tha a man with no costume at all, barring the barst altogether, is most of a man in the water, as is very well known,. Consequently no one</p>
        <p>paid the slightest Ired to costumes. But I venture to aver that not a soul of those wl o took in the field but appraised the veteranship of those stalwarts by ever other token than the right one. They went by sowesuch indication as a bald head, a proud cheat, or a leg mote or less advanced, but I went la- the twinkle of the eye; and I was right. Aud though this same feat performer, as a matter of fact, was the only one who wore the still:es, it was more as if, knowing a thing or two, he had deliberately honoured himself before the event, as indeed the tvriekling of his eye well-nigh suggested. The com- mittee thus seeming to know shat a la al veterans' race cons'sted in, all being in order, the marker or timekeeper, set them to it when, in they went, every mother's son of them; and a better sight of the sort was never seen I tell you. Some, of course, made off at a great pace, and were soon leading the van, while others, forging on at their very best, gained heart to keep apace by seeing others plying or plungiog in their wake, but, I don't think no discreetly did one gentleman keep entirely in the rear that he not only came in lar, but came in at that hooked to a life-bet which acme device of his enabled him to bitch ox to the near foot ' and ankle of the swimmer just ahead. Bravo ! talk about a Boman triumph on the way to the Capitol —it wasn't in it! All those who procetded him seemed only to announce their chief veteran and victor's cute rag who followed on his own terms, switched serenely to his life belt. The prize for that win on the part of the spectamrs was the biggest cheer that ever went up to heaven. But, though the Committee did extraerdinarilv well, they showed a cm lain want of imagination in rot making the events even still more va ied, and in particular in not giving that direction to some of them which appeals to a more serious &amp;de of the observer than mere contests of agility and endurance, or displays t excite mere mirth. And in this depsrtment, if I should ever have the honour to serve upon the committee, I sill suggest to them a whole field of novelties, and which I trust will be among the customary events of an aquatic gala; and it may be assumed their frequency will even add to their popularity. For example, as proving that a good contour is a good thing in the water, for the sake of that great number who require the ocular demonstration of visible signs and tokens, and who cannot take a statement of fact on ttust, could not the best shaped swimmers be placed in competition with those who are not the best shaped, so that, in a matter where interest would be necessarily great, repeated proofs could be affoided during the season to satisfy these who hove neither Lith nor imagination that good swimmers are as ornamental outside as they are useful inside the water ? Then, again, take that great question of the weary and worn and sod unto death, vrto know not what on eat th they want, but for whom a swim would be the very thing, could they not be induced to stage themselves, and then, immediately after a full description of their maladies was rendered to the audience by the marker, at once take the waters 1 Surely the spectators could have no more interesting object than to a etch these noel- sufferers from the doldrums girding up their loins for mice, beneath their costumes, descend- ing into the deep and coming up again, refreshed, thoroughly c'othed and in their right mind. In that I see a great catch, and will assuredly propose it as sure never I have the hone or of serving on the Committee. Aod last'', take life- saving. Who can doubt that here, at least, if any- where, interest is to be expected, that it will be brought to the due pitch here. A work of mercy of this character Must, and does appeal to the heart of all. But how dens it come Omit that there was no realistic exemplification of it on last Saturday, a demonstrated, too, in strict accordance with the natural ciicumstances and the strict facts if the case when such a humane operation is most likely to be called fen th ? It is a young lady, say who has fallen into the water. She las been study lag her reflection too intently in the lake, when all at once, enraptured and captivated by her charms she tumbles in, fully tigged, as you might say, with all her canvas spread; it may be she was in her Sunday frock. Well then, let some magnanimous male equipped to look exactly Ile such a young lady, topple, like her, into the depths beneath, and find himself crying out for help, the moments all too precious gliding by—and then let a real natund male step like • hero bravely forward, take in the situation with his eagle eye, and like an avalanche hurl himself within one arm's length of the drowning beauty's side. "Will you be mine " then let him shout " without a straggle 1' The imaginal, She must then scream out: "Be thine? no ! drown first !" No, help he will, and does approach. " Save me" she shrieks. " Faith and isn't that just what I'm after " says Le, "if you'll give me half a ceance." And theo, so long as he keeps her sweet month above the water, it doesn't matter if he tows her in by the hair of the head. But, once alongside the jetty, let him : Lift her up tenderly, lift her with care, Fashioned to slenderly, young and so fair." And on those teims do Sslvester. And that would be showing life-eaving ta the life. Yours, SOBERSIDES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>HALF THE KETCH.</head>
        <p>HALF THE KETCH.</p>
        <p>There was me and dad, and some more of the crowd, Was slain' around in Parkinson's store, When Bill Hawkins told a tale, he 'lowed Would at the fellers all in a roar. Says be : "When I was out in the West, Along on the edge of loway, I knowed a feller there that made A hundred dollars in half a day. ' The old Missoo' got on a t aise— A realer old Missouri flood— And this feller th;nks be sees a chance In ke•chin' &amp;stein' Icgs of wood. So be advertised for fifty men. In the Roarin' City " Weekly sews." To meet him on the river bank With skiffs and boats, or with canoes. An' he hired them fellers to ketch that wood : • An' all that mornin' the wood they ketched. An' the pay he geve's where the joke came in— He gave 'em—half of what they ketehed ' And the fellers laughed at Old Billy's yarn, Laughed, and aaid they thought it grand : Yet all them fellers that cackled so, Was working on share* on rented land.' JJUDNAI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>FAVOURED TRADE. MORE WARRINGTON PUBLIC-HM.ISE ASSESSMENTS REDUCED. The Case of the Patten Arms Hotel.</head>
        <p>THE FAVOURED TRADE. MORE WARRINGTON PUBLIC-HM.ISE ASSESSMENTS REDUCED. The Case of the Patten Arms Hotel.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington , Assessment Committee was held yesterday morning. Mr. A. Brooks presided and there were present :—Messrs. G. Mather, A. J. Jolley, W. Houghton, J. W. Knowles, W. Furness, W. C. Brooks, W. Gill, J. Billinge, A. Bottornley (clerk), R. I. Partridge (assistant clerk), W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer), and E. Gunson (valuer). There were many appeals and the ccm- mittee sat for three and a half hours. Mr. Herbert Hatton (Messrs. Robert Davies anu Co., solicitors) appealed against the assessment of the Patten Arms Hotel, owned by Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co.. Ltd. The amount was fixed at £376 gross and £319 10s. rateable. Mr. Hatton said he objected to the assessment, as under the circumstances it was too much. The owners bought the hotel in 1907 and had made a heavy loss on it since. It was a hotel much too big for the requirements of the town and the business done was not sufficient to pay for the necessary upkeep of</p>
        <p>the place. At the same time there was a fair amount of use for it as a hotel and it would be a great inconvenience if it were closed. The situation was such that there was very little population around it andreall y there was a small trade compared with the extent of the house. It was difficult to say what the assessment should be, but they would be prepared to let the place, stabling and everything—except the tie as regards beer—for £2OO a year. He suggested that the rateable value should be £240. After consideration the committee reduced the assessment to £3OO gross and £255 rateable.</p>
        <p>In the matter of the district appeals Mr. Garner (solicitor, of St. Helens) and Mr. Kirby (valuer, of Liverpool), appeared, while Mr. D. P. Davies (valuer, of Manchester) and Mr. W. J. Shaw appeared against the borough assessments.</p>
        <p>The following are the particulars showing the old and new assessments:</p>
