      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>TARIFF-RIDDEN FRANCE.</head>
        <p>TARIFF-RIDDEN FRANCE.</p>
        <p>Dear-Food Riots.</p>
        <p>There has been rioting in France owing to the increa,ing cost of food. All the accounts from the department of the Nord speak of the increasing gravity of the dear food agitation. A curious feature is that in many districts the butchers, who are them- selves looked upon as enemies by the consumers, are themselves agitating against their tyrants the cattle- dealers. The Sub-Prefict of Valenciennes received two dele- gates yesterday—one of the consumers and the other of the butchers. The latter demanded that the expor- tation if cattle be prohibited, that custom tariffs be suppressed , that a fixed price should be establi,hed for meat, and, lastly, the suppression of the trusts of food supplies in general and meat in particular. Five hundred miners wires and a hundred miners, preceded by flags and placards, marched from Dorignies to Douui to protest against the price of butter and eggs. They sang the '' Internationale " on the way. At the Hotel de Ville they were received by the municipal council, which declared its inability to lower the price of food, but promised to address a petition to the Government for the suppression of Customs tariff on cattle and food supplies of prime necessity. Incidents were numerous. A violent demonstration took place at Fresnes, near Valenciennes, where 500 women surrounded a farm and called upon the farmer to reduce the price of his Mllt. The farmer birticaded himself in his house, and fired his gun into the air as a signal for help. The women there- upon broke into the farm and pillaged or smashed everything they could lay hands upon. The farmer, who was sabring from heart disease, fell dead from the shcck. Two thousand women demonstrated on Thursday at Somain to the cry of "Down with the shavers." They burst into the station and searched the baskets and even the pockets of the c;tintry people arriving by train L.r themarket. "We roust have butter," they cried, "at 30 sous (Is. 21d.) or there will be a revolution." At Soissons the Mayor, interviewed by a body of 2,000 women, declared that the market should not be held, and that the municipal council would meet to consider the situation. He agreed to fix a maximum</p>
        <p>price for butter and egge and t, let the public know by posters every decision taken with a view to diminishing the ccst of ford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>An excellent all-round programme is witnessed at the above popular place of entertainment this week, when the premier place is occupied by Frank L. Gregory and troupe. These artistes, seven in number, provide one of the most original and exhilarating performances ever witnessed at this " house." They are deecribe.l as the world's greatest hoop rollers and juggler and this title is not exaggerated for the numerous manipulations and manoeuvres of the hoops are really clever. The hoops are to be seen flying and rolling in all directions and at various speedo, but all have a destination and reach it withc,nt a flaw. The Yorkshire rustic, Jack Lane, who established himself .uch a warm favourite on his previous visit, is again to the fore, and his country " simpleness " is the cause of repeated rounds of laughter. The story of his first visit to the Isle-of-Man and his humorous imitations of the renderirg of " Kelly" by different personalities g, a long way to make his turn a great success. Gillin i. a unique ventrilo- quial humorist far away from the ordinary run of these artintes, his jokes and patter being of a quality that appeal t ) all sitiona. Fanny Robina is seen to advantage as a burlesque actress and vocalist. Eileen Hall cm tributes an oriainal s ng scent for which she receives due reward, while Flon-ie Cooper's singing is of a meritorious character. The pictures continue to maintain their high standard su4 the orchestra under the able conductorship of Mr. E. A. Widdop, render a number of choice selections. - -</p>
        <p>The progratntre for next week promises an abund- ance of mit th, when three of he principal " turn." will be given by comedians. The premier Vane on the bill 14 occupied by T m Foy, he famous little c ',median and Ins company who will be seen in 'The ' First of April." Foy on previous visits has always been a favourite, and tie occasion promises to be no exception to the rule. A number of tiller well-known artistes are also announced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WELL NOURISHED ON 3s. A WEEK!</head>
        <p>WELL NOURISHED ON 3s. A WEEK!</p>
        <p>At a Stepney inquest on Thursday on Robs Ferhy, formerly a stevedore, St. George's, E., it was stated that his only 8011,C0 of income WU the old-age pension of ss. a week, out of which he paid 2r. for rent. The doctor stated that the body waa well nourished.—The Coroner : It is remarkable how some people live.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>:TE AND READY TO BEGIN</head>
        <p>:TE AND READY TO BEGIN</p>
        <p>Manchester Guardian Photo. WARRINGTON BRIDGE AS I'</p>
        <p>IS TO-DAY. THE CONTRACTORS FO]</p>
        <p>The Bridge still stands, but there ara increasing indications that its days are rapidly shortening. During the week Mr. Webster, the engineer, and Mr. J. Mansfield Cobb (the resident Clerk of Works) have been seen in consultation. Various loads of " tackle" have arrived intermittently, including a plwerful shear-legs or crane, which at prese t lies prone among the headstones on the site of 'le Bridge Inn. On the other side of the river, the vacant space adjoining Wharf*treet, has been braided in and will soon be stocked w.th contractors' material and supplies. Many things were held up by the strike,</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk mild correspondence with Mr. J. J. Webster misting to the appointment of as assistant to Mr. Cobb for the purpose of cement testing. After consideration the Committee decided not to mike the sppui, tment, and instrulted tl.e Town Cark to c rnonicete with Mr. Webs er thereon. Ths Town Clerk submitted the drift contract with Mesim. Alfre I Thorne and Sons for the construction of Warringtou Bridge. Resolved, that the same be approved.</p>
        <p>THE NEW BRIDGE ARE ON THE s:</p>
        <p>which has caused s delsy in commencing opera- Tito following letter from the Clerk to the Duchy of tions at Bridge Foot. Lancaster was read : The following mi r utateof the Briiges Sub-Committee Sir,' come up tor confirm sti to by tha C3iincil on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>31st July, 19:1.</p>
        <p>AIERIET FORESHORE. BRIDGE AND TRAINING WALLS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Your letter of the 25:h inst. has nos lived, and I ens to say that the Chancellor of the Duchy will be advised to rant the right of cmistract- Liz and for ere; hereafter mainttium; the Bridge and Training Walls in question, 83 for as His Alaj sty's rights jure do.,atus in the foreshore and bed of the River Mersey are concerned, in considera- tion of the stim of £5.</p>
        <p>K ally izt zrzu we if your Corporation &amp;gran to these terns.</p>
        <p>If no, a draft grant will be forwarded in doe course for approval. Toe fees for its preparation and the stump duty, amounting together to El Is. Gd. would be payable by the Clrporation. The pica which you forwarded is retained pending the cmcpletion of the matter. I am. Your obedient servant, LOUIS F. FERGUSSON, For the Clerk of the Council. The Town Clerk of Warrington. Resolve I, tha: the above terms be agreed to. Resolved, that the Engiaeer's Insd oa the works be insured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Bibby--Calvert.</p>
        <p>A wedding of considerable interest was solemnized at St. Andrew's Church, Southport, on Wednesday last in the presence tf a large gathering, when Miss Eleanor Saunders (Nellie) Calvert,(lily daughter of Mr. J. R. Calvert: 11, Cross arm*, Southport, was married to Mr. Harry Bibby, electrician, third ton of Mr. William Bibby, of Arpley-street, Warrington. The Rev 03 Howson,vicar of St. Andrews, performed the ceremony and Mr B Lofthouse who presided at the organ, played appropriate music. The bride who was given array by her fathsr was attired in a Duchess satin dress trimmed with Limerick km, and full mutt train lined with ninon. The train was prettily set off with the horseshoe and wedding bells. She wore a tiny Juliet cap under a Brussels net veil (kindly lent by Mrs. Hargreaves) and carried a sheaf of Mlles. 7he bride's present to the bride- groom was a diamond and pearl pin and stud, and the bridegroom's present to the Mide was a pearl and I peridA pendant and chain, There were four brides- maids, i.s. :—The Misses Janet and Ada Bibby (sisters of ihe bridegroom) and kli s Genie Lawrence and Miss Emma Brown (friends of the bride), all of whom loaked charming in dresses of varied pale shades, viz. :—Blue, pink, heliotrope and green chiffon taffAa. They wore tiny Juliet caps to match and carried sheaves of pale pink chrysanthe- mums. They also wore gold mother of pearl andante, the gifts of the In idegroom. Mr. Tom Bibby, brother of the bridegroom, was the , best man, and the groomsmen were Messrs. Veal] Calvert, H. Horrocks and W. Green. The bride's mother was dressed in Royal blue silk with under- dress of brocade with fringe and Oriental trimming to match. She wore a mole coloured hat and carried a b uquet of pale pink carnations. The bridegroom's mother wore a dress of black silk, trimmed with emerald green. Her black hat was trimmed with emerald green and willow plume and she carried a bouquet of red carnations. Mrs. 11. Ingram, sister of the bridegroom, of Toronto, Canada, was attired in a is be of black ninon over white lace, and wore a leghorn bat with willow plumes. Subsequently are eption and luncheon were held at Fletcher's Cafe, Lord-street, Southport, when about PO guests were present. Later in the day the newly-married couple left for London and Bournem-uth, where the honeymoon is 1-eing spent. The bride travelled in a rose-coloured costnme richly braided in the same colour and trimmed with black and white silk. Her satin hat to match was trimmed with large ostrich plumes. The f Alowing is a het of the presents • , Father of bride, set of oil paintings Mother of bride, household linen Father and mother of bridegroom, canteen of silver cutlery Mr C Calvert and Miss Hall, silver and pearl fish knives and forks</p>
        <p>Mr \ esa Calvert and Mi.sti Molly Todd, silver tea tray Mr and Mrs A Dickens (Warringtso), toilet set Misses B and C Dickson (Warrington), net of jugs Masters Charles and Arthur Dickens, preserve die% Mr and Mrs John Bibby (Warrington tea service. Minn Dora Bibby (Warrington), cups sad ssucers Masten Willie and Jackie Bibby (Warrington), teapot ad hot water jug Mr and Mrs William Bibby, jon (Warrington), after- oon tea cloth</p>
        <p>Miss Kathleen Bibby (SVArrington), rose boa I Mr and Mrs H Ingram ( Toronto), electric standard</p>
        <p>lamp Misi Janet Bibby and Mr W Green (Warrington:</p>
