      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Booth---West.</head>
        <p>Booth---West.</p>
        <p>A wedding of considerable I cal interest was solemnized at St. James' Church on Tuesday afternoon last, the contracting parties being Miss Louie Booth, second daughter of Mr. William Booth. Wilderspool Causeway, and Mr. Herbert West, eldest son of Mr. Jams West, "Lyndon Bewsey-road. The tfficiating clergyman was the Bev. H. B. Firth, vicar of St. James'. Mr. Davies presided at the organ, and contributed appropriate selections, including Mendelssohn's "Wedding March." The bride, who was given away by her father looked charming in a pretty dress of cream crepe-de-chine in Empire style, with wreath of orange blos-lm and veil She also wore a gold bangle, the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were Miss Florrie Booth, sister of the bride, and Min Bessie Weet,sister of the bridegroom, who were attired in handsome. gowns of biscuit cloth with black picture hats, and carried bouquets of pink and red geraniums, the gifts. of the bridegroom. Mr Summersgill Wild rei formed the duties of best man in a most efficient manner. The- reception was held at the home of the bride, and later in the day the happy pair left for Southport. where. the honeymoon is being spear. The presents were both numerous and valuable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Light—Hawthorn.</head>
        <p>Light—Hawthorn.</p>
        <p>A vary pretty wedding was solemnised at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Saturday afternoan, when Miss Mary Elizabeth Hawthorn, second daughter o.r. the late Mr. Thomas Hawthorn and Mr.. Hawthorn, of 27, Lovely-lane, was mirried to Mr. Frederick Thomas Light, sin of the late Mr. William Light and Mrs Light of Bewsey-street. There was a large congregation at the ceremony, which was. conducted by the Rev. John Yonge. Mr. W. H. Payton, Hun. Rae., pretided at the organ and played appropriate milections as the congrega- tion was assembling. During the service the hymn " How welcome was the call " was sung and Mr. Payton a'so played Mendelssohn's Wedding March. The bride, who was given away dy her brother. Councillor Joseph Hawthorn, wore a dress of embroidered ivrry rnesaline silk, and also an embroidered tulle veil. Her bouquet consisted of lilies and white heather. The bridesmaids were Miss Helen Hawthorn (sister cf the bride, and Miss Light (sister of the bridegroom), who were gowned in reseda silk eolienne. They wore black hats and carried bouquets of pink carnations. Mr. Ernest Light (brother of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best man, and Mr II Hawthorn acted as- groomsman. A reception was held in the Wycliffe Memorial Building. Later in the day Mr. and Mrs. P. T. Light left on the honeymoon tour fir the Channel Islands via London. The bride travelled in a grey coat and skirt with silver grey satin hst. Numerics presents were receiver!.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>TROOPS FIRED AT</head>
        <p>TROOPS FIRED AT</p>
        <p>Startling New Light on Liverpool</p>
        <p>WAS PRENDERGAST SHOT FROM THE CROWD ?</p>
        <p>Evidence that the soldiers were tired at at least twice from the crowd was given when the inquest Was [-rimmed at Liverpool on Thursday on the bodies of the two men killed by the troops on the occasion of the attack on the prison van on Auguit 15. It was ,uggested that one of the men killed met death in this way. . . .</p>
        <p>Captain Stews' t, who was in charge of the escort ; Lieutensat Hetherington, who gate the order to are. and Troop-sergeant Earl, who fired the fwat shots, were among the witnesses. The jury returned a verdict rf .2fis;i6able homicide, I mud added that they unanimously cousideted the firing necesalry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>"SPY: SCARE. Troops with Ball Cartridge Guard Magazine</head>
        <p>THE "SPY: SCARE. Troops with Ball Cartridge Guard Magazine</p>
        <p>A dJuble corcpsny of the 2nd Lincolnshire Regiment msde up to war strength luis been posted as guard one- the Merchnood Msgsnine, on Southampton Water, Too men, whose numb,* it is understood sill be increase : shortly, here been on this duty since the beginning of August,their presence apparently being due to suspicions entertained es to the possible activities of foreign agents on espionage bent. 8,11 cartridge has beee served out to the men, not examining outposts bare been throne oat, their duty being to stop and search and, if necessary, detain any suspicious person.</p>
